@start
@org
; Dictionary index: where each letter's words start
;
; 32 two-byte offsets from #R$5DBE($5DBF). Entries 1-26 (indexed by a letter's
; ASCII code AND $1F) give the first word starting with that letter; entry 31
; marks the end of the dictionary (#R$67B7). The tokenizer (#R$DA34) finds the
; end of a letter's words by skipping forward to the next non-zero entry.
; .
; CORRECTED: the automatic code/data split had this as code, which is why it
; used to disassemble as nonsense.
w$5D80 DEFW $0000    ; Unused
 $5D82 DEFW $0001    ; 'A' words
 $5D84 DEFW $00A7    ; 'B' words
 $5D86 DEFW $0164    ; 'C' words
 $5D88 DEFW $020D    ; 'D' words
 $5D8A DEFW $0280    ; 'E' words
 $5D8C DEFW $02DD    ; 'F' words
 $5D8E DEFW $036A    ; 'G' words
 $5D90 DEFW $03BA    ; 'H' words
 $5D92 DEFW $042D    ; 'I' words
 $5D94 DEFW $048B    ; 'J' words
 $5D96 DEFW $0494    ; 'K' words
 $5D98 DEFW $04B0    ; 'L' words
 $5D9A DEFW $0532    ; 'M' words
 $5D9C DEFW $0599    ; 'N' words
 $5D9E DEFW $05E8    ; 'O' words
 $5DA0 DEFW $0636    ; 'P' words
 $5DA2 DEFW $06EB    ; 'Q' words
 $5DA4 DEFW $06F9    ; 'R' words
 $5DA6 DEFW $075A    ; 'S' words
 $5DA8 DEFW $0882    ; 'T' words
 $5DAA DEFW $090C    ; 'U' words
 $5DAC DEFW $094B    ; 'V' words
 $5DAE DEFW $095A    ; 'W' words
 $5DB0 DEFW $09DF    ; 'X' words
 $5DB2 DEFW $09E3    ; 'Y' words
 $5DB4 DEFW $09F5    ; 'Z' words
 $5DB6 DEFW $0000    ; Unused
 $5DB8 DEFW $0000    ; Unused
 $5DBA DEFW $0000    ; Unused
 $5DBC DEFW $0000    ; Unused
 $5DBE DEFW $09F8    ; End of dictionary

; The dictionary: 592 words
;
; Each record (see dictionary.py, which decodes them exactly as #R$A94D does):
; byte 0's low nibble picks a suffix from the table at #R$A85C ($F = none);
; byte 1 says how many characters to keep from the previous word (bits 4-6) and
; how many new 5-bit letters follow (bits 0-3); if bit 7 of byte 1 is set, two
; more bytes give the offset of the word this one is a synonym of. The first
; letter is never stored: it is implied by the letter index.
; .
; Because each word is stored as a change to the previous one, words can only
; be decoded in order - the reason a standalone call to #R$A94D early in this
; project produced garbage.
; .
; Checked against play: every word the game accepted or echoed decodes
; correctly, and the synonym links match its behaviour (READ is EXAMINE: READ
; CHRONICLE answers '...THAT YOU CAN EXAMINE'; PICK is CARRY: PICK UP LAMP
; answers 'I DO NOT KNOW THE VERB UP CARRY'; BREAK is KILL). One link is
; unexplained: IN is recorded as a synonym of VISITED.
b$5DC0 DEFB $8F,$00                         ; A
 $5DC2 DEFB $D0,$00                         ; AS
