; Message bytecode: everything the game prints
;
; From the end of the dictionary (#R$67B7) up to the verb patterns at #R$8259,
; this is text in the message interpreter's bytecode (#R$A59F): a byte with bit
; 7 set starts a two-byte dictionary reference, $00-$1C are control codes (jump
; table at #R$A55D), $20-$3F are literal characters, $40-$5F introduce a run of
; packed 5-bit letters, and $60 and above are handled at #R$A83A.
; .
; CORRECTED: the automatic code/data split had 23 'code' blocks in here, mostly
; starting on page boundaries (#R$6CFD($6D00), #R$6FFA($7000), ...). None of
; them is called or jumped to from anywhere; they came from this project's
; coverage map, which counted the printer's data reads as executed bytes. Two
; of them collided with message starts and broke the round trip, which is how
; this was found.
; .
; 212 messages are split out below: those printed by an LD HL shortly before a
; printer call, those loaded into HL or DE anywhere in code, those printed or
; said by plan scripts (PRINT and SAY TO, see #R$CC78), those only a room or
; thing record points to (room descriptions, for instance), and the rest, found
; by walking the bytecode from #R$67FF message by message. The 72 bytes before
; #R$67FF (this block) are not an ordinary message.
b$67B7 DEFB $9D,$08,$DB,$08,$01,$00,$5D,$00
 $67BF DEFB $EE,$09,$EC,$05,$99,$08,$85,$04
 $67C7 DEFB $50,$04,$6A,$09,$9A,$00,$2D,$04
 $67CF DEFB $88,$04,$D5,$05,$DF,$03,$63,$02
 $67D7 DEFB $86,$09,$4B,$09,$BF,$09,$FC,$03
 $67DF DEFB $94,$07,$5E,$01,$E3,$03,$FF,$05
 $67E7 DEFB $7A,$00,$20,$06,$51,$03,$E0,$05
 $67EF DEFB $C0,$00,$96,$09,$14,$04,$94,$05

