; Verb patterns: the verb number is the position in this table
;
; 54 eight-byte records, one per verb phrase the parser accepts, ended by
; $0000. Bytes 0-5 are up to three word slots: the low 12 bits are a dictionary
; offset (see the dictionary at #R$5DC0), the high 4 bits are flags (see
; below). Bytes 6-7 are a fourth word slot, laid out the same way: GO for the
; ten directions (so GO NORTH works), CLOSELY for verb #R$E135(20) (EXAMINE
; CLOSELY) and FURIOUSLY for verb #R$9AE1(30) (GO FURIOUSLY TO); the narrator
; prints this word first ('Furiously go to the old mill road'). The high
; nibbles of the four slots are the record's flags, gathered by #R$A2BD into
; #R$A01A($A01D)/#R$A01A($A01E). CORRECTED: bytes 6-7 were 'not yet decoded'.
; .
; The verb number is not stored anywhere: #R$B1AB computes it from which record
; the parser matched, as (record - #R$8259) / 8 + 1, and stores it in
; #R$A032($A033). Confirmed by watching writes to #R$A032($A033) during a real
; TAKE LAMP (41 is written by #R$B1BF), and by typing about twenty commands and
; checking the verb number each one produced against this table: every one
; matched (NORTH=1 ... DOWN=10, CARRY=11, CLOSE=14, DROP=16, ENTER=18,
; EXAMINE=19, FOLLOW=21, GET=23, GIVE TO=26, HAIL=31, INVENTORY=33, LOOK=34,
; SIT IN=40, TAKE=41, TELL=47, WAIT=49, WEAR=51, KILL=52, LOCK=53, UNLOCK=54).
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator: GO NORTH works like NORTH, and EXAMINE CLOSELY
; LAMP is narrated 'You closely examine the oil lamp.' - the fourth word first
; - and takes five minutes; EXAMINE LAMP CAREFULLY (the adverb after the
; object) gets 'WHAT ?'.
; .
; The flag nibbles, as far as the code and the 54 records show. #R$A2BD packs
; the first two slots' nibbles into #R$A01A($A01D) and the last two into
; #R$A01A($A01E). #R$A01A($A01D) bit 3 (first word, nibble bit 3): the verb
; takes a direct object - set for every transitive verb and for none of the
; directions, LOOK, INVENTORY, HELLO or WAIT HERE; the narrator prints a direct
; object only then (#R$A3A5), and it is also bit 7 of the first word's
; reference, which is what #R$B39C/#R$A01A($A01C) copy, so ALL can only be used
; with such verbs (in the emulator EXAMINE ALL goes through everything in the
; room, while KILL ALL, KILL having no flags at all, prints nothing).
; #R$A01A($A01D) bit 2: the verb takes an indirect object - DROP IN, GIVE TO,
; PAY TO, PUT IN, PUT ON, TAKE FROM, TAKE OUT OF, TAKE TO, TELL, TELL ABOUT
; (#R$A3E9). #R$A01A($A01D) bit 5 (second word, nibble bit 1): the phrase's
; second word goes before the direct object - CLIMB INTO, GET OUT OF, GO TO,
; LOOK THROUGH, SIT IN, TALK TO, WAIT UNTIL and others (#R$A3A9).
; #R$A01A($A01E) bit 7 (fourth word, nibble bit 3, only CLIMB OUT OF and GET
; OUT OF): one more word before the object in the narration (#R$A3AE).
; #R$A01A($A01D) bit 0: the object need not be here - FOLLOW, COME WITH, the GO
; TOs, HAIL, TAKE OFF, TAKE TO, TELL - copied to #R$A032($A038), which lets the
; thing search #R$D36A skip its is-it-here test (#R$B4DA, #R$B581).
; #R$A01A($A01D) bit 4: no 'You ...' narration at all (#R$A326 jumps over it) -
; LOOK, INVENTORY, HELLO, ENTER, FOLLOW, COME WITH, GO INTO, the GO TOs, TAKE
; TO, TELL and WAIT UNTIL, whose own code says what happens. #R$A01A($A01E)
; bits 2-3 both set (third word's nibble 12 or 13: WAIT UNTIL, PAY TO): the
; direct object is a number, requirement code 5 rather than 7 (#R$C4A1);
; #R$A01A($A01E) bits 0-1 both set do the same for the indirect object
; (#R$C4C0), though no verb sets them. #R$A01A($A01E) bit 4 (HAIL, CLIMB INTO):
; try every candidate for the second object rather than the first that fits
; (#R$B18D($B190), #R$B2DF), and no ALL. #R$A01A($A01E) bit 5 (GIVE TO, PAY
; TO): with no preposition typed, the second noun phrase is the direct object
; (#R$B421) - so GIVE WATSON LAMP gives the lamp to Watson (confirmed in the
; emulator, 8 November). Bits 6-7 of #R$A01A($A01D) (bit 7: ENTER, GO INTO, the
; GO TOs; bit 6: TAKE TO) and bit 6 of #R$A01A($A01E) (set for most transitive
; verbs) were not traced to a reader here; see the correction below.
; .
; CORRECTION 2026-11-08: bits 7 and 6 of #R$A01A($A01D) are read, by #R$C493
; and #R$C4B2, which set the requirement codes #R$A00A($A00F) (direct object)
; and #R$A00A($A010) (indirect object): no direct object (bit 3 clear) -> 0;
; bit 7 set -> 4; both number bits of #R$A01A($A01E) (bits 2-3) set -> 5;
; otherwise 7. The indirect object is the same with bit 2, bit 6 and
; #R$A01A($A01E) bits 0-1. So bit 7 (ENTER, GO INTO, the GO TOs) and bit 6
; (TAKE TO) make that object's requirement code 4 - the code #R$C6BE treats
; after the room codes - instead of an ordinary thing (7). Only bit 6 of
; #R$A01A($A01E) is still unread.
