; Plan script: you (actor 0) - read, parse and do the player's commands
;
; The player is a character like any other, and this is its script. Each pass:
; #R$D6BF reads a line from the keyboard with a time limit (#R$D898; if the
; player types nothing for a few seconds the step fails and the player WAITs
; instead, so the game goes on), #R$D715 turns the line
; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00)) into a list of words at
; #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B81)($5B81) (#R$D9C5), #R$D769 checks for words left,
; #R$D77E looks the next words up (#R$AAB2) and splits off one sentence
; (#R$9FF0 -> #R$9FF2), #R$D7D9 checks that there is a sentence, #R$D7FA parses
; it (#R$B191) into a verb and objects and copies them into the plan registers
; (#R$D826, #R$D82C), and DO plan verb / plan dobj / plan iobj carries it out.
; When a step fails the script goes back a step or two, which is how a line
; with several commands is worked through one sentence per turn. Read from the
; code; the five RUN targets are the same five addresses an earlier live trace
; saw at the start of every command.
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator: 'TAKE LAMP. EXAMINE LAMP. INVENTORY' typed as one
; line is carried out one sentence a minute - the lamp is taken at 8:02,
; examined at 8:03 and the inventory listed at 8:04, with the clock printed
; between, before the next prompt.
; .
; Entry points: #R$9921 = you (actor 0).
; .
; Decoded by tools/plan.py; see #R$CC78 for the language. Each sub-block is one
; instruction. Room and object numbers are as the game uses them (the reference
; pages list both). Labels for verbs 128-140 and for the common helper records
; (#R$E52F, #R$E57A, #R$E588, #R$E5A8, #R$DDBE, #R$F198) were read from those
; records' code, not tried one by one in the emulator.
; .
; In an instruction, 'plan dobj' and 'plan iobj' are not fixed objects: they
; mean the direct and indirect object currently held in the actor's plan
; registers (bytes +4 and +5 of its actor record), so the same step works on
; whatever the actor is dealing with - for the player, the objects of the
; command just typed. 'plan verb' is the verb register (+3), 'self' is the
; acting character, and '-' means none.
b$9921 DEFB $01,$22             ; DO 34 #R$DC7B(LOOK)
 $9923 DEFB $13,$BF,$D6,$48,$99 ; RUN #R$D6BF [fail #R$9948]
 $9928 DEFB $13,$15,$D7,$23,$99 ; RUN #R$D715 [fail #R$9923]
 $992D DEFB $13,$69,$D7,$23,$99 ; RUN #R$D769 [fail #R$9923]
 $9932 DEFB $13,$7E,$D7,$23,$99 ; RUN #R$D77E [fail #R$9923]
 $9937 DEFB $13,$D9,$D7,$2D,$99 ; RUN #R$D7D9 [fail #R$992D]
 $993C DEFB $13,$FA,$D7,$37,$99 ; RUN #R$D7FA [fail #R$9937]
 $9941 DEFB $02,$FA,$FD,$FC     ; DO plan verb: plan dobj, plan iobj
 $9945 DEFB $0E,$37,$99         ; GOTO #R$9937
 $9948 DEFB $01,$31             ; DO 49 #R$DDB4(WAIT HERE)
 $994A DEFB $0E,$23,$99         ; GOTO #R$9923

; The case's relation words
;
; Word references for the verbs a statement about the case can use; a
; statement's relation number is a position here (1-based). #R$BDD1 finds the
; verb of what the player said in this list; #R$BF45 looks a relation up to
; print it (#R$BF1C prints a statement, with AND, word $005D, for relation 0).
; .
; CORRECTED: an earlier version of this overlay left these 24 bytes undecoded.
b$994D DEFW $00FE    ; Relation 1: BLACKMAILED
 $994F DEFW $03BA    ; Relation 2: HAD
 $9951 DEFW $03D8    ; Relation 3: HAS
 $9953 DEFW $0485    ; Relation 4: IS
 $9955 DEFW $0498    ; Relation 5: KILLED
 $9957 DEFW $0956    ; Relation 6: VISITED
 $9959 DEFW $03DB    ; Relation 7: HATED
 $995B DEFW $02CD    ; Relation 8: EXAMINED
 $995D DEFW $040A    ; Relation 9: HID
 $995F DEFW $0632    ; Relation 10: OWNS
 $9961 DEFW $0538    ; Relation 11: MADE
 $9963 DEFW $0000    ; End marker

; The case: statements about the crime, and the evidence each needs
;
; Eleven groups. Each starts with a statement record: +0 a thing or person, +1
; a relation number, +2 another thing; +3 flags; and, with bit 4 or 5 of the
; flags, +4/+5 a routine run when it is proven. Bit 2 of the flags opens a
; group of evidence records (+0 and +2 are things, +1 is 0) and bit 3 closes
; it; #R$BE5A steps over one record and #R$BE72 over a whole group. In the
; state bits, bit 0 means the statement is waiting to be proven and bit 1 that
; it has been (#R$BCC9 sets bit 1 and runs the routine once the evidence checks
; out through #R$BF08; #R$9A74 does the same from a routine).
; .
; With the relation words filled in (#R$994D) the statements read as sentences:
; MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT, MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF, MRS JONES
; KILLED TRICIA FENDER, PHIPPS HAS PLANS, SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD,
; TRICIA FENDER HAD PLANS, TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN, and four with
; no evidence and no routine (flags $B4: bit 5 set) saying that Tricia Fender
; or Phipps killed one of the two victims. What bit 5 does is not fully traced:
; #R$BCC9 only marks such a statement as waiting, and Watson's reply (#R$BA8C)
; answers it differently. The order is as stored (first thing, verb, second
; thing), which is also the order the player's words are matched in (#R$BD69).
; Some groups also hold a statement as evidence (MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES
; VISITED OPIUM DEN, under the Major's innocence). An evidence record with
; relation 0 is a pair of things that the player must have seen (#R$BE91: flags
; $00, both things' +6 bit 7) or examined (flags $40, +6 bit 6). Confirmed in
; the emulator that +6 bit 7 is set on everything in view in the first room and
; that EXAMINE LAMP sets bit 6 of the lamp's +6 ($80 -> $C0); the proving was
; tried too: Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/case_test.py): with
; the player moved to Inspector Lestrade's room at the start, SAY TO LESTRADE
; "MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF" gets 'I do not believe that you have the evidence
; to prove that Mrs Brown killed herself' and the statement's flags go $A4 ->
; $A5 (bit 0: waiting). With the evidence poked in first - the female corpse
; and the sandstone bridge marked seen and examined (+6 bits 7 and 6), the
; large rock marked seen - the same words get 'So Mrs Brown killed herself,
; excellent Holmes, another brilliant conclusion.' and the flags go $A4 -> $A6
; (bit 1: proven). Which order (subject first) the statements are matched in is
; read from #R$BD69.
; .
; Also tried in the emulator: TRICIA FENDER KILLED MRS JONES (one of the bit-5
; statements) told to Lestrade gets the same 'I do not believe that you have
; the evidence to prove that Tricia Fender killed Mrs Jones' and its flags go
; $B4 -> $B5; MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER gets the same answer with its
; flags $A4 -> $A5. Told to Watson, all three statements tried get 'That is
; brilliant Holmes...'. So nothing visible yet separates the bit-5 statements.
; .
; How a piece of evidence is checked (#R$BE91, read from the code): the top two
; bits of its flags choose the test. $00: both things seen (+6 bit 7); $40:
; both examined (+6 bit 6); $80: never true by itself - it only counts once
; something else has marked it done (bit 1), as the statement routines do
; through #R$9A74, so a nested statement such as MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES
; VISITED OPIUM DEN ($8C) has to be established first; $C0: no test of its own,
; only its group. A record with children (bit 2) also needs all of them
; (#R$BF08). Once a piece of evidence passes it is marked done and stays so.
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/major_test.py): with the player
; moved to Lestrade at the start, MAJOR FFOULKES IS INNOCENT is marked waiting
; ($A4 -> $A5) and Lestrade at once asks 'Well can you prove that Major
; Percival Ffoulkes is innocent yet, Holmes ?'; MAJOR FFOULKES VISITED THE
; OPIUM DEN gets 'I do not believe that you have the evidence...'. With bit 1
; of #R$996F($9972) set first - as #R$F103 does when the Major confesses - the
; opium-den statement is proven, the waiting innocence statement is proven with
; it (#R$9965($9968) $A5 -> $A6), #R$9A3D sets #R$9E87($9E88) and Lestrade says
; 'Very good Holmes, I shall return to Leather head tomorrow to have another
; look around.'
; .
; This is what Lestrade's 'WELL CAN YOU PROVE THAT ... YET, HOLMES ?' (#R$9DD8,
; through #R$BC46-#R$BC9C) is about, and the routines here are the 'handlers'
; at #R$99FC-#R$9A7C (overlay 07).
b$9965 DEFB $07,$04,$62,$A4,$3D,$9A ; Statement: 7 major percival ffoulkes / IS
                                    ; / 98 innocent; flags $A4; when proven
                                    ; #R$9A3D
 $996B DEFB $07,$06,$63,$8C         ;   Evidence statement: 7 major percival
                                    ; ffoulkes / VISITED / 99 opium den; flags
                                    ; $8C (last of the group)
 $996F DEFB $00,$00,$00,$08         ;   (empty; flags $08) (last of the group)
 $9973 DEFB $5E,$05,$60,$A4,$4B,$9A ; Statement: 94 mrs brown / KILLED / 96
                                    ; herself; flags $A4; when proven #R$9A4B
