; Lestrade's flags
;
; #R$9D90: set when Inspector Lestrade has met the player together with Chief
; Constable Straker (#R$9E14) - the introduction. Until then the police men let
; no one by, Straker does not greet anyone, and at 3pm on Monday Lestrade's
; script ends the game (#R$9E6A: 'I do not think you are doing too well...').
; #R$9D91-#R$9D91($9D94): Lestrade's message and greeting state. Read from the
; code; the 3pm ending is confirmed in the emulator for a player who never
; meets him.
b$9D90 DEFB $00             ; Lestrade has been introduced
 $9D91 DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00 ; Lestrade's message state

; Lestrade's after-turn routine, after the introduction
;
; Greets the player in his own way (#R$9DC6: 'WELL, HOLMES FANCY SEEING YOU
; HERE.' the first time), asks whether the player can prove the case yet
; (#R$9DD8: 'WELL CAN YOU PROVE THAT ... YET, HOLMES ?', worded from the
; deduction state at #R$BC46-#R$BC9C - not traced), greets Watson, and clears
; bit 0 of +3 in three of the records after the player's script. Read from the
; code.
; .
; Instruction coverage over the 15 stored routes (9 November,
; tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py): all 16 instructions were executed.
;
; Output:F None used
c$9D95 LD HL,$9D91    ; Point HL at #R$9D91 in "Lestrade's flags" - Greeting
                      ; for Holmes (HL = #R$9D91, C = 0) via #R$9DC6
 $9D98 LD C,$00       ; Load C with $00
 $9D9A CALL $9DC6     ; Call the routine "Lestrade greets you: 'WELL, HOLMES
                      ; FANCY SEEING YOU..." (#R$9DC6)
 $9D9D LD HL,$9D94    ; Point HL at #R$9D91($9D94) in "Lestrade's flags" -
                      ; 'Well can you prove that ... yet, Holmes ?' via #R$9DD8
 $9DA0 LD C,$00       ; Load C with $00
 $9DA2 CALL $9DD8     ; Call the routine "Lestrade asks whether you can prove a
                      ; waiting..." (#R$9DD8)
 $9DA5 LD HL,$9D93    ; Point HL at #R$9D91($9D93) in "Lestrade's flags" - A
                      ; word for Watson (C = 3) via #R$9CE9
 $9DA8 LD C,$03       ; Load C with $03
 $9DAA CALL $9CE9     ; Call the routine "Say good morning to C, if just met"
                      ; (#R$9CE9)
 $9DAD LD IX,$99E3    ; Point IX at #R$99E3 in "The case: statements about the
                      ; crime, and..." - Clear bit 0 of +3 in the three case
                      ; records at #R$99E3, #R$99F1, #R$99A9 (those statements
                      ; are not yet told)
 $9DB1 RES 0,(IX+$03) ; Clear bit 0 of byte +3 of the record at IX
 $9DB5 LD IX,$99F1    ; Point IX at #R$99F1 in "The case: statements about the
                      ; crime, and..."
 $9DB9 RES 0,(IX+$03) ; Clear bit 0 of byte +3 of the record at IX
 $9DBD LD IX,$99A9    ; Point IX at #R$99A9 in "The case: statements about the
                      ; crime, and..."
 $9DC1 RES 0,(IX+$03) ; Clear bit 0 of byte +3 of the record at IX
 $9DC5 RET            ; Return to the caller

; Lestrade greets you: 'WELL, HOLMES FANCY SEEING YOU HERE.' the first time
;
; Once the greeting countdown allows (#R$9CF2), says #R$71ED the first time
; (the flag at HL becomes 1) and an ordinary greeting (#R$9CEC, #R$6929: '...
; SAYS TO ... "GOOD MORNING"') after that. Called by Lestrade's after-turn
; routine #R$9D95. Read from the code; both lines seen in the emulator
; ('Inspector Lestrade with a surprised look on his face, says " Well, Holmes
; fancy seeing you here. "' and 'Inspector Lestrade says to you " Good
; afternoon "').
;
;  Input:HL The greeting flag of Lestrade ($9D91; its countdown is the next
; .         byte)
;        C The person to greet
; Output:F From $A59F
c$9DC6 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte - Greeting countdown allows
                     ; it (#R$9CF2)?
 $9DC7 CALL $9CF2    ; Call #R$9CF2 (inside "Say good morning to C, if just
                     ; met")
 $9DCA DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $9DCB LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - Already greeted once
                     ; (#R$9DCC non-zero): ordinary greeting (#R$9CEC)
 $9DCC AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $9DCD JP NZ,$9CEC   ; If A is not zero, go back to #R$9CEC
 $9DD0 INC A         ; Add one to A - First time: mark it greeted...
 $9DD1 LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to
 $9DD2 LD HL,$71ED   ; Point HL at the message "YOU WITH A SURPRISED LOOK ON
                     ; HIS FACE, SAYS"... (#R$71ED) - ...and say #R$71ED
 $9DD5 JP $A59F      ; Jump on to the routine "Print the message at HL"
                     ; (#R$A59F)

; Lestrade asks whether you can prove a waiting statement yet
;
; Re-checks the waiting statements (#R$BC46) and, if one is still waiting for
; evidence (#R$BC9C, #R$BC74), says #R$722D: 'WELL CAN YOU PROVE THAT
; <statement> YET, HOLMES ?' (code $05 prints the statement). Seen in the
; emulator in jones_test.py ('Well can you prove that Mrs Jones killed Tricia
; Fender yet, Holmes ?'). Read from the code.
;
;  Input:HL The countdown for the question
;        C The person asked
; Output:F From $A59F when it is printed; nothing is printed if no statement
; .        waits
c$9DD8 CALL $9CF2     ; Call #R$9CF2 (inside "Say good morning to C, if just
                      ; met") - Greeting countdown allows it (#R$9CF2)? Set the
                      ; flag to 1
 $9DDB LD (HL),$01    ; Store $01 at the address HL points to
 $9DDD CALL $BC46     ; Re-check the waiting statements (#R$BC46)
 $9DE0 CALL $BC9C     ; Find the first one that cannot be proven yet (#R$BC9C)
 $9DE3 LD A,B         ; Copy B into A - B non-zero: #R$9DF5
 $9DE4 AND A          ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                      ; carry)
 $9DE5 JR NZ,$9DF5    ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$9DF5
 $9DE7 CALL $BC74     ; Call the routine "Count the waiting statements that can
                      ; now be proven" (#R$BC74) - None: count those that can
                      ; be proven (#R$BC74); none: finish
 $9DEA AND A          ; Clear the carry flag for the caller (A is unchanged)
 $9DEB RET Z          ; If A is zero, return
 $9DEC PUSH IX        ; Print #R$722D 'WELL CAN YOU PROVE THAT ... YET, HOLMES
                      ; ?'
 $9DEE LD HL,$722D    ; Point HL at the message "YOU SAY" WELL CAN YOU PROVE
                      ; THAT YOU AND YOU... (#R$722D)
 $9DF1 CALL $A59F     ; Call the routine "Print the message at HL" (#R$A59F)
 $9DF4 RET            ; Return to the caller
*$9DF5 BIT 4,(IX+$03) ; Test bit 4 of byte +3 of the record at IX
 $9DF9 RET NZ         ; If bit 4 of byte +3 of the record at IX is set, return
 $9DFA LD A,$01       ; Load A with $01
 $9DFC JP $BBE5       ; Jump on to #R$BBE5

; Lestrade's after-turn routine, until the introduction
;
; Waits until Chief Constable Straker and the player are both with Lestrade, in
; sight of the player (#R$9CCE); until then it does the same as #R$9D95. Then:
; sets #R$9D90, clears +$0A of rooms 92 and 62, rewrites Lestrade's own entry
; in the after-turn table (#R$9C85($9C86)) to #R$9D95 so this never runs again
; (self-modifying data), and prints Straker's introduction - #R$7207, 'LESTRADE
; I WOULD LIKE YOU TO MEET MR SHERLOCK HOLMES...', or #R$7224. Read from the
; code.
;
; Output:F From $9D95 or $A59F
c$9DFF LD C,$04        ; Load C with $04 - Is Straker here?
 $9E01 CALL $9CCE      ; Call the routine "Is thing C in the same room as the
                       ; current actor, and..." (#R$9CCE)
 $9E04 JR NZ,$9D95     ; No: the usual routine
 $9E06 LD C,$00        ; Load C with $00 - Is the player here?
 $9E08 CALL $9CCE      ; Call the routine "Is thing C in the same room as the
                       ; current actor, and..." (#R$9CCE)
 $9E0B JR NZ,$9D95     ; If the zero flag is clear, go back to the routine
                       ; "Lestrade's after-turn routine, after the..."
                       ; (#R$9D95)
 $9E0D LD A,$04        ; Load A with $04 - Straker is the one speaking
 $9E0F LD ($A034),A    ; Store A in #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables"
 $9E12 LD A,$01        ; Load A with $01 - Lestrade has been introduced
 $9E14 LD ($9D90),A    ; Store A in "Lestrade's flags" (#R$9D90)
 $9E17 LD A,$5C        ; Load A with $5C - Room 92...
 $9E19 CALL $D224      ; Call the routine "Find a room record" (#R$D224)
 $9E1C LD (IX+$0A),$00 ; Set byte +10 of the record at IX to $00
 $9E20 LD A,$3E        ; Load A with $3E - ...and room 62: clear +$0A
 $9E22 CALL $D224      ; Call the routine "Find a room record" (#R$D224)
 $9E25 LD (IX+$0A),$00 ; Set byte +10 of the record at IX to $00
 $9E29 LD HL,$9D95     ; Point HL at "Lestrade's after-turn routine, after
                       ; the..." (#R$9D95) - From now on run #R$9D95 after his
                       ; turns
 $9E2C LD ($9C86),HL   ; Store HL in #R$9C85($9C86) in "After-turn routines for
                       ; nine characters"
 $9E2F LD A,$01        ; Load A with $01
 $9E31 LD ($9D92),A    ; Store A in #R$9D91($9D92) in "Lestrade's flags"
 $9E34 LD A,($9D91)    ; Read #R$9D91 in "Lestrade's flags" into A - Which
                       ; introduction?
 $9E37 AND A           ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                       ; carry)
 $9E38 LD HL,$7207     ; Point HL at the message "YOU SAY" THE OIL LAMP I WOULD
                       ; LIKE YOU TO MEET... (#R$7207)
 $9E3B JR NZ,$9E44     ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$9E44
 $9E3D INC A           ; Add one to A
 $9E3E LD ($9D91),A    ; Store A in #R$9D91 in "Lestrade's flags"
 $9E41 LD HL,$7224     ; "YOU SAY" WELL, HOLMES FANCY SEEING YOU HERE. THE OIL
                       ; LAMP..."
*$9E44 CALL $A59F      ; Call the routine "Print the message at HL" (#R$A59F) -
                       ; Print it
 $9E47 RET             ; Return to the caller

; The last scene: Basil arrives at Victoria (the winning ending)
;
; Run by Phipps's script (#R$EF89) when he walks onto platform one at Victoria
; and finds you there. If Watson (3) and Lestrade (2) are both in your room
; (#R$9E60), ends the game with #R$6AF3: 'AS BASIL ENTERS HE SEES YOU AND PULLS
; A GUN. WITH SUPRISING AGILITY WATSON PUSHES YOU OUT OF THE WAY OF BASILS
; FIRST SHOT ALLOWING LESTRADE TIME TO PULL HIS GUN AND CAPTURE THE CULPRITS.
; AS INCREDIBLE AS IT MAY SEEM YOU HAVE SOLVED THIS CASE. YOU TRULY DESERVE THE
; TITLE OF WORLDS GREATEST DETECTIVE.' - the only winning ending. If either is
; missing, with #R$6B79: 'AS BASIL ENTERS HE SEES YOU AND PULLS A GUN. WITH HIS
; USUAL SKILL HIS FIRST SHOT DISPOSES OF YOU. PERHAPS YOU SHOULD TRY AGAIN.'
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator, all three ways
; (tools/experiments/endgame_test.py).
; .
; Reached by play too: see the winning line under #R$E7E7.
; .
; Played, 9 November: reached by typed commands only (no poke), with Watson and
; Lestrade aboard the 6:29 underground train. With Lestrade left on the
; platform the same room and minute give 'With his usual skill his first shot
; disposes of you.' instead (endings/end5_victoria_without_lestrade).
;
; Output:F The game ends through $9E74
c$9E48 DEFB $00,$00  ; Action record header: flags $00; byte 1 $00
 $9E4A LD HL,$6B79   ; Point HL at the message "AS BASIL ENTERS HE SEES YOU AND
                     ; PULLS A GUN.... (#R$6B79)
 $9E4D LD A,$03      ; Load A with $03
 $9E4F CALL $9E60    ; Call #R$9E60 (inside "The last scene: Basil arrives at
                     ; Victoria...")
 $9E52 JR NZ,$9E74   ; If the zero flag is clear, skip ahead to the routine
                     ; "End the game with the message in HL" (#R$9E74)
 $9E54 LD A,$02      ; Load A with $02
 $9E56 CALL $9E60    ; Call #R$9E60 (inside "The last scene: Basil arrives at
                     ; Victoria...")
 $9E59 JR NZ,$9E74   ; If the zero flag is clear, skip ahead to the routine
                     ; "End the game with the message in HL" (#R$9E74)
 $9E5B LD HL,$6AF3   ; Point HL at the message "AS BASIL ENTERS HE SEES YOU AND
                     ; PULLS A GUN.... (#R$6AF3)
 $9E5E JR $9E74      ; Skip ahead to the routine "End the game with the message
                     ; in HL" (#R$9E74)
*$9E60 CALL $D237    ; Call the routine "Resolve an id (in A) to its full
                     ; entity record" (#R$D237)
 $9E63 LD A,($A009)  ; Read the room the player is in, which decides what is
                     ; shown (#R$A002($A009)) into A
 $9E66 CP (IX+$0F)   ; Compare A with byte +15 of the record at IX
 $9E69 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Game-over record: 'I do not think you are doing too well'
;
; Ends the game with #R$693A unless #R$9D90 is set - that is, unless the player
; has been introduced to Lestrade (see #R$9DFF). Run by Inspector Lestrade's
; script (#R$E932). Confirmed in the emulator: a player who only types WAIT
; from the start gets 'I do not think you are doing too well, surely Sherlock
; Holmes could do a better job than you have done so far. Perhaps you should
; try again.' at 2:59pm on Monday, and the game restarts.
; .
; #R$9E74 is the shared ending: prints the message in HL, waits for a key
; (#R$C306), and restarts (#R$B0AC).
;
; Output:F NZ and nothing printed once Lestrade has been introduced; otherwise
; .        the game ends through $9E74
c$9E6A DEFB $00,$00  ; Action record header: flags $00; byte 1 $00
 $9E6C LD A,($9D90)  ; Read "Lestrade's flags" (#R$9D90) into A
 $9E6F AND A         ; Clear the carry flag for the caller (A is unchanged)
 $9E70 RET NZ        ; If A is not zero, return
 $9E71 LD HL,$693A   ; "I DO NOT THINK YOU ARE DOING TOO WELL, SURELY SHERLOCK
                     ; HO..."

; End the game with the message in HL
;
; Prints HL's message, waits for a key (#R$C306), calls #R$A296 and restarts
; the game (#R$B0AC). Reached from the game-over records at
; #R$9E48-#R$9EBE($9EBF) and from #R$DFCA.
;
;  Input:HL The message to print
; Output:F None: the game restarts through $B0AC
c$9E74 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - Output on
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) = 1)
 $9E76 LD ($5C3C),A  ; Store A in the output-switch byte (TV_FLAG)
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C))
 $9E79 CALL $A59F    ; Print the message in HL (#R$A59F)
 $9E7C CALL $C306    ; Wait for a key (#R$C306)
 $9E7F CALL $A296    ; Clear the text window: 18 newlines (#R$A296)
 $9E82 JP $B0AC      ; Get ready to load a saved game (#R$B0AC)

; Game-over record: the Major is arrested
;
; Does nothing once #R$9E87($9E88) - the operand of its own LD A,$00
; (self-modifying) - is non-zero, which the routine of the case statement MAJOR
; PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT sets when it is proven (#R$9A3D, which also
; prints #R$70F0: 'VERY GOOD HOLMES, I SHALL RETURN TO LEATHER HEAD TOMORROW TO
; HAVE ANOTHER LOOK AROUND.'). Otherwise prints #R$6975 ('AS THE MAJOR ENTERS
; LESTRADE ATTEMPTS TO ARREST HIM...') and ends the game with #R$69BF ('...WELL
; HOLMES THAT WRAPS UP THIS CASE, SORRY YOU WERE NOT OF MORE HELP...') if
; Lestrade is where the player is (#R$A002($A009) = #R$A00A), or #R$6A26
; ('INSPECTOR LESTRADE ENTERS AND SAYS...') if not. Run by Lestrade's script
; (#R$E96B). CORRECTED the same day: this first said the opposite (that it runs
; only when #R$9E87($9E88) is set) - the test is AND A / RET NZ. Read from the
; code.
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/night_test.py: #R$9D90 poked so
; the 3pm ending passes, then only WAIT): Lestrade's script reaches this record
; at about 1am on Tuesday with #R$9E87($9E88) still 0, and the game ends with
; 'Inspector Lestrade enters and says to you " Well Holmes that wraps up this
; case, sorry you were not of more help but you cannot solve them all you know.
; ". Despite his assurances you cannot help feeling there should be more to
; this case. Perhaps you should try again.' (the player was elsewhere, so
; #R$6975 was not shown).
; .
; #R$9E74 is the shared ending: prints the message in HL, waits for a key
; (#R$C306), and restarts (#R$B0AC).
;
; Output:F NZ and nothing printed once $9E88 is non-zero; otherwise the game
; .        ends through $9E74
c$9E85 DEFB $00,$00  ; Action record header: flags $00; byte 1 $00
 $9E87 LD A,$00      ; Load A with $00
 $9E89 AND A         ; Clear the carry flag for the caller (A is unchanged)
 $9E8A RET NZ        ; If A is not zero, return
 $9E8B LD HL,$6975   ; Point HL at the message "AS THE MAJOR ENTERS LESTRADE
                     ; ATTEMPTS TO ARREST... (#R$6975)
 $9E8E CALL $A59F    ; Call the routine "Print the message at HL" (#R$A59F)
 $9E91 LD A,($A009)  ; Read the room the player is in, which decides what is
                     ; shown (#R$A002($A009)) into A
 $9E94 LD HL,$A00A   ; Point HL at the current actor's room (#R$A00A)
 $9E97 CP (HL)       ; Compare A with the byte at the current actor's room
                     ; (#R$A00A)
 $9E98 LD HL,$69BF   ; Point HL at the message "INSPECTOR LESTRADE SAYS TO YOU"
                     ; WELL HOLMES THAT... (#R$69BF)
 $9E9B JR Z,$9E74    ; If A equals the byte at the message "INSPECTOR LESTRADE
                     ; SAYS TO YOU" WELL HOLMES THAT... (#R$69BF), go back to
                     ; the routine "End the game with the message in HL"
                     ; (#R$9E74)
 $9E9D LD HL,$6A26   ; Point HL at the message "INSPECTOR LESTRADE ENTERS AND
                     ; SAYS TO YOU" WELL... (#R$6A26)
 $9EA0 JR $9E74      ; Go back to the routine "End the game with the message in
                     ; HL" (#R$9E74)

; Game-over record: the morning papers
;
; Run by Lestrade's script (#R$EAB3). Unless both MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF
; (#R$9973) and MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER (#R$9981) have been proven (bit
; 1 of their flags), ends with #R$6A33: 'NOT ONLY HAVE YOU FAILED TO SOLVE THE
; CRIMES AT HAND BUT THE MORNING PAPERS CARRY A REPORT OF MR BROWNS LOST P...';
; if both have, with #R$6ABD: 'YOU HAVE DONE EXTREMELY WELL TO SOLVE THE CASE
; AT HAND BUT THE MORNING PAPERS CARRY A REPORT OF MR BROWNS LOST...'. Read
; from the code; both messages rendered only in part.
; .
; #R$9E74 is the shared ending: prints the message in HL, waits for a key
; (#R$C306), and restarts (#R$B0AC).
;
; Output:F The game ends through $9E74 with the failure or success message
c$9EA2 DEFB $00,$00   ; Action record header: flags $00; byte 1 $00
 $9EA4 LD HL,$6A33    ; Point HL at the message "NOT ONLY HAVE YOU FAILED TO
                      ; SOLVE THE CRIMES AT... (#R$6A33)
 $9EA7 LD IX,$9973    ; Point IX at #R$9973 in "The case: statements about the
                      ; crime, and..."
 $9EAB BIT 1,(IX+$03) ; Test bit 1 of byte +3 of the record at IX
 $9EAF JR Z,$9E74     ; If bit 1 of byte +3 of the record at IX is clear, go
                      ; back to the routine "End the game with the message in
                      ; HL" (#R$9E74)
 $9EB1 LD IX,$9981    ; Point IX at #R$9981 in "The case: statements about the
                      ; crime, and..."
 $9EB5 BIT 1,(IX+$03) ; Test bit 1 of byte +3 of the record at IX
 $9EB9 JR Z,$9E74     ; If bit 1 of byte +3 of the record at IX is clear, go
                      ; back to the routine "End the game with the message in
                      ; HL" (#R$9E74)
 $9EBB LD HL,$6ABD    ; Point HL at the message "YOU HAVE DONE EXTREMELY WELL
                      ; TO SOLVE THE CASE... (#R$6ABD)
 $9EBE JR $9E74       ; Go back to the routine "End the game with the message
                      ; in HL" (#R$9E74)

; Strachan's after-turn state
;
; The room Strachan left at bedtime, or 0 while up and about: written and read
; by the shared bedtime code at #R$9ED7 (see #R$9EC1). CORRECTED: was 'not
; traced'.
b$9EC0 DEFB $00

; After-turn routines for Strachan, the cook and the gardener: bedtime
;
; #R$9EC1 (Strachan, with B = room 74, Daphne's room), #R$9ECB (the cook) and
; #R$9ED1 (the gardener, both with B = 0, off stage) share #R$9ED7, with a
; one-byte state each (#R$9EC0, #R$9ECA, #R$9ED0). At 9pm the character's room
; is saved in the state byte, the character moves to room B, is described as
; SLEEPING (word $07F0 written to its +9/+10 by #R$F1C1), stops counting as a
; person (+5 bit 6 cleared, so it cannot be talked to) and has its next turn
; put back 504 to 533 minutes - until about 5:30am. On that next turn it goes
; back to the saved room and is a person again. Confirmed in the emulator for
; the evening (tools/experiments/bedtime_test.py, with #R$9D90 poked so the 3pm
; ending does not come first): at 9:00pm the cook (room 65) and the gardener
; were still about; at 9:05pm both were in room 0 and Strachan in room 74, each
; with +5 gone from $E0 to $A0 (bit 6 cleared) and +9/+10 = $07F0, SLEEPING.
; The return in the morning was not watched.
; .
; The 504 to 533 minutes are only right when the random part is below 8;
; otherwise 256 minutes are lost and the character is back at about 1:30am -
; see the Bugs page.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Each of the three characters has a state byte (the room he left), which is
; 0 while he is about.
; .
; 2. If the state byte is non-zero he is away: put him back in that room with
; no container, clear the state byte, give him no state word and set bit 6 of
; +5 so he is a person to talk to again.
; .
; 3. Otherwise, if it is am, do nothing. If it is not 9 o'clock in the evening,
; do nothing.
; .
; 4. At 9pm: store the room he is in, move him to B (0 for the cook and
; gardener, room 74 for Strachan), and set his next turn 504 plus a random 0 to
; 30 minutes on. Only the low byte takes the random part, so from 8 up the
; carry is lost.
; .
; 5. Give him the state word SLEEPING ($07F0) and clear bit 6 of +5.
;
;  Input:IY The actor's entry in the actor table
; Output:F None used
c$9EC1 LD HL,$9EC0   ; HL = the character's countdown byte (#R$9EC0)
 $9EC4 LD C,$80      ; Load C with $80 - C = $80, B = $4A
 $9EC6 LD B,$4A      ; Load B with $4A
 $9EC8 JR $9ED7      ; Join the common code at #R$9ED7

; The cook's after-turn state
;
; The room the cook left at bedtime, or 0 while up and about: written and read
; by the shared bedtime code at #R$9ED7 (see #R$9EC1). CORRECTED: was 'not
; traced'.
b$9ECA DEFB $00

; After-turn routine: the cook
;
; See #R$9EC1.
;
;  Input:IY The actor's entry in the actor table
; Output:F None used
c$9ECB LD HL,$9ECA   ; Point HL at "The cook's after-turn state" (#R$9ECA)
 $9ECE JR $9ED4      ; Skip ahead to #R$9ED4

; The gardener's after-turn state
;
; The room the gardener left at bedtime, or 0 while up and about: written and
; read by the shared bedtime code at #R$9ED7 (see #R$9EC1). CORRECTED: was 'not
; traced'.
b$9ED0 DEFB $00

; After-turn routine: the gardener
;
; See #R$9EC1.
; .
; At 9pm he is put off stage with the state word SLEEPING ($07F0, looked up
; with source/dictionary.py) and +5 bit 6 cleared (the bit the flag-word table
; at #R$C471 calls DEAD when it is clear); at his next turn he is put back in
; the room he left and woken. Logged in the emulator: off at 9:05pm, back at
; 5:30am, 1:40am and 1:35am - see the Bugs page.
;
;  Input:IY The actor's entry in the actor table
; Output:F None used
c$9ED1 LD HL,$9ED0    ; The gardener's state byte #R$9ED0: the room he left, or
                      ; 0 while about
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$9ECB.
*$9ED4 LD BC,$0000    ; Load BC with $0000 - B = 0, C = 0 (the cook joins here
                      ; too; Strachan comes in at #R$9ED7 with B = 74, C = $80)
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$9EC1.
*$9ED7 LD A,(HL)      ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $9ED8 AND A          ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                      ; carry)
 $9ED9 JR Z,$9EF2     ; Not away: see whether it is time to go
 $9EDB LD B,A         ; Away, and this is the turn to come back: B = the room
                      ; left
 $9EDC XOR A          ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Store 0 at
                      ; #R$9ED0, put him in room B, next turn in 0 minutes
                      ; (#R$9F1E)
 $9EDD LD DE,$0000    ; Load DE with $0000
 $9EE0 CALL $9F1E     ; Call the routine "Store A at HL, put the actor in room
                      ; B, and its next..." (#R$9F1E)
 $9EE3 LD A,($A036)   ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A - State
                      ; word and bits back to none (#R$F1C1, DE = 0, B = C)
 $9EE6 LD B,C         ; Copy C into B
 $9EE7 LD DE,$0000    ; Load DE with $0000
 $9EEA CALL $F1C1     ; Call the routine "Set a thing's state word and state
                      ; bits" (#R$F1C1)
 $9EED SET 6,(IX+$05) ; Set bit 6 of byte +5 of the record at IX - and +5 bit 6
                      ; set: a person again
 $9EF1 RET            ; Return to the caller
*$9EF2 LD A,($9FE2)   ; Read 'a' or 'p' (#R$9FE2) into A - Is it am?
 $9EF5 CP $61         ; Compare A with $61
 $9EF7 RET Z          ; Yes: nothing to do
 $9EF8 LD A,($9FE0)   ; Read the hour, 1-12 (#R$9FE0) into A - Is it 9 (pm)?
 $9EFB CP $09         ; Compare A with $09
 $9EFD RET NZ         ; If A differs from $09, return
 $9EFE LD DE,$01F8    ; DE = 504 minutes (8 hours 24)
 $9F01 LD A,$1E       ; Load A with $1E - A = a random number from 0 to 30
 $9F03 CALL $D2A4     ; Call the routine "A random number from 0 to A"
                      ; (#R$D2A4)
 $9F06 ADD A,E        ; Add E to A - BUG: added to E only - from 8 up the carry
                      ; into D is lost and 256 minutes go with it
 $9F07 LD E,A         ; Copy A into E
 $9F08 LD A,($A00A)   ; Read the current actor's room (#R$A00A) into A -
                      ; Remember the room in the state byte, move to room B (0,
                      ; or 74 for Strachan), next turn DE minutes on
 $9F0B CALL $9F1E     ; Call the routine "Store A at HL, put the actor in room
                      ; B, and its next..." (#R$9F1E)
 $9F0E LD A,($A036)   ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A - State
                      ; word SLEEPING ($07F0), bit 4 of +8 (#R$F1C1)
 $9F11 LD B,$10       ; Load B with $10
 $9F13 LD DE,$07F0    ; Load DE with $07F0
 $9F16 CALL $F1C1     ; Call the routine "Set a thing's state word and state
                      ; bits" (#R$F1C1)
 $9F19 RES 6,(IX+$05) ; Clear bit 6 of byte +5 of the record at IX - and +5 bit
                      ; 6 cleared: no longer a person to talk to
 $9F1D RET            ; Return to the caller

; Store A at HL, put the actor in room B, and its next turn DE minutes on
;
; The actor's own record (#R$A012($A017)) gets room B and no container; the
; actor-table entry at IY has its next-turn minute (+6/+7) moved on by DE. Used
; twice by the gardener's after-turn routine (#R$9ED1): at 9pm he is put off
; stage (room 0, his room remembered at #R$9ED0) with his next turn about 504
; minutes later (plus a random number up to 30, added to the low byte only),
; and on that turn he is put back. Read from the code.
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/phipps_route.py 8): the gardener
; left at 9:05pm each night and came back at 5:30am on Tuesday, but at 1:40am
; on Wednesday and 1:35am on Thursday - see the Bugs page.
;
;  Input:A The value to store
;        HL Where to store it
;        B The room to put the actor in
;        DE The minutes until its next turn
;        IY The actor's entry in the actor table
; Output:IX The actor's own record ($A017)
c$9F1E LD (HL),A       ; Store A at HL
 $9F1F LD IX,($A017)   ; Read #R$A012($A017) in "Game variables" into IX - The
                       ; actor's own record (#R$A012($A017)): its room (+15) =
                       ; B...
 $9F23 LD (IX+$0F),B   ; Store B in byte +15 of the record at IX
 $9F26 LD (IX+$01),$FF ; ...and held by nothing (+1 = $FF)
 $9F2A LD L,(IY+$06)   ; Read byte +6 of the record at IY into L - The actor's
                       ; next turn (IY+6/+7) is DE minutes later
 $9F2D LD H,(IY+$07)   ; Read byte +7 of the record at IY into H
 $9F30 ADD HL,DE       ; Add DE to HL
 $9F31 LD (IY+$06),L   ; Store L in byte +6 of the record at IY
 $9F34 LD (IY+$07),H   ; Store H in byte +7 of the record at IY
 $9F37 RET             ; Return to the caller

; Each verb's default action record
;
; Three-byte records (verb number, address of an action record), ended by $FF,
; searched by #R$D34E from #R$C972. 38 of the 54 verbs have a default here;
; several verbs share one record (all ten directions use #R$DD03; TAKE, GET,
; CARRY, TAKE FROM and TAKE OUT OF all use #R$DCC1; PUT IN, PUT ON and DROP IN
; use #R$DEC1).
; .
; Verbs with no entry here (CLIMB INTO, CLIMB OUT OF, CLOSE, COUNT, GET INTO,
; GET OUT OF, GO TO, HAIL, OPEN, SIT IN, TAKE OFF, TAKE TO, WEAR, LOCK, UNLOCK)
; do nothing unless the object itself lists the verb in its own action list -
; which is why OPEN only ever reached a door's own entry.
; .
; Confirmed by breakpointing #R$C972/#R$C981 during real commands and logging
; the verb and the record found (LOOK 34 -> #R$DC7B, INVENTORY 33 -> #R$DE6F,
; TAKE 41 -> #R$DCC1, DROP 16 -> #R$DC8D, WAIT 49 -> #R$DDB4).
b$9F38 DEFB $01,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(1) (NORTH) -> #R$DD03
 $9F3B DEFB $02,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(2) (SOUTH) -> #R$DD03
 $9F3E DEFB $03,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(3) (EAST) -> #R$DD03
 $9F41 DEFB $04,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(4) (WEST) -> #R$DD03
 $9F44 DEFB $05,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(5) (NORTHEAST) -> #R$DD03
 $9F47 DEFB $06,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(6) (NORTHWEST) -> #R$DD03
 $9F4A DEFB $07,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(7) (SOUTHEAST) -> #R$DD03
 $9F4D DEFB $08,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(8) (SOUTHWEST) -> #R$DD03
 $9F50 DEFB $09,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(9) (UP) -> #R$DD03
 $9F53 DEFB $0A,$03,$DD ; Verb #R$DD03(10) (DOWN) -> #R$DD03
 $9F56 DEFB $29,$C1,$DC ; Verb #R$DCC1(41) (TAKE) -> #R$DCC1
 $9F59 DEFB $17,$C1,$DC ; Verb #R$DCC1(23) (GET) -> #R$DCC1
 $9F5C DEFB $2C,$C1,$DC ; Verb #R$DCC1(44) (TAKE OUT OF) -> #R$DCC1
 $9F5F DEFB $2A,$C1,$DC ; Verb #R$DCC1(42) (TAKE FROM) -> #R$DCC1
 $9F62 DEFB $10,$8D,$DC ; Verb #R$DC8D(16) (DROP) -> #R$DC8D
 $9F65 DEFB $22,$7B,$DC ; Verb #R$DC7B(34) (LOOK) -> #R$DC7B
 $9F68 DEFB $21,$6F,$DE ; Verb #R$DE6F(33) (INVENTORY) -> #R$DE6F
 $9F6B DEFB $13,$40,$DF ; Verb #R$DF40(19) (EXAMINE) -> #R$DF40
 $9F6E DEFB $14,$35,$E1 ; Verb #R$E135(20) (EXAMINE CLOSELY) -> #R$E135
 $9F71 DEFB $1A,$02,$DF ; Verb #R$DF02(26) (GIVE TO) -> #R$DF02
 $9F74 DEFB $12,$52,$DD ; Verb #R$DD52(18) (ENTER) -> #R$DD52
 $9F77 DEFB $1B,$52,$DD ; Verb #R$DD52(27) (GO INTO) -> #R$DD52
 $9F7A DEFB $2E,$9E,$DD ; Verb #R$DD9E(46) (TALK TO) -> #R$DD9E
 $9F7D DEFB $0B,$C1,$DC ; Verb #R$DCC1(11) (CARRY) -> #R$DCC1
 $9F80 DEFB $11,$C1,$DE ; Verb #R$DEC1(17) (DROP IN) -> #R$DEC1
 $9F83 DEFB $26,$C1,$DE ; Verb #R$DEC1(38) (PUT IN) -> #R$DEC1
 $9F86 DEFB $27,$C1,$DE ; Verb #R$DEC1(39) (PUT ON) -> #R$DEC1
 $9F89 DEFB $15,$52,$E4 ; Verb #R$E452(21) (FOLLOW) -> #R$E452
 $9F8C DEFB $16,$52,$E4 ; Verb #R$E452(22) (COME WITH) -> #R$E452
 $9F8F DEFB $32,$D0,$DD ; Verb #R$DDD0(50) (WAIT UNTIL) -> #R$DDD0
 $9F92 DEFB $31,$B4,$DD ; Verb #R$DDB4(49) (WAIT HERE) -> #R$DDB4
 $9F95 DEFB $25,$70,$E3 ; Verb #R$E370(37) (PAY TO) -> #R$E370
 $9F98 DEFB $1C,$57,$DC ; Verb #R$DC57(28) (GO THROUGH) -> #R$DC57
 $9F9B DEFB $23,$19,$DC ; Verb #R$DC19(35) (LOOK THROUGH) -> #R$DC19
 $9F9E DEFB $30,$D6,$E1 ; Verb #R$E1D6(48) (TELL ABOUT) -> #R$E1D6
 $9FA1 DEFB $2F,$D6,$E1 ; Verb #R$E1D6(47) (TELL) -> #R$E1D6
 $9FA4 DEFB $20,$7E,$9A ; Verb #R$9A7E(32) (HELLO) -> #R$9A7E
 $9FA7 DEFB $34,$C5,$9F ; Verb #R$9FC5(52) (KILL) -> #R$9FC5
 $9FAA DEFB $FF         ; End marker

; Verbs 128-140: the verbs only plan scripts use
;
; A DO instruction in a plan script with a verb number of 128 or more comes
; here instead of to the parser's verb table: #R$C4D1 sends verbs with bit 7
; set to #R$C583, which takes the record for verb 128+n from entry n. The
; player can never type these. Records with flags bit 7 set are sub-scripts.
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator over 84 turns: verb #R$E5AD(131) succeeded 864
; times, every time with the direct object really in the room named by the
; indirect object; verb #R$E497(128) ran 4 times (Inspector Lestrade moving
; himself to room 53, the Major to room 92, and two others) and each time the
; object ended up in the named room. Verb #R$F1D6(136) is checked in the Trivia
; entry on appointments. The other verbs' labels are read from their records'
; code.
; .
; Verb #R$E5D8(129) was checked the same way: in 84 turns its comparison ran
; 482 times and came out equal exactly when the two things' rooms (record +$0F)
; were equal.
; .
; CORRECTED: an earlier version said where the index came from was not traced
; and that some records 'do not read as code' - they are plan scripts.
; .
; Found the hard way: once the verb default table at #R$9F38 was marked as
; data, the automatic split's code block starting at #R$9FC3 - in the middle of
; this table - collided with the action record at #R$9FC5, and the round trip
; failed until this table was marked as data too.
w$9FAB DEFW $E497    ; Verb #R$E497(128): #R$E497 - PUT: move the direct object
                     ; into room (indirect object); room 0 is off stage
 $9FAD DEFW $E5D8    ; Verb #R$E5D8(129): #R$E5D8 - IS WITH: are the direct and
                     ; indirect objects in the same room?
 $9FAF DEFW $E5FD    ; Verb #R$E5FD(130): #R$E5FD - IS HERE: is the direct
                     ; object with the actor (#R$C863)?
 $9FB1 DEFW $E5AD    ; Verb #R$E5AD(131): #R$E5AD - IS IN: is the direct object
                     ; in room (indirect object)?
 $9FB3 DEFW $F210    ; Verb #R$F210(132): #R$F210 - LOCK: set bit 0 (locked) of
                     ; the direct object's +5 flags
 $9FB5 DEFW $F203    ; Verb #R$F203(133): #R$F203 - UNLOCK: clear bit 0
                     ; (locked) of the direct object's +5 flags
 $9FB7 DEFW $EB8B    ; Verb #R$EB8B(134): #R$EB8B - sub-script: FOLLOW the
                     ; direct object until in room (indirect object)
 $9FB9 DEFW $ED28    ; Verb #R$ED28(135): #R$ED28 - sub-script: OPEN, GO
                     ; THROUGH and CLOSE a door
 $9FBB DEFW $F1D6    ; Verb #R$F1D6(136): #R$F1D6 - IS HOUR: is the clock's
                     ; hour (1-24) the direct object?
 $9FBD DEFW $EB9B    ; Verb #R$EB9B(137): #R$EB9B - sub-script: do verb (direct
                     ; object), then EXAMINE the indirect object
 $9FBF DEFW $EDE9    ; Verb #R$EDE9(138): #R$EDE9 - sub-script: go through a
                     ; door (135), then CHANCE (indirect object)
 $9FC1 DEFW $EDDD    ; Verb #R$EDDD(139): #R$EDDD - sub-script: do verb (direct
                     ; object), then CHANCE (indirect object)
 $9FC3 DEFW $F2A5    ; Verb #R$F2A5(140): #R$F2A5 - sub-script: #R$E4C9 on the
                     ; direct object, then WAIT (indirect object) minutes

; Default action: KILL (also BREAK and a profane synonym)
;
; The pre-check is the body itself: it always refuses, handing back #R$9FCE,
; which prints message #R$680B. Confirmed in the emulator: BREAK LAMP and KILL
; WATSON both answer 'ONLY AN IDIOT WOULD TRY THAT.'
;
; Output:HL $9FCE, the routine that prints the refusal
;        A 0 (refused)
c$9FC5 DEFB $40,$00,$C9,$9F ; Action record header: flags $40; bit 6: pre-check
                            ; at #R$9FC9; byte 1 $00
 $9FC9 LD HL,$9FCE          ; Always refuse...
 $9FCC XOR A                ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the
                            ; caller
 $9FCD RET                  ; Return to the caller
 $9FCE LD HL,$680B          ; "ONLY AN IDIOT WOULD TRY THAT."
 $9FD1 JP $A59F             ; Jump on to the routine "Print the message at HL"
                            ; (#R$A59F)

; Unused
s$9FD4 DEFS $02

; Game variables
;
; The plan-script registers, the clock and the heads of the engine's linked
; lists (each list's head word doubles as the link of a pseudo-node 2 bytes
; further on, which is the address the list walk #R$A0F7 starts from: #R$9FE7
; for countdowns, #R$9FE9 for tokens, #R$9FEB for frames, #R$9FF0 for orders).
; The clock fields were read from #R$BFD9; the rest from their users. Bytes not
; listed are not identified yet: among them #R$A002($A009) (the room the player
; is in, which decides what is shown), #R$A00A (the current actor's room),
; #R$A00A($A00F)/#R$A00A($A010) (requirement codes),
; #R$A012($A013)/#R$A012($A015) (the direct and indirect objects' records),
; #R$A01A($A01F) (minutes the action takes), #R$A032($A033)-#R$A032($A036)
; (verb, direct object, indirect object, actor).
; .
; CORRECTED: the automatic split had this as code.
b$9FD6 DEFB $00                             ; #R$9FD6: the plan's verb
                                            ; (argument $FA)
 $9FD7 DEFB $00                             ; #R$9FD7: the plan's direct object
                                            ; (argument $FD)
 $9FD8 DEFB $00                             ; #R$9FD8: the plan's indirect
                                            ; object (argument $FC)
 $9FD9 DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FD9: the plan instruction
                                            ; being run
 $9FDB DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FDB: the current actor's
                                            ; record in the actor table
 $9FDD DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FDD: minutes since the start
 $9FDF DEFB $3B                             ; #R$9FDF: the minute
 $9FE0 DEFB $07                             ; #R$9FE0: the hour, 1-12
 $9FE1 DEFB $00                             ; #R$9FE1: the day (0 = Monday)
 $9FE2 DEFB $61                             ; #R$9FE2: 'a' or 'p'
 $9FE3 DEFW $9570                           ; #R$9FE3: the heap's free list
 $9FE5 DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FE5: countdowns (2-byte
                                            ; nodes: actor, count)
 $9FE7 DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FE7: SIGNAL tokens (2-byte
                                            ; nodes: from, to)
 $9FE9 DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FE9: sub-script call frames
                                            ; (7-byte nodes)
 $9FEB DEFB $00                             ; #R$9FEB: not identified (#R$D781
                                            ; clears it)
 $9FEC DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FEC: quoted speech waiting to
                                            ; be handed over (#R$B049, #R$B86A)
 $9FEE DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FEE: orders and remarks
                                            ; waiting for actors (8-byte nodes)
 $9FF0 DEFW $0000                           ; #R$9FF0: words the player has
                                            ; said, for the next remark
 $9FF2 DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00
 $9FFA DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00
 $A002 DEFB $71,$09,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00
 $A00A DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00
 $A012 DEFB $FF,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00
 $A01A DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00
 $A022 DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$01,$00,$00,$00
 $A02A DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00
 $A032 DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00
 $A03A DEFB $00,$00,$00,$04,$40,$58

; Start the game
;
; The game's own entry point (the tape loader jumps here once everything is
; loaded - see loader_notes.md). Clears two flag bytes, resets the stack to
; #R$5D80 (reusing the loader's own workspace, which is no longer needed), and
; clears the screen.
; .
; Then it waits for a single keypress to choose New Game vs Continue: pressing
; 'n' or 'N' starts a new game (clears a flag at #R$A00A($A011)); anything else
; leaves that flag set, and the code that follows loads state back from a table
; at #R$9860, so this is very likely also how a restored/continued game resumes
; its characters' positions (not yet confirmed with an actual saved game in
; hand - see verification.md's 'say which things are verified' guidance).
; .
; Seeds a simple RNG from the Z80 refresh register (R) - a common
; period-accurate trick, since R free-runs on every instruction fetch and so is
; effectively unpredictable at the moment the player first presses a key.
; .
; CORRECTED 2026-11-07: the actor-table walk that follows is not part of
; start-up. From #R$A064 on is the game's main loop - see there.
;
; Output:F None: this routine never returns, it falls into the main loop
c$A040 XOR A         ; Clear the 'currently examining' flag
 $A041 LD ($D6B8),A  ; Store A in "A flag of the player's script step, and
                     ; spare..." (#R$D6B8)
 $A044 LD ($A01B),A  ; Clear another one-shot flag
 $A047 DI            ; No interrupts while we set things up
 $A048 CALL $C14A    ; Clear the screen and set up the text window
 $A04B LD SP,$5D80   ; Stack now safely below the game's own code/data
 $A04E CALL $C415    ; Wait here for the player's very first keypress
 $A051 CP $6E        ; Did they press lowercase 'n'?
 $A053 JR Z,$A05B    ; If A equals $6E, skip ahead to #R$A05B
 $A055 CP $4E        ; ...or uppercase 'N'?
 $A057 LD A,$01      ; Default: treat as "continue" (flag = 1)
 $A059 JR NZ,$A05C   ; If A differs from $4E, skip ahead to #R$A05C
*$A05B XOR A         ; "New game" was chosen instead (flag = 0)
*$A05C LD ($A011),A  ; Store A in #R$A00A($A011) in "Game variables"
 $A05F LD A,R        ; A = the free-running refresh register...
 $A061 LD ($A037),A  ; ...used as an unpredictable RNG seed

; The main loop: one minute of game time per pass
;
; Each pass is one minute. The clock moves on (#R$BFD9) and is printed
; (#R$BF79); then every actor in the table at #R$9860 whose next-turn time
; (+6/+7) has come takes its turn. A turn starts with #R$CC37 (find the actor,
; check for orders or remarks waiting for it) and then runs plan-script steps
; (#R$CC78) until one takes time (#R$A01A($A01F) non-zero); that many minutes
; are added to the actor's next-turn time, and the actor's own after-turn
; routine, if it has one, runs (#R$9C77). The player is an actor like the rest:
; the player's script step is the one that waits for a typed command.
; .
; Before the walk, if the player is in the middle of WAIT UNTIL (plan verb
; #R$DDD0(50)) and ENTER is pressed, the player's next-turn time is pulled back
; to now, so a long wait can be cut short.
; .
; #R$A0D9($A0DA) is set at the end of the minute's walk to leave the loop: 1
; (#R$B0AC, after a game-over message) loads a saved game (#R$B0D7) and starts
; again from #R$A040; 2 (#R$B0C6, not traced back to the command that sets it)
; saves the game (#R$B0E4) and carries on.
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator: the player's +6/+7 equals the minute counter
; (#R$9FDD) whenever the game waits for a command, and moves on by 5 for WAIT
; and by 1 for TAKE LAMP or EXAMINE LAMP; Watson's and Lestrade's move on in
; step. The per-actor call counts in the #R$B91F note (#R$CC37 once per actor
; per turn) agree.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Clear the ending flag #R$A0D9($A0DA). If it is set on a later pass, go to
; the ending code at #R$A0E7.
; .
; 2. Move the clock on one minute (#R$BFD9) and print it (#R$BF79).
; .
; 3. If the player is waiting until a time (plan verb #R$DDD0(50)) and ENTER is
; held down, make the player's next turn now.
; .
; 4. Walk the actor table from #R$9860 in 8-byte steps until the $FF marker.
; For each actor whose time has come (#R$A0DB): start its turn (#R$CC37), then
; run plan steps (#R$CC78) until one takes time (#R$A01A($A01F) non-zero).
; .
; 5. Add that time to the actor's next-turn time and run the actor's after-turn
; routine (#R$9C77).
; .
; 6. At the end of the table, loop for the next minute. When the ending flag is
; 2 the game is saved (#R$B0E4) and the loop continues; otherwise it is loaded
; (#R$B0D7) and the game restarts at #R$A040.
;
; Output:F None: the loop never ends by itself; $A0DA stops it
c$A064 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Not ending yet
 $A065 LD ($A0DA),A  ; Store A in #R$A0D9($A0DA) in "The main loop: one minute
                     ; of game time per..."
*$A068 LD A,($A0DA)  ; Read #R$A0D9($A0DA) in "The main loop: one minute of
                     ; game time per..." into A - Is the game ending?
 $A06B AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A06C JR NZ,$A0E7   ; Yes: restart or reload
 $A06E CALL $BFD9    ; A minute passes
 $A071 CALL $BF79    ; Print the clock
 $A074 LD IY,$9860   ; The player (first actor)...
 $A078 LD A,(IY+$03) ; Read byte +3 of the record at IY into A - ...waiting
                     ; until a time (plan verb #R$DDD0(50))?
 $A07B CP $32        ; Compare A with $32
 $A07D JR NZ,$A093   ; If A differs from $32, skip ahead to #R$A093
 $A07F CALL $C41F    ; Call the routine "GET-KEY: has the player pressed a
                     ; key?" (#R$C41F) - Is a key down...
 $A082 BIT 7,A       ; Test bit 7 of A
 $A084 JR Z,$A093    ; If bit 7 of A is clear, skip ahead to #R$A093
 $A086 CP $8D        ; Compare A with $8D - ...and is it ENTER?
 $A088 JR NZ,$A093   ; If A differs from $8D, skip ahead to #R$A093
 $A08A LD HL,($9FDD) ; Read minutes since the start (#R$9FDD) into HL - Then
                     ; the player acts now: next-turn time = this minute
 $A08D LD (IY+$06),L ; Store L in byte +6 of the record at IY
 $A090 LD (IY+$07),H ; Store H in byte +7 of the record at IY
*$A093 LD IY,$9860   ; Walk the actor table
*$A097 LD A,(IY+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IY into A - End of
                     ; the table?
 $A09A INC A         ; Add one to A
 $A09B JR Z,$A068    ; Yes: the next minute
 $A09D LD HL,($9FDD) ; Read minutes since the start (#R$9FDD) into HL - Has
                     ; this actor's time come?
 $A0A0 CALL $A0DB    ; Call #R$A0DB (inside "The main loop: one minute of game
                     ; time per...")
 $A0A3 JR C,$A0D1    ; Not yet: next actor
 $A0A5 LD A,(IY+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IY into A - Start
                     ; its turn
 $A0A8 PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack
 $A0AA PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A0AB CALL $CC37    ; Call the routine "Start an actor's turn" (#R$CC37)
*$A0AE POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack - One plan step
 $A0AF PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A0B0 CALL $CC78    ; Call the routine "Run one step of the current actor's
                     ; plan script" (#R$CC78)
 $A0B3 LD HL,($A01F) ; Read #R$A01A($A01F) in "Game variables" into HL - Did it
                     ; take any time?
 $A0B6 LD A,L        ; Copy L into A
 $A0B7 OR H          ; Set in A the bits that are set in H
 $A0B8 JR Z,$A0AE    ; If the result is zero, go back to #R$A0AE - No: another
                     ; step
 $A0BA POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A0BB POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A0BD LD E,(IY+$06) ; Read byte +6 of the record at IY into E - Add the time
                     ; to its next-turn time
 $A0C0 LD D,(IY+$07) ; Read byte +7 of the record at IY into D
 $A0C3 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A0C4 LD (IY+$06),L ; Store L in byte +6 of the record at IY
 $A0C7 LD (IY+$07),H ; Store H in byte +7 of the record at IY
 $A0CA PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack - Its own after-turn routine, if
                     ; any
 $A0CC CALL $9C77    ; Call the routine "Run the current actor's own after-turn
                     ; routine" (#R$9C77)
 $A0CF POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
*$A0D1 LD DE,$0008   ; Load DE with $0008, the amount that is added next - Next
                     ; actor (8 bytes on)
 $A0D4 ADD IY,DE     ; Add DE to IY
 $A0D6 JR $A097      ; Go back to #R$A097
 $A0D8 INC C         ; Add one to C
 $A0D9 OR $00        ; Set bits  of A (mask $00)
*$A0DB LD E,(IY+$06) ; Read byte +6 of the record at IY into E - Compare the
                     ; minute counter (HL) with the actor's next-turn time
 $A0DE LD D,(IY+$07) ; Read byte +7 of the record at IY into D
 $A0E1 LD A,H        ; Copy H into A
 $A0E2 CP D          ; Compare A with D
 $A0E3 RET NZ        ; If A differs from D, return
 $A0E4 LD A,L        ; Copy L into A
 $A0E5 CP E          ; Compare A with E
 $A0E6 RET           ; Return to the caller
*$A0E7 CP $02        ; Compare A with $02 - Ending: 2 = carry on (reloaded?), 1
                     ; = start again
 $A0E9 JR Z,$A0F1    ; If A equals $02, skip ahead to #R$A0F1
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$C1CB.
*$A0EB CALL $B0D7    ; Load: restore the saved game (#R$B0D7)...
 $A0EE JP $A040      ; ...and carry on at #R$A040
*$A0F1 CALL $B0E4    ; Save: write the game out (#R$B0E4)...
 $A0F4 JP $A064      ; ...and carry on at #R$A064

; Move IX to the next node of a list
;
; Every engine list links its nodes through the 2 bytes in front of each node
; (IX-2/IX-1). Z at the end of the list.
;
;  Input:IX A node of a list
; Output:IX The next node
;        F Z at the end of the list (A = 0)
c$A0F7 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - HL = IX's link (IX-2), the next
                     ; node; A = 0 and Z at the end of the list
 $A0F8 LD L,(IX-$02) ; Read byte -2 of the record at IX into L
 $A0FB LD H,(IX-$01) ; Read byte -1 of the record at IX into H
 $A0FE LD A,L        ; Copy L into A
 $A0FF OR H          ; Set in A the bits that are set in H
 $A100 EX (SP),HL    ; Swap HL with the word on top of the stack - IX = the
                     ; next node
 $A101 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A103 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Find the node before node DE in the list at IY
;
; Leaves IY at the node (or list head) whose link points at DE, so the caller
; can unlink DE.
;
;  Input:IY The list head
;        DE The node to find
; Output:IY The node (or list head) whose link points at DE
c$A104 INC IY        ; Step IY on to the next byte - Start after the list
                     ; head's own link
 $A106 INC IY        ; Step IY on to the next byte
*$A108 LD L,(IY-$02) ; Read byte -2 of the record at IY into L - HL = the link
                     ; (the next node's address) at IY - 2
 $A10B LD H,(IY-$01) ; Read byte -1 of the record at IY into H
 $A10E AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A10F PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A110 SBC HL,DE     ; Subtract DE and the carry from HL - Is it node DE? Then
                     ; IY is the node before it
 $A112 JR Z,$A118    ; If the two values are equal, skip ahead to #R$A118
 $A114 POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack - No: step to that node and
                     ; look again
 $A116 JR $A108      ; Go back to #R$A108
*$A118 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A119 RET           ; Return to the caller

; $A11C: the free-list link before the best block so far
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A11C.
b$A11A DEFW $0000    ; Link address

; Allocate A + 3 bytes from the free list, cleared
;
; Walks the free blocks from #R$9FE3 (each starts with its size and a link) and
; takes the smallest that is big enough - best fit. If what would be left is
; under 4 bytes the whole block is unlinked, otherwise it is split. The block
; is zeroed, its size stored before it, and IX returned on it with NZ. No block
; big enough: #R$C1D8 (out of memory). Word lists, remarks and orders are built
; in these blocks; #R$A1AF frees them. Read from the code.
; .
; Checked 9 November over the first 120 commands of the winning route: 2957
; runs, every one met its stated postcondition (the block from IX-2 for A+2
; bytes is zero, IX is non-zero, and the size byte at IX-3 is A+3).
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. C = the number of bytes wanted + 3 (the size field and the link).
; .
; 2. Walk the free list from #R$9FE3. Each free block begins with its size and
; a link. Blocks that are too small are passed over.
; .
; 3. Of those that are big enough, remember the smallest (best fit), and the
; link that points to it.
; .
; 4. If none fits, go to the out-of-memory routine at #R$C1D8.
; .
; 5. If the chosen block would leave 4 bytes or more, split it: the free block
; keeps the front part and the end is handed out. Otherwise the whole block is
; taken and unhooked from the free list.
; .
; 6. Store the size before the block, clear its data and return NZ with IX at
; the block.
;
;  Input:A The number of data bytes wanted (3 more are used for the header)
; Output:IX The cleared block
;        F NZ on success; out of memory ends in $C1D8
c$A11C PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack - Save IY, HL, DE, BC
 $A11E PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A11F PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A120 PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack
 $A121 ADD A,$03     ; Add $03 to A - C = the size wanted, A + 3
 $A123 LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $A124 LD B,$00      ; B bit 0: a block has been chosen
 $A126 LD HL,($9FE3) ; Read the heap's free list (#R$9FE3) into HL - HL = the
                     ; first free block; IY = the link that points to it
 $A129 LD IY,$9FE3   ; Point IY at the heap's free list (#R$9FE3)
*$A12D LD A,L        ; Copy L into A - End of the free list?
 $A12E OR H          ; Set in A the bits that are set in H
 $A12F JR Z,$A160    ; If the result is zero, jump on to #R$A160
 $A131 LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E - DE = this block's
                     ; size; HL on to its link
 $A132 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A133 LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $A134 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A135 LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - A size over 255 is always big enough
 $A136 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A137 JR NZ,$A13D   ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$A13D
 $A139 LD A,E        ; Copy E into A - Under 256: too small for C?
 $A13A CP C          ; Compare A with C
 $A13B JR C,$A157    ; Yes: next block
*$A13D BIT 0,B       ; Test bit 0 of B - Big enough. Nothing chosen yet?
 $A13F JR Z,$A14E    ; If bit 0 of B is clear, skip ahead to #R$A14E
 $A141 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - Otherwise is it smaller than the
                     ; chosen block (size at IX-2)?
 $A142 LD L,(IX-$02) ; Read byte -2 of the record at IX into L
 $A145 LD H,(IX-$01) ; Read byte -1 of the record at IX into H
 $A148 AND A         ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that
                     ; follows is a plain one (a compare)
 $A149 SBC HL,DE     ; Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller,
                     ; zero = equal)
 $A14B POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A14C JR C,$A157    ; No: keep the one we have
*$A14E PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - Choose it: IX = its link field
 $A14F POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A151 LD ($A11A),IY ; Store IY in "#R$A11C: the free-list link before the best
                     ; block..." (#R$A11A) - and remember the link that points
                     ; to it
 $A155 SET 0,B       ; Set bit 0 of B
*$A157 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - IY = this block's link field;
                     ; follow it to the next block
 $A158 POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A15A LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E
 $A15B INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A15C LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $A15D EX DE,HL      ; Swap DE and HL
 $A15E JR $A12D      ; Go back to #R$A12D
*$A160 BIT 0,B       ; Test bit 0 of B - End of the list: was anything chosen?
 $A162 JR Z,$A1AC    ; If bit 0 of B is clear, jump on to #R$A1AC
 $A164 LD L,(IX-$02) ; Read byte -2 of the record at IX into L - HL = what
                     ; would be left of the chosen block after taking C
 $A167 LD H,(IX-$01) ; Read byte -1 of the record at IX into H
 $A16A LD B,$00      ; Load B with $00
 $A16C AND A         ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that
                     ; follows is a plain one (a compare)
 $A16D SBC HL,BC     ; Subtract BC from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller,
                     ; zero = equal)
 $A16F LD A,H        ; Copy H into A
 $A170 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A171 JR NZ,$A18A   ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$A18A - 256 or more:
                     ; split it
 $A173 LD A,L        ; Copy L into A
 $A174 CP $04        ; Compare A with $04 - 4 or more: split it
 $A176 JR NC,$A18A   ; If A is $04 or more, skip ahead to #R$A18A
 $A178 LD IY,($A11A) ; Read "#R$A11C: the free-list link before the best
                     ; block..." (#R$A11A) into IY - Under 4: take the whole
                     ; block - point the previous link past it
 $A17C LD A,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into A
 $A17F LD (IY+$00),A ; Store A in the first byte of the record at IY
 $A182 LD A,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into A
 $A185 LD (IY+$01),A ; Store A in byte +1 of the record at IY
 $A188 JR $A193      ; Skip ahead to #R$A193
*$A18A LD (IX-$02),L ; Store L in byte -2 of the record at IX - Split: the free
                     ; block keeps the first part, now HL bytes
 $A18D LD (IX-$01),H ; Store H in byte -1 of the record at IX
 $A190 EX DE,HL      ; Swap DE and HL - and IX moves to the part being handed
                     ; out
 $A191 ADD IX,DE     ; Add DE to IX
*$A193 INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte - Store the block's size
                     ; before it
 $A195 LD (IX-$03),C ; Store C in byte -3 of the record at IX
 $A198 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - Clear the C - 1 bytes of the
                     ; block
 $A19A POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A19B DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $A19C DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $A19D LD B,C        ; Copy C into B
 $A19E DEC B         ; Subtract one from B
 $A19F XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
*$A1A0 LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to
 $A1A1 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A1A2 DJNZ $A1A0    ; Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back to
                     ; #R$A1A0
 $A1A4 OR $01        ; Set bit 0 of A - NZ: success, IX = the block
 $A1A6 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A1A7 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A1A8 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A1A9 POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A1AB RET           ; Return to the caller
*$A1AC JP $C1D8      ; Nothing big enough: out of memory

; Free every node of the list starting at IX
;
; Used by the routines at #R$ACFB, #R$B6FD, #R$BA20, #R$D87D and #R$D88A.
;
;  Input:IX The first node of the list
; Output:The whole list is freed
c$A1AF PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - HL = the first node
 $A1B0 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A1B2 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A1B3 JR $A1C1      ; Skip ahead to #R$A1C1
*$A1B5 LD L,(IX-$02) ; Read byte -2 of the record at IX into L - HL = this
                     ; node's link (IX-2)
 $A1B8 LD H,(IX-$01) ; Read byte -1 of the record at IX into H
 $A1BB CALL $A1C7    ; Call the routine "Free node IX back to the heap"
                     ; (#R$A1C7) - Free this node (#R$A1C7)
 $A1BE PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A1BF POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
*$A1C1 LD A,L        ; Copy L into A - Until the link is 0
 $A1C2 OR H          ; Set in A the bits that are set in H
 $A1C3 JR NZ,$A1B5   ; If the result is not zero, go back to #R$A1B5
 $A1C5 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A1C6 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Free node IX back to the heap
;
; Puts the block back in the free list in address order and joins it to free
; neighbours (#R$A20C).
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Save HL and DE, and work out where the freed block starts: three bytes
; before the node in IX.
; .
; 2. Walk the list of free blocks from its head at #R$9FE3. The list is kept in
; address order, so stop at the first free block that lies above this one, or
; at the end of the list.
; .
; 3. Link the block in at that point: the previous link now points at it, and
; its own link points at the block that came after.
; .
; 4. Try to join it to the free block that follows it (#R$A20C). If the freed
; block joined onto the one before it instead, step back to that block and try
; again to join it to what follows.
;
; Input:IX The node to free
;       IX is not preserved
c$A1C7 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A1C8 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A1C9 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - HL = the block's size byte, three
                     ; bytes before the node
 $A1CB POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A1CC DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $A1CD DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $A1CE DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $A1CF LD IX,$9FE3   ; Point IX at the heap's free list (#R$9FE3) - Walk the
                     ; free list from #R$9FE3 to the first block above this one
*$A1D3 LD E,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into E
 $A1D6 LD D,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into D
 $A1D9 LD A,D        ; Copy D into A
 $A1DA OR E          ; Set in A the bits that are set in E
 $A1DB JR Z,$A1EB    ; If the result is zero, skip ahead to #R$A1EB - (end of
                     ; the list: put it last)
 $A1DD PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A1DE AND A         ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that
                     ; follows is a plain one (a compare)
 $A1DF SBC HL,DE     ; Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller,
                     ; zero = equal)
 $A1E1 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A1E2 JR C,$A1EB    ; If the carry flag is set, skip ahead to #R$A1EB
 $A1E4 INC DE        ; Step DE on to the next byte
 $A1E5 INC DE        ; Step DE on to the next byte
 $A1E6 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A1E7 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A1E9 JR $A1D3      ; Go back to #R$A1D3
*$A1EB LD (IX+$00),L ; Store L in the first byte of the record at IX - Link it
                     ; in: the previous link now points here,
 $A1EE LD (IX+$01),H ; Store H in byte +1 of the record at IX
 $A1F1 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte - its size's high byte
                     ; cleared, and its own link to the next free block
 $A1F2 LD (HL),$00   ; Store $00 at the address HL points to
 $A1F4 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A1F5 LD (HL),E     ; Store E at the address HL points to
 $A1F6 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A1F7 LD (HL),D     ; Store D at the address HL points to
 $A1F8 CALL $A20C    ; Call the routine "Join free block IX to the next one if
                     ; they touch" (#R$A20C) - Join it to the block after it if
                     ; they touch (#R$A20C)...
 $A1FB JR Z,$A206    ; If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$A206
 $A1FD LD L,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into L - ...and
                     ; the block before it to this one
 $A200 LD H,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into H
 $A203 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A204 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
*$A206 CALL $A20C    ; Call the routine "Join free block IX to the next one if
                     ; they touch" (#R$A20C)
 $A209 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A20A POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A20B RET           ; Return to the caller

; Join free block IX to the next one if they touch
;
; Used by #R$A1C7. NZ if there is no next free block; Z after joining (sizes
; added, link taken over).
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Work out the address just past this free block: its start plus the size
; held in its first two bytes.
; .
; 2. Read the link to the next free block. If there is none, return NZ.
; .
; 3. If the next free block does not start at exactly that address, return NZ:
; there is a gap between them.
; .
; 4. Otherwise add the next block's size to this one's and take over the next
; block's link, which unhooks it from the list, and return Z.
;
;  Input:IX The link field of a free block (its size is the word before it)
; Output:F Z if the next free block was joined on, NZ if not
c$A20C PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - HL = the start of this block (the
                     ; size word sits at IX-2)
 $A20E POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A20F DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $A210 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $A211 LD E,(IX-$02) ; Read byte -2 of the record at IX into E - Add its size:
                     ; HL = the address just past it
 $A214 LD D,(IX-$01) ; Read byte -1 of the record at IX into D
 $A217 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A218 LD E,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into E - DE =
                     ; the link to the next free block
 $A21B LD D,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into D
 $A21E LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - No next block: return NZ (nothing to
                     ; join)
 $A21F OR E          ; Set in A the bits that are set in E
 $A220 JR NZ,$A225   ; If the result is not zero, skip ahead to #R$A225
 $A222 OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $A224 RET           ; Return to the caller
*$A225 AND A         ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that
                     ; follows is a plain one (a compare) - Does this block end
                     ; where the next one starts? If not, return NZ
 $A226 SBC HL,DE     ; Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller,
                     ; zero = equal)
 $A228 RET NZ        ; If the two values differ, return
 $A229 LD L,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into L - Yes: HL
                     ; points at the next block
 $A22C LD H,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into H
 $A22F PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack
 $A230 LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E - DE = its size, BC =
                     ; its link
 $A231 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A232 LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $A233 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A234 LD C,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into C
 $A235 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A236 LD B,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into B
 $A237 LD L,(IX-$02) ; Read byte -2 of the record at IX into L - Add its size
                     ; to this block's
 $A23A LD H,(IX-$01) ; Read byte -1 of the record at IX into H
 $A23D ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A23E LD (IX-$02),L ; Store L in byte -2 of the record at IX
 $A241 LD (IX-$01),H ; Store H in byte -1 of the record at IX
 $A244 LD (IX+$00),C ; Store C in the first byte of the record at IX - and take
                     ; over its link, unhooking it from the chain
 $A247 LD (IX+$01),B ; Store B in byte +1 of the record at IX
 $A24A POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A24B XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; Return Z: joined
 $A24C RET           ; Return to the caller

; Articles: A / AN / SOME, or THE
;
; Two tables of four word references, chosen between by #R$A7EB: the first
; (THE, A, AN, SOME) normally, the second (THE, THE, THE, SOME) when
; #R$A022($A027) or #R$A2AA is set - so a thing is 'a lamp' or 'the lamp' as
; the sentence needs. The entry is picked by bits 4-5 of D, the high byte of
; the article code in a thing's name. Words looked up with
; source/dictionary.py. CORRECTED: the automatic split had it as code.
; .
; Seen in every room listing: 'You can see an oil lamp, your armchair and
; watsons armchair.' - AN from the first table, and THE in 'You open the plain
; door.'
w$A24D DEFW $089D    ; Normal 0: THE
 $A24F DEFW $0001    ; Normal 1: A
 $A251 DEFW $005A    ; Normal 2: AN
 $A253 DEFW $080A    ; Normal 3: SOME
 $A255 DEFW $089D    ; Definite 0: THE
 $A257 DEFW $089D    ; Definite 1: THE
 $A259 DEFW $089D    ; Definite 2: THE
 $A25B DEFW $080A    ; Definite 3: SOME

; Is thing B in the list at ($A0D8)? (only when $A011 is set)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A0D8, #R$A00A($A011).
; .
; Z when #R$A00A($A011) is 0 or there is no list; otherwise searches it
; (#R$D34E) and returns NZ if B is there. Used by #R$A27C($A27D). Read from the
; code.
;
;  Input:A The thing number
; Output:F Z when $A011 is 0, there is no list, or the thing is not in it; NZ
; .        if it is
;        IX The list at ($A0D8) when there is one
c$A25D PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack - B = thing A
 $A25E LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $A25F LD A,($A011)  ; Read #R$A00A($A011) in "Game variables" into A - Only
                     ; when #R$A00A($A011) is set...
 $A262 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A263 JR Z,$A275    ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$A275
 $A265 LD HL,($A0D8) ; Read #R$A0D8 in "The main loop: one minute of game time
                     ; per..." into HL - ...and there is a list at #R$A0D8...
 $A268 LD A,H        ; Copy H into A
 $A269 OR L          ; Set in A the bits that are set in L
 $A26A JR Z,$A275    ; If the result is zero, skip ahead to #R$A275
 $A26C PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A26D POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A26F LD A,B        ; Copy B into A
 $A270 CALL $D34E    ; Call the routine "Find a 3-byte record by id, in a
                     ; sentinel-terminated..." (#R$D34E) - ...look A up in it
                     ; (#R$D34E): CP $FF gives Z if it is not there
 $A273 CP $FF        ; Compare A with $FF
*$A275 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A276 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Describe a room's picture
;
; Called while describing the displayed room (#R$D118). #R$A25D decides whether
; there is a picture; if so the record's +1/+2 is passed to the picture
; interpreter at #R$F3C2.
; .
; The picture interpreter draws through the ROM (PIXEL-ADD at
; #LINK(rom_symbols#a22AA)($22AA)), which the harness does not have, so the
; harness skips the call to #R$F3C2 (it changes no game state - see
; harness_notes). Before that, entering the entrance hallway (room 6) ran off
; into empty memory.
;
;  Input:IX The record whose picture is wanted (+1/+2 point to the picture
; .         data)
; Output:All registers kept
c$A277 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - Save IX, HL, BC, AF
 $A279 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A27A PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack
 $A27B PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A27C CALL $A25D    ; Call the routine "Is thing B in the list at (#R$A0D8)?
                     ; (only when #R$A00A($A011)..." (#R$A25D) - Is there a
                     ; picture?
 $A27F JR Z,$A290    ; If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$A290
 $A281 CALL $A296    ; Call the routine "Print 18 newlines" (#R$A296)
 $A284 CALL $C129    ; Call the routine "Narrow the text lines to the left half
                     ; (a picture is..." (#R$C129)
 $A287 LD L,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into L - The picture
                     ; data...
 $A28A LD H,(IX+$02) ; Read byte +2 of the record at IX into H
 $A28D CALL $F3C2    ; Call the routine "Draw a picture (the picture
                     ; interpreter)" (#R$F3C2) - ...drawn by the picture
                     ; interpreter
*$A290 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A291 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A292 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A293 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A295 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Print 18 newlines
;
; Clears the text window by scrolling it: 18 newlines (#R$A2A2), clearing
; #R$A012($A019) after each. Used by #R$9E7F($9E80) (the end of the game),
; #R$A281($A282) and #R$B00E($B00F). Read from the code.
;
; Output:A, B Changed
c$A296 LD B,$12      ; 18 times...
*$A298 CALL $A2A2    ; Call the routine "Print a newline (A kept)" (#R$A2A2) -
                     ; ...start a new line (#R$A2A2) and clear #R$A012($A019)
 $A29B XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $A29C LD ($A019),A  ; Store A in #R$A012($A019) in "Game variables"
 $A29F DJNZ $A298    ; Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back to
                     ; #R$A298
 $A2A1 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Print a newline (A kept)
;
; Prints character $0D through the word-wrapping printer (#R$A9B7). Fifteen
; callers. Read from the code.
;
; Output:A Kept
c$A2A2 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack - New line: print a carriage return
                     ; (13) (#R$A9B7), keeping A
 $A2A3 LD A,$0D      ; Load A with $0D
 $A2A5 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A2A8 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A2A9 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Use THE: a flag for the article printer ($A7F2)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A7F2.
; .
; Set, the article printer at #R$A7EB takes its words from the second table at
; #R$A255 (THE, THE, THE, SOME). Set to 1 around #R$D5B7's naming. Read from
; the code.
b$A2AA DEFB $00      ; Flag

; Fill B bytes at HL with zero (or, from $A2AC, with A)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A2AC.
; .
; Read from the code.
; .
; Checked 9 November over the first 120 commands of the winning route: 1326
; runs, every one met its stated postcondition (the B bytes at HL are zero
; afterwards).
;
; Input:B how many
;       HL where
c$A2AB XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - (Next routine)
                     ; clear B bytes at HL
*$A2AC LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to
 $A2AD INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A2AE DJNZ $A2AC    ; Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back to
                     ; #R$A2AC
 $A2B0 RET           ; Return to the caller

; HL = verb A's record in the verb table
;
; HL = #R$8259 + 8 x (A - 1): the 8-byte records of the verb table, verb 1
; first (see #R$8259). Read from the code.
; .
; Checked 9 November over the first 120 commands of the winning route: 2033
; runs, every one met its stated postcondition (HL = #R$8259 + 8 x (A - 1)).
;
;  Input:A verb number
; Output:HL its record
c$A2B1 DEC A         ; Subtract one from A - Verb number A (1-based) times 8
                     ; into HL...
 $A2B2 LD L,A        ; Copy A into L
 $A2B3 LD H,$00      ; Load H with $00
 $A2B5 ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $A2B6 ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $A2B7 ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $A2B8 LD DE,$8259   ; Load DE with #R$8259 (-32167 as a signed offset), the
                     ; amount that is added next - ...added to the verb table
                     ; #R$8259: HL = its record
 $A2BB ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A2BC RET           ; Return to the caller

; Gather a verb record's flag nibbles into $A01D and $A01E
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A01A($A01D), #R$A01A($A01E).
; .
; IX is a verb record (#R$8259): #R$A01A($A01D) = the high nibbles of its first
; two word slots (+1, +3), #R$A01A($A01E) those of the last two (+5, +7) - the
; verb's grammar flags, described at #R$8259 (direct object, indirect object,
; preposition before the object...). Read from the code.
;
;  Input:IX A verb record
; Output:$A01D The high nibbles of +1 and +3
;        $A01E The high nibbles of +5 and +7
c$A2BD LD A,(IX+$05) ; Read byte +5 of the record at IX into A - IX = a verb
                     ; record. +5's high nibble, brought down...
 $A2C0 RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A2C1 RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A2C2 RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A2C3 RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A2C4 AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
 $A2C6 LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $A2C7 LD A,(IX+$07) ; Read byte +7 of the record at IX into A - ...plus +7's
                     ; high nibble (above it) = #R$A01A($A01E)
 $A2CA AND $F0       ; Keep only bits 4, 5, 6, 7 of A (mask $F0)
 $A2CC ADD A,C       ; Add C to A
 $A2CD LD ($A01E),A  ; Store A in #R$A01A($A01E) in "Game variables"
 $A2D0 LD A,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into A - +1's high
                     ; nibble, brought down...
 $A2D3 RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A2D4 RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A2D5 RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A2D6 RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A2D7 AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
 $A2D9 LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $A2DA LD A,(IX+$03) ; Read byte +3 of the record at IX into A - ...plus +3's
                     ; high nibble (above it) = #R$A01A($A01D)
 $A2DD AND $F0       ; Keep only bits 4, 5, 6, 7 of A (mask $F0)
 $A2DF ADD A,C       ; Add C to A
 $A2E0 LD ($A01D),A  ; Store A in #R$A01A($A01D) in "Game variables"
 $A2E3 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Narrate a command as a sentence ('You take the oil lamp.')
;
; Saves the verb and objects (#R$A35D-#R$A35F), then - unless it is too dark
; and the player is the one acting, when it prints 'SORRY IT IS TOO DARK.'
; (#R$68D1) - prints the actor (#R$A724), the verb's words from its pattern
; (#R$A2B1/#R$A2BD) and the objects, ending the line (#R$A2A2). This is the
; sentence every successful action shows, for the player and for characters in
; sight ('Inspector Lestrade hails the hansom cab.'). Read from the code; the
; output it describes is what the emulator shows.
; .
; .
; .
; Line comments added from reading the code. What #R$A00A($A00C) means (the B
; of this routine) was not established, so the two narration paths are
; described by the branch, not by who is acting.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Set #R$A2AA to 1 while narrating, and keep the verb and both objects at
; #R$A35D to #R$A35F.
; .
; 2. Work out whether the text is printed as quoted (#R$A022($A027) is 1 when
; #R$A00A($A00C) is 0).
; .
; 3. If it is too dark to see (#R$D078) and the actor is the player, print
; 'SORRY IT IS TOO DARK.' and finish.
; .
; 4. Find the verb's record and gather its flags (#R$A2BD). If bit 4 of
; #R$A01A($A01D) is set the verb is never narrated.
; .
; 5. Start a fresh line if the actor is a character other than the player,
; Watson or Lestrade's usual set (#R$DC6C), and print the actor's name
; (#R$A724).
; .
; 6. If the actor was the player print the word YOU and narrate the command
; (#R$A360); otherwise narrate it as it stands (#R$A37D). End the line, clear
; #R$A2AA and #R$A022($A027).
;
;  Input:$A033 The verb
;        $A034 The first object, $ A035 the second
;        $A036 The actor
; Output:All registers kept
c$A2E4 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - #R$A2AA = 1 while narrating
 $A2E6 LD ($A2AA),A  ; Store A in "Use THE: a flag for the article printer
                     ; (#R$A7F2)" (#R$A2AA)
 $A2E9 PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack - Save the registers
 $A2EB PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack
 $A2EC PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A2EE PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A2EF PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A2F0 LD A,($A033)  ; Read #R$A032($A033) in "Game variables" into A - Save
                     ; the verb and the two objects at #R$A35D-#R$A35F
 $A2F3 LD ($A35D),A  ; Store A in "A remembered command: verb, direct
                     ; object,..." (#R$A35D)
 $A2F6 LD HL,($A034) ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into HL
 $A2F9 LD ($A35E),HL ; Store HL in #R$A35E in "A remembered command: verb,
                     ; direct object,..."
 $A2FC LD A,($A00C)  ; Read #R$A00A($A00C) in "Game variables" into A - B =
                     ; #R$A00A($A00C) (what it means was not established;
                     ; #R$CB43 clears it before narrating)
 $A2FF LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $A300 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A301 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - #R$A022($A027) (quoted printing) = 1
                     ; if B is zero, else 0 (so the text is quoted when B is
                     ; zero)
 $A303 JR Z,$A306    ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$A306
 $A305 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
*$A306 LD ($A027),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A027) in "Game variables"
 $A309 CALL $D078    ; Is it too dark to see (#R$D078)?
 $A30C JR NC,$A31A   ; No: carry on
 $A30E LD A,B        ; Copy B into A - Dark, but B non-zero: carry on
                     ; regardless
 $A30F AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A310 JR NZ,$A31A   ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$A31A
 $A312 LD HL,$68D1   ; Point HL at the message "SORRY IT IS TOO DARK."
                     ; (#R$68D1) - Dark: 'SORRY IT IS TOO DARK.' (#R$68D1)
 $A315 CALL $A59F    ; Call the routine "Print the message at HL" (#R$A59F)
 $A318 JR $A34E      ; Jump on to #R$A34E
*$A31A LD A,($A35D)  ; Read "A remembered command: verb, direct object,..."
                     ; (#R$A35D) into A - The verb's pattern record (#R$A2B1)
                     ; into IX
 $A31D CALL $A2B1    ; Call the routine "HL = verb A's record in the verb
                     ; table" (#R$A2B1)
 $A320 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A321 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A323 CALL $A2BD    ; Set the verb flags #R$A01A($A01D)/#R$A01A($A01E)
                     ; (#R$A2BD)
 $A326 LD A,($A01D)  ; Read #R$A01A($A01D) in "Game variables" into A - Verb
                     ; flag bit 4: no narration
 $A329 AND $10       ; Isolate bit 4 of A (mask $10)
 $A32B JR NZ,$A34E   ; Set: skip to the end
 $A32D CP B          ; Compare A with B - B zero?
 $A32E JR Z,$A336    ; If A equals B, skip ahead to #R$A336
 $A330 CALL $DC6C    ; An actor other than 0, 1 or 100?
 $A333 CALL Z,$A2A2  ; Then start a fresh line (#R$A2A2)
*$A336 CALL $A724    ; Print the actor's name (#R$A724)
 $A339 LD DE,$017F   ; Load DE with $017F - DE = $017F
 $A33C XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $A33D CP B          ; Compare A with B - B zero: print the word at DE ($017F)
                     ; (#R$A887), then narrate with the actor set to 0 = YOU
                     ; (#R$A360)
 $A33E JR NZ,$A348   ; If A differs from B, skip ahead to #R$A348
 $A340 CALL $A887    ; Call #R$A887 (inside "Print the dictionary word that HL
                     ; refers to")
 $A343 CALL $A360    ; Call the routine "Code $18: narrate the remembered
                     ; command" (#R$A360)
 $A346 JR $A34B      ; Skip ahead to #R$A34B
*$A348 CALL $A37D    ; B non-zero: narrate as it stands (#R$A37D)
*$A34B CALL $A2A2    ; End the line (#R$A2A2)
*$A34E XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Clear #R$A2AA and
                     ; #R$A022($A027)
 $A34F LD ($A2AA),A  ; Store A in "Use THE: a flag for the article printer
                     ; (#R$A7F2)" (#R$A2AA)
 $A352 LD ($A027),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A027) in "Game variables"
 $A355 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack - Restore the registers and
                     ; return
 $A356 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A357 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A359 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A35A POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A35C RET           ; Return to the caller

; A remembered command: verb, direct object, indirect object
;
; Copied from #R$A032($A033)-#R$A032($A035) by the narrator (#R$A2F3($A2F4),
; #R$A2F9($A2FA)) and written by the plan interpreter
; (#R$CDD0($CDD1)-#R$CDE2($CDE3)); read back when a command is narrated
; (#R$A31A($A31B), #R$A381($A382), #R$A3DB($A3DC)-#R$A40E($A40F)).
b$A35D DEFB $00      ; Verb
 $A35E DEFB $00      ; Direct object
 $A35F DEFB $00      ; Indirect object

; Code $18: narrate the remembered command
;
; Narrates the command at #R$A35D (#R$A37D) with the actor (#R$A032($A036)) set
; to 0 for the duration; if the actor was that command's direct object, the
; direct object is set to 0 as well. 0 is the player, YOU. Read from the code;
; where code $18 is used was not looked up.
;
; Output:F Z
;        IX Changed
c$A360 LD A,($A036)  ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A - Keep
                     ; the current actor; compare it with the remembered one
                     ; (#R$A35E)
 $A363 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A364 LD HL,$A35E   ; Point HL at #R$A35E in "A remembered command: verb,
                     ; direct object,..."
 $A367 CP (HL)       ; Compare A with the byte at #R$A35E in "A remembered
                     ; command: verb, direct object,..."
 $A368 LD A,$00      ; Load A with $00 - A = 0, the player: not the remembered
                     ; actor - narrate as the player
 $A36A JR NZ,$A36D   ; If A differs from the byte at #R$A35E in "A remembered
                     ; command: verb, direct object,...", skip ahead to #R$A36D
 $A36C LD (HL),A     ; The same: forget the remembered actor (#R$A35E = 0)
*$A36D LD ($A036),A  ; Store A in #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" - Either
                     ; way the actor is 0 while the remembered command is
                     ; narrated (#R$A37D)
 $A370 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A372 CALL $A37D    ; Call the routine "Print the rest of a narrated command:
                     ; verb words,..." (#R$A37D)
 $A375 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A377 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack - Restore the actor; return Z
 $A378 LD ($A036),A  ; Store A in #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables"
 $A37B XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A37C RET           ; Return to the caller

; Print the rest of a narrated command: verb words, objects, numbers
;
; Part of #R$A2E4. From the verb pattern (#R$A2B1/#R$A2BD) and the parse flags
; (#R$A01A($A01D)/#R$A01A($A01E)) prints the verb's words (#R$A880), then the
; direct object - as a number or money (#R$A419) when it is one ($FE), as ME
; (word $055B, looked up with source/dictionary.py; CORRECTED, was 'yourself')
; when it is the actor, or by name (#R$A745) - then any preposition and the
; indirect object the same way, and a full stop. Read from the code; 'You pay
; 2/6 to Watson.' in the emulator is one of its sentences.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Look up the remembered command's verb record and flags (#R$A2B1, #R$A2BD,
; #R$C493, #R$C4B2).
; .
; 2. Print the verb's words: the pair at +6 of the record, then the pair at +0
; (#R$A880).
; .
; 3. If the verb takes a direct object (flags bit 3): print any word that goes
; before it, then the object itself - a number or sum of money (#R$A419) if it
; is one, ME if it is the actor, or its name (#R$A745).
; .
; 4. If there is an indirect object and the flags allow one: print its
; preposition, then the object the same three ways.
; .
; 5. End with a full stop. The result is a sentence such as 'You pay 2/6 to
; Watson.'
;
; Input:$A35D The remembered command: verb, direct object, indirect object
c$A37D XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - No article flag;
                     ; the remembered command's verb...
 $A37E LD ($A028),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A028) in "Game variables"
 $A381 LD A,($A35D)  ; Read "A remembered command: verb, direct object,..."
                     ; (#R$A35D) into A
 $A384 CALL $A2B1    ; Call the routine "HL = verb A's record in the verb
                     ; table" (#R$A2B1) - ...gives its record in the verb table
                     ; (#R$A2B1) and its flags (#R$A2BD,
                     ; #R$A01A($A01D)/#R$A01A($A01E))
 $A387 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A388 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A38A PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A38B CALL $A2BD    ; Call the routine "Gather a verb record's flag nibbles
                     ; into #R$A01A($A01D) and..." (#R$A2BD)
 $A38E CALL $C493    ; Call the routine "Set the direct object's requirement
                     ; from the parsed..." (#R$C493) - (#R$C493, #R$C4B2: also
                     ; from the record)
 $A391 CALL $C4B2    ; Call the routine "Set the indirect object's requirement
                     ; from the parsed..." (#R$C4B2)
 $A394 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A395 LD A,($A01D)  ; Read #R$A01A($A01D) in "Game variables" into A
 $A398 LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $A399 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - Print the verb's words: the pair
                     ; at +6, then the pair at +0 (#R$A880)
 $A39A LD DE,$0006   ; Load DE with $0006, the amount that is added next
 $A39D ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A39E CALL $A880    ; Call the routine "Print the dictionary word that HL
                     ; refers to" (#R$A880)
 $A3A1 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A3A2 CALL $A880    ; Call the routine "Print the dictionary word that HL
                     ; refers to" (#R$A880)
 $A3A5 BIT 3,C       ; Test bit 3 of C - Flags bit 3: the verb takes a direct
                     ; object
 $A3A7 JR Z,$A3E2    ; If bit 3 of C is clear, jump on to #R$A3E2
 $A3A9 BIT 5,C       ; Test bit 5 of C - A word before it (bit 5), and one more
                     ; if #R$A01A($A01E) bit 7 is set
 $A3AB CALL NZ,$A880 ; If bit 5 of C is set, call the routine "Print the
                     ; dictionary word that HL refers to" (#R$A880)
 $A3AE LD A,($A01E)  ; Read #R$A01A($A01E) in "Game variables" into A
 $A3B1 BIT 7,A       ; Test bit 7 of A
 $A3B3 CALL NZ,$A880 ; If bit 7 of A is set, call the routine "Print the
                     ; dictionary word that HL refers to" (#R$A880)
 $A3B6 LD A,($A35E)  ; Read #R$A35E in "A remembered command: verb, direct
                     ; object,..." into A - A number or a sum of money ($FE):
                     ; print it (#R$A419)
 $A3B9 CP $FE        ; Compare A with $FE
 $A3BB JR NZ,$A3C7   ; If A differs from $FE, skip ahead to #R$A3C7
 $A3BD PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A3BE LD HL,($A02F) ; Read #R$A02A($A02F) in "Game variables" into HL
 $A3C1 CALL $A419    ; Call the routine "Print an amount of money" (#R$A419)
 $A3C4 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A3C5 JR $A3E2      ; Skip ahead to #R$A3E2
*$A3C7 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - The actor itself: ME ($055B)
 $A3C8 LD HL,$A036   ; Point HL at #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables"
 $A3CB CP (HL)       ; Compare A with the byte at #R$A032($A036) in "Game
                     ; variables"
 $A3CC JR NZ,$A3D6   ; If A differs from the byte at #R$A032($A036) in "Game
                     ; variables", skip ahead to #R$A3D6
 $A3CE LD DE,$055B   ; Load DE with $055B
 $A3D1 CALL $A887    ; Call #R$A887 (inside "Print the dictionary word that HL
                     ; refers to")
 $A3D4 JR $A3E1      ; Skip ahead to #R$A3E1
*$A3D6 LD A,($A00F)  ; Read #R$A00A($A00F) in "Game variables" into A -
                     ; Otherwise its name: a room for requirement codes below
                     ; 5, else a thing (#R$A745)
 $A3D9 CP $05        ; Compare A with $05
 $A3DB LD A,($A35E)  ; Read #R$A35E in "A remembered command: verb, direct
                     ; object,..." into A
 $A3DE CALL $A745    ; Call the routine "Name room or thing A" (#R$A745)
*$A3E1 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
*$A3E2 LD A,($A35F)  ; Read #R$A35F in "A remembered command: verb, direct
                     ; object,..." into A - An indirect object, if there is one
                     ; and the flags (bit 2) allow it
 $A3E5 CP $FF        ; Compare A with $FF
 $A3E7 JR Z,$A414    ; If A equals $FF, jump on to #R$A414
 $A3E9 BIT 2,C       ; Test bit 2 of C
 $A3EB JR Z,$A414    ; If bit 2 of C is clear, jump on to #R$A414
 $A3ED BIT 5,C       ; Test bit 5 of C - Its preposition: the words not printed
                     ; before
 $A3EF CALL Z,$A880  ; If bit 5 of C is clear, call the routine "Print the
                     ; dictionary word that HL refers to" (#R$A880)
 $A3F2 LD A,($A01E)  ; Read #R$A01A($A01E) in "Game variables" into A
 $A3F5 BIT 7,A       ; Test bit 7 of A
 $A3F7 CALL Z,$A880  ; If bit 7 of A is clear, call the routine "Print the
                     ; dictionary word that HL refers to" (#R$A880)
 $A3FA LD A,($A35F)  ; Read #R$A35F in "A remembered command: verb, direct
                     ; object,..." into A - A number or money (#R$A419), or a
                     ; name (#R$A745)
 $A3FD CP $FE        ; Compare A with $FE
 $A3FF JR NZ,$A409   ; If A differs from $FE, skip ahead to #R$A409
 $A401 LD HL,($A031) ; Read #R$A02A($A031) in "Game variables" into HL
 $A404 CALL $A419    ; Call the routine "Print an amount of money" (#R$A419)
 $A407 JR $A414      ; Skip ahead to #R$A414
*$A409 LD A,($A010)  ; Read #R$A00A($A010) in "Game variables" into A
 $A40C CP $05        ; Compare A with $05
 $A40E LD A,($A35F)  ; Read #R$A35F in "A remembered command: verb, direct
                     ; object,..." into A
 $A411 CALL $A745    ; Call the routine "Name room or thing A" (#R$A745)
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$BB67, #R$D113, #R$D526,
; #R$D60B and #R$D68C.
*$A414 LD A,$2E      ; Load A with $2E - And a full stop
 $A416 JP $A9B7      ; Jump on to the routine "Print character A, a word at a
                     ; time (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)

; Print an amount of money
;
; HL = a sum in the packed form the parser makes (#R$DB67). Prints it the old
; way: '£n' if there are pounds, then shillings and pence as 's/d', 's/-' or
; 'dD'. Divides with #R$A476 and prints digits with #R$A490. Confirmed in the
; emulator through PAY: 30 pence prints as '2/6', 36 as '3/-', 240 as '£1'.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Take away the marker bit and convert the packed sum to pence, then print a
; space.
; .
; 2. Pounds = pence / 240. If there are any, print the pound sign and the
; number. Stop if nothing is left; otherwise print a space.
; .
; 3. Shillings = what is left / 12. If there are none, print the pence and 'D.'
; (as in '6D.').
; .
; 4. Otherwise print the shillings, a stroke, and the pence, or '-' for none,
; as in '2/6' or '1/-'.
;
;  Input:HL A sum of money in the parser's packed form
; Output:The sum printed as pounds, shillings and pence
c$A419 PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack
 $A41A RES 7,H       ; Clear bit 7 of H - Unpack the sum: HL = total pence
                     ; (seven bits a byte, marker bit dropped)
 $A41C SLA L         ; Shift L left (double it)
 $A41E SRL H         ; Shift H right (halve it)
 $A420 RR L          ; Rotate L right through the carry
 $A422 LD A,$20      ; Load A with $20 - A space
 $A424 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A427 LD DE,$00F0   ; Load DE with $00F0 - Pounds = pence / 240 (#R$A476)
 $A42A CALL $A476    ; Call the routine "Divide HL by DE: A = quotient, HL =
                     ; remainder" (#R$A476)
 $A42D AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A42E JR Z,$A442    ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$A442
 $A430 LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $A431 LD A,$60      ; Load A with $60 - Any: the pound sign (character $60 in
                     ; the Spectrum's set) and the pounds
 $A433 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A436 CALL $A490    ; Call the routine "Print the number B (0-255) in decimal"
                     ; (#R$A490)
 $A439 LD A,L        ; Copy L into A - Nothing left: done
 $A43A OR H          ; Set in A the bits that are set in H
 $A43B JR Z,$A467    ; If the result is zero, jump on to #R$A467
 $A43D LD A,$20      ; Load A with $20
 $A43F CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
*$A442 LD DE,$000C   ; Load DE with $000C - Shillings = what is left / 12
 $A445 CALL $A476    ; Call the routine "Divide HL by DE: A = quotient, HL =
                     ; remainder" (#R$A476)
 $A448 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A449 JR Z,$A469    ; None: just the pence and 'D.' (#R$A469), as in '6D.'
 $A44B LD B,A        ; Copy A into B - The shillings, a stroke, and the pence -
                     ; or '-' for none: '2/6', '1/-'
 $A44C CALL $A490    ; Call the routine "Print the number B (0-255) in decimal"
                     ; (#R$A490)
 $A44F LD A,$2F      ; Load A with $2F
 $A451 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A454 LD A,L        ; Copy L into A
 $A455 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A456 LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $A457 JR Z,$A45E    ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$A45E
 $A459 CALL $A490    ; Call the routine "Print the number B (0-255) in decimal"
                     ; (#R$A490)
 $A45C JR $A467      ; Skip ahead to #R$A467
*$A45E LD A,$2D      ; Load A with $2D
*$A460 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A463 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - (#R$A022($A026) =
                     ; 0)
 $A464 LD ($A026),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A026) in "Game variables"
*$A467 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A468 RET           ; Return to the caller
*$A469 LD B,L        ; Copy L into B
 $A46A CALL $A490    ; Call the routine "Print the number B (0-255) in decimal"
                     ; (#R$A490)
 $A46D LD A,$44      ; Load A with $44
 $A46F CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A472 LD A,$2E      ; Load A with $2E
 $A474 JR $A460      ; Go back to #R$A460

; Divide HL by DE: A = quotient, HL = remainder
;
; Used by the routines at #R$A419 and #R$A480.
;
;  Input:HL The number to divide
;        DE The divisor
; Output:A The quotient
;        HL The remainder
c$A476 LD A,$FF      ; Load A with $FF - Divide HL by DE by repeated
                     ; subtraction: A counts from -1 (so A = the quotient)
*$A478 INC A         ; Add one to A
 $A479 AND A         ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that
                     ; follows is a plain one (a compare)
 $A47A SBC HL,DE     ; Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller,
                     ; zero = equal)
 $A47C JR NC,$A478   ; If it did not go below zero, go back to #R$A478
 $A47E ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL - Add the divisor back: HL = the remainder
 $A47F RET           ; Return to the caller

; Print one digit of HL / DE, skipping leading zeros (C)
;
; Used by the routine at #R$A490.
;
;  Input:HL The number
;        DE The place value (100 or 10)
;        C 0 while still skipping leading zeros
; Output:HL The remainder
;        C Non-zero once a digit has been printed
c$A480 CALL $A476    ; One digit of the number (#R$A476)
 $A483 ADD A,$30     ; Make it an ASCII digit
 $A485 DEC C         ; Subtract one from C - C = the leading-zero flag: still
                     ; zero?
 $A486 INC C         ; Add one to C
 $A487 JR NZ,$A48D   ; If C is not zero, skip ahead to #R$A48D
 $A489 CP $30        ; Compare A with $30 - A '0' while leading: print nothing
 $A48B RET Z         ; If A equals $30, return
 $A48C INC C         ; Otherwise from now on print zeros too (C = 1)
*$A48D JP $A9B7      ; Print the digit (#R$A9B7)

; Print the number B (0-255) in decimal
;
; Used by the routine at #R$A419.
;
;  Input:B The number, 0 to 255
; Output:The number printed in decimal; HL, DE kept
c$A490 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - HL = the number in B (0-255)
 $A491 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A492 LD L,B        ; Copy B into L
 $A493 LD H,$00      ; Load H with $00
 $A495 LD C,H        ; Copy H into C
 $A496 LD DE,$0064   ; Load DE with $0064 - The hundreds digit (#R$A480, with a
                     ; leading zero suppressed)
 $A499 CALL $A480    ; Call the routine "Print one digit of HL / DE, skipping
                     ; leading zeros (C)" (#R$A480)
 $A49C LD DE,$000A   ; Load DE with $000A - The tens digit
 $A49F CALL $A480    ; Call the routine "Print one digit of HL / DE, skipping
                     ; leading zeros (C)" (#R$A480)
 $A4A2 LD A,L        ; Copy L into A - The units digit, always printed
 $A4A3 ADD A,$30     ; Add $30 to A
 $A4A5 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A4A8 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A4A9 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A4AA RET           ; Return to the caller

; HL = room A's record + 1
;
; Used by the routine at #R$A745.
;
;  Input:A The room number
; Output:HL The room's record + 1
c$A4AB PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack - HL = room A's record + 1
                     ; (#R$D224)
 $A4AC PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A4AE CALL $D224    ; Call the routine "Find a room record" (#R$D224)
 $A4B1 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A4B3 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A4B4 LD DE,$0001   ; Load DE with $0001, the amount that is added next
 $A4B7 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A4B8 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A4BA POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A4BB RET           ; Return to the caller

; HL = thing A's record + 7
;
; Used by the routines at #R$A745, #R$BA67, #R$BF1C and #R$CBC2.
;
;  Input:A The thing number
; Output:HL The thing's record + 7, the start of its name words
c$A4BC PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack - HL = thing A's record + 7, the
                     ; start of its name words (#R$D237)
 $A4BD PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A4BF CALL $D237    ; Call the routine "Resolve an id (in A) to its full
                     ; entity record" (#R$D237)
 $A4C2 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A4C4 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A4C5 LD DE,$0007   ; Load DE with $0007, the amount that is added next
 $A4C8 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A4C9 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A4CB POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A4CC RET           ; Return to the caller

; Do the pattern words at IY name the words at HL? ($A4CD, $A4E0)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A4E0.
; .
; #R$A4E0: the first pair must match (#R$A51B; an empty pattern word matches
; anything), then the next two pairs are tried both ways round, so two
; adjectives can be typed in either order; A = 0 for a match in the order given
; and 1 for a match with the second and third pairs swapped (the verb lookup's
; #R$B405 then swaps #R$B185($B188) and #R$B185($B18A) to suit); #R$A02A($A02E)
; is set to 1 as soon as the first pair matches. NZ means no match. #R$A4CD
; first requires the fourth pair (six bytes on) to match, and is used by the
; verb lookup (#R$B3EB($B3EC)); #R$A4E0 by the thing and room searches
; (#R$D37A($D37B), #R$D3B7($D3B8)) and the scenery check (#R$B763($B764)). Read
; from the code.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. A4CD tries the fourth pair of the pattern first, six bytes on; if it
; fails, the whole match fails.
; .
; 2. A4E0 then needs the first pair to match (#R$A51B; an empty pattern word
; matches anything) and notes that in #R$A02A($A02E).
; .
; 3. The second and third pairs are tried as they stand (A = 0).
; .
; 4. If that fails they are tried swapped, the pattern's third against the
; command's second and its second against their third (A = 1).
;
;  Input:HL The words of a command (four pairs)
;        IY The pattern words
; Output:F Z when the pattern names the words
;        A 0 when the second and third pairs matched as they stand; 1 when they
; .        matched swapped
;        Registers HL, IY, DE kept
c$A4CD PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A4CE PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A4CF PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack
 $A4D1 LD DE,$0006   ; Load DE with $0006, the amount that is added next - The
                     ; fourth pair first (six bytes on)
 $A4D4 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A4D5 ADD IY,DE     ; Add DE to IY
 $A4D7 CALL $A51B    ; Call the routine "Does the word reference at HL match
                     ; the one at IY? (Z)" (#R$A51B)
 $A4DA JR NZ,$A516   ; If the zero flag is clear, jump on to #R$A516 - (no
                     ; match: NZ)
 $A4DC POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A4DE POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A4DF POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$B6FD, #R$D36A and #R$D39B.
*$A4E0 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A4E1 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A4E2 PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack
 $A4E4 CALL $A51B    ; Call the routine "Does the word reference at HL match
                     ; the one at IY? (Z)" (#R$A51B) - #R$A4E0: the first pair
                     ; must match
 $A4E7 JR NZ,$A516   ; If the zero flag is clear, jump on to #R$A516
 $A4E9 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - (#R$A02A($A02E) = 1: the first word
                     ; matched)
 $A4EB LD ($A02E),A  ; Store A in #R$A02A($A02E) in "Game variables"
 $A4EE CALL $A51B    ; Call the routine "Does the word reference at HL match
                     ; the one at IY? (Z)" (#R$A51B) - Second and third pairs
                     ; as they are: A = 0
 $A4F1 JR NZ,$A4FA   ; If the zero flag is clear, skip ahead to #R$A4FA
 $A4F3 CALL $A51B    ; Call the routine "Does the word reference at HL match
                     ; the one at IY? (Z)" (#R$A51B)
 $A4F6 LD A,$00      ; Load A with $00
 $A4F8 JR Z,$A516    ; If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$A516
*$A4FA POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack - Or swapped: the pattern's
                     ; third against the words' second...
 $A4FC POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A4FD PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A4FE PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack
 $A500 LD DE,$0004   ; Load DE with $0004, the amount that is added next
 $A503 ADD IY,DE     ; Add DE to IY
 $A505 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A506 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A507 CALL $A51B    ; Call the routine "Does the word reference at HL match
                     ; the one at IY? (Z)" (#R$A51B)
 $A50A JR NZ,$A516   ; If the zero flag is clear, skip ahead to #R$A516
 $A50C LD DE,$FFFC   ; Load DE with #R$FFF7($FFFC) (-4 as a signed offset), the
                     ; amount that is added next - ...and its second against
                     ; their third: A = 1
 $A50F ADD IY,DE     ; Add DE to IY
 $A511 CALL $A51B    ; Call the routine "Does the word reference at HL match
                     ; the one at IY? (Z)" (#R$A51B)
 $A514 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01
*$A516 POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A518 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A519 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A51A RET           ; Return to the caller

; Does the word reference at HL match the one at IY? (Z)
;
; An empty one (0) at HL matches anything. Otherwise compares the low byte and
; the low nibble of the high byte. HL and IY both move on two bytes. Read from
; the code.
;
;  Input:HL A word reference of the command (0 matches anything)
;        IY A word reference of the pattern
; Output:F Z when they match
;        HL, IY Both moved on two bytes
c$A51B PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - A zero word at HL ends the
                     ; comparison (Z unset)
 $A51C LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $A51D INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A51E OR (HL)       ; Set in A the bits that are set in the byte HL points at
 $A51F JR Z,$A52E    ; If the result is zero, skip ahead to #R$A52E
 $A521 LD A,(IY+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IY into A - The low
                     ; nibbles of the two high bytes must agree...
 $A524 XOR (HL)      ; Flip in A the bits that are set in the byte HL points at
 $A525 AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
 $A527 JR NZ,$A52E   ; If any of bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A was set, skip ahead to
                     ; #R$A52E
 $A529 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte - ...and the low bytes: a 12-bit
                     ; match
 $A52A LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $A52B CP (IY+$00)   ; Compare A with the first byte of the record at IY
*$A52E POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack - Either way both pointers
                     ; move on two bytes
 $A52F INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A530 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A531 INC IY        ; Step IY on to the next byte
 $A533 INC IY        ; Step IY on to the next byte
 $A535 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Wait for a key, but not for ever
;
; Polls the keyboard (#R$C41F) up to #R$A03A($A03B) times (reset to 3,000 each
; call), with a 256-step delay loop between polls. A key: returns it with bit 7
; cleared. No key in that time: rubs out whatever has been typed on the line
; (#R$D9B7) and returns 0 with Z set. Read from the code; the real-time length
; of the limit was not measured (the harness answers key reads itself), but the
; give-up path was seen working - see #R$D898.
;
; Output:A The key pressed (0 if none)
;        F Z when nothing was typed in time
c$A536 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A537 LD HL,($A03B) ; How many polls before giving up
*$A53A CALL $C41F    ; A key?
 $A53D BIT 7,A       ; Test bit 7 of A - Yes
 $A53F JR NZ,$A553   ; If bit 7 of A is set, skip ahead to #R$A553
 $A541 LD A,$00      ; Load A with $00 - No: a short delay...
*$A543 DEC A         ; Subtract one from A
 $A544 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A545 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A546 JR NZ,$A543   ; If the zero flag is clear, go back to #R$A543
 $A548 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte - ...and count a poll
 $A549 LD A,H        ; Copy H into A
 $A54A OR L          ; Set in A the bits that are set in L
 $A54B JR NZ,$A53A   ; If the result is not zero, go back to #R$A53A - Try
                     ; again
 $A54D POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A54E PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A54F CALL $D9B7    ; Time is up: rub out the line
 $A552 XOR A         ; Z: nothing typed
*$A553 AND $7F       ; The key
 $A555 LD HL,$0BB8   ; Load HL with $0BB8 - Reset the limit for next time
 $A558 LD ($A03B),HL ; Store HL in #R$A03A($A03B) in "Game variables"
 $A55B POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A55C RET           ; Return to the caller

; Routines for the message control codes $00-$1F
;
; One address per control code, used by #R$A621. Codes below $1D are called and
; the interpreter carries on; $1D-$1F are jumped to instead (#R$A62F).
; CORRECTED: the automatic split had all but the first entry as code.
; .
; What the handlers do, from reading them (2026-11-08; codes not listed were
; not worked out): $00 print the thing whose address is the next parameter; $01
; print the next parameter as a word; $05 print the case statement at the next
; parameter; $06 name the current actor; $07 name the direct object; $08 print
; the character in A; $09 name the indirect object; $0A pick one of the next n
; elements at random; $0B print a message at an offset from here; $0C YOUR for
; the player, HIS for anyone else (the actor); $0D print the character in A;
; $0E YOUR for the player, HIS for anyone else (the direct object); $0F only in
; part of the day 5; $10 name the actor with the verb form (ARE / IS); $11 name
; the direct object with the verb form; $13 name the person in the next
; parameter with the verb form; $14 only in part of the day 4; $15 only in part
; of the day 3; $16 only in part of the day 2; $17 only in part of the day 1;
; $18 narrate the remembered command; $19 print the message at the next
; parameter; $1D end the message, no full stop, with a newline; $1E end the
; message with a full stop and a newline; $1F does nothing.
w$A55D DEFW $A65D    ; Code $00: #R$A65D
 $A55F DEFW $A66C    ; Code $01: #R$A66C
 $A561 DEFW $A679    ; Code $02: #R$A679
 $A563 DEFW $A702    ; Code $03: #R$A702
 $A565 DEFW $A709    ; Code $04: #R$A709
 $A567 DEFW $A711    ; Code $05: #R$A711
 $A569 DEFW $A720    ; Code $06: #R$A720
 $A56B DEFW $A72C    ; Code $07: #R$A72C
 $A56D DEFW $A736    ; Code $08: #R$A736
 $A56F DEFW $A73B    ; Code $09: #R$A73B
 $A571 DEFW $A6D3    ; Code $0A: #R$A6D3
 $A573 DEFW $A75B    ; Code $0B: #R$A75B
 $A575 DEFW $A780    ; Code $0C: #R$A780
 $A577 DEFW $A63A    ; Code $0D: #R$A63A
 $A579 DEFW $A78E    ; Code $0E: #R$A78E
 $A57B DEFW $A68C    ; Code $0F: #R$A68C
 $A57D DEFW $A793    ; Code $10: #R$A793
 $A57F DEFW $A7AC    ; Code $11: #R$A7AC
 $A581 DEFW $A65C    ; Code $12: #R$A65C
 $A583 DEFW $A7B4    ; Code $13: #R$A7B4
 $A585 DEFW $A694    ; Code $14: #R$A694
 $A587 DEFW $A69C    ; Code $15: #R$A69C
 $A589 DEFW $A6A4    ; Code $16: #R$A6A4
 $A58B DEFW $A6AC    ; Code $17: #R$A6AC
 $A58D DEFW $A360    ; Code $18: #R$A360
 $A58F DEFW $A771    ; Code $19: #R$A771
 $A591 DEFW $A684    ; Code $1A: #R$A684
 $A593 DEFW $A852    ; Code $1B: #R$A852
 $A595 DEFW $A682    ; Code $1C: #R$A682
 $A597 DEFW $A63F    ; Code $1D: #R$A63F
 $A599 DEFW $A643    ; Code $1E: #R$A643
 $A59B DEFW $A65A    ; Code $1F: #R$A65A

; Where the message's stack parameters end
;
; Set by #R$A5A9 to the stack pointer as it was before the caller pushed the
; message's parameters; #R$A5BF reloads it on the way out, so the printer
; itself throws the parameters away.
w$A59D DEFW $0000    ; Stack pointer to return with

; Print the message at HL
;
; The message interpreter's entry point; almost every word the game prints
; comes through here (see the message area at #R$67B7 for the bytecode).
; Parameters - extra messages or words, such as what a character says - are
; pushed on the stack before the call and control codes pick them up; on the
; way out the stack pointer is reset past them (#R$A59D), so callers push and
; never pop. Saves the registers, runs the byte loop (#R$A5C4 / #R$A5CD) and
; returns through JP (HL). Read from the code; the rendering of 118 messages in
; the emulator relies on it.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Save the registers and take the address of the caller's parameters (stack
; pointer plus 12) for #R$A59D.
; .
; 2. Clear #R$A00A($A00C) unless #R$A00A($A00B) is set.
; .
; 3. Run the interpreter over the message (#R$A5C4).
; .
; 4. Restore the registers, drop the parameters from the stack and return.
;
;  Input:HL The message
;        (SP+) Any parameters the caller pushed, dropped on return
; Output:All registers kept
c$A59F PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack - Print message HL: save the
                     ; registers
 $A5A0 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A5A1 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A5A3 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A5A4 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A5A5 LD HL,$000C   ; Load HL with $000C - HL = SP + 12: the caller's
                     ; parameters
 $A5A8 ADD HL,SP     ; Add SP to HL
 $A5A9 LD ($A59D),HL ; Save the message pointer in (#R$A59D)
 $A5AC POP HL        ; Restore HL
 $A5AD LD A,($A00B)  ; Read #R$A00A($A00B) in "Game variables" into A -
                     ; #R$A00A($A00B) set? Then skip the next store
 $A5B0 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A5B1 JR NZ,$A5B6   ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$A5B6
 $A5B3 LD ($A00C),A  ; Otherwise #R$A00A($A00C) = 0
*$A5B6 CALL $A5C4    ; Call the routine "Interpret message bytes until the end
                     ; of the message" (#R$A5C4) - Interpret the message
 $A5B9 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A5BA POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A5BC POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A5BD POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A5BE POP HL        ; The return address
 $A5BF LD SP,($A59D) ; Drop the parameters
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$A621.
*$A5C3 JP (HL)       ; Return

; Interpret message bytes until the end of the message
;
; IX walks the message; #R$A5CD handles one element - a dictionary word (bit 7
; set: two bytes), packed letters, a literal character or a control code
; (#R$A621) - and returns Z at the end.
; .
; Only the driver loop: #R$A5CD does the work for each element of the message
; (its own block, with the element types listed there). Every message the game
; prints goes through here.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 77,495 times in 14 of them. Every one of
; its 5 instructions was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:HL The message
; Output:F From the last element
c$A5C4 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - IX = the message at HL
 $A5C5 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
*$A5C7 CALL $A5CD    ; Call the routine "Interpret one message element at IX"
                     ; (#R$A5CD) - Run the interpreter one step at a time
                     ; (#R$A5CD) until it says stop
 $A5CA JR NZ,$A5C7   ; If the zero flag is clear, go back to #R$A5C7
 $A5CC RET           ; Return to the caller

; Interpret one message element at IX
;
; Bit 7 set: a two-byte dictionary reference, printed by #R$A887 - unless its
; flag nibble is $20, $30 or $60, which #R$A647 treats as references to the
; command's own words (actor, objects). $00-$1F: a control code (#R$A621).
; $20-$3F: that character. $40-$5F: a run of (low 5 bits + 1) letters packed 5
; bits each, unpacked by #R$A990. $60 and up: #R$A83A. Read from the code; it
; is what the 118 rendered messages went through.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Bit 7 set: a two-byte dictionary reference. Classes $20, $30 and $60 are
; the sentence-ending words (#R$A647); any other is printed (#R$A887).
; .
; 2. Codes $20 to $3F: a single literal character. $40 to $5F: a run of (code
; AND $1F) + 1 letters packed five bits each (#R$A990), each printed as it is
; unpacked.
; .
; 3. $60 and above: one of the common words (#R$A83A).
; .
; 4. Below $20: a control code, which is looked up in the handler table at
; #R$A55D and called. Codes below $1D carry on; $1D and above end the message.
;
;  Input:IX The message position
; Output:F NZ to go on with the next element; Z when the message has ended
;        IX Moved on
c$A5CD LD A,(IX+$00) ; The next byte
 $A5D0 BIT 7,A       ; Test bit 7 of A - A word reference?
 $A5D2 JR Z,$A5F2    ; If bit 7 of A is clear, skip ahead to #R$A5F2
 $A5D4 AND $7F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 of A (mask $7F) - DE
                     ; = the reference
 $A5D6 LD D,A        ; Copy A into D
 $A5D7 LD E,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into E
 $A5DA INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $A5DC AND $F0       ; Keep only bits 4, 5, 6, 7 of A (mask $F0) - One of the
                     ; command's own words?
 $A5DE CP $30        ; Compare A with $30
 $A5E0 JR Z,$A647    ; If A equals $30, jump on to #R$A647
 $A5E2 CP $20        ; Compare A with $20
 $A5E4 JR Z,$A647    ; If A equals $20, jump on to #R$A647
 $A5E6 CP $60        ; Compare A with $60
 $A5E8 JR Z,$A647    ; If A equals $60, jump on to #R$A647
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$A621 and #R$A83A.
*$A5EA CALL $A887    ; No: print the dictionary word
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$A621.
*$A5ED INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte - Move on
 $A5EF OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $A5F1 RET           ; Return to the caller
*$A5F2 CP $20        ; Compare A with $20 - A control code?
 $A5F4 JR C,$A621    ; If A is below $20, jump on to the routine "Run control
                     ; code A" (#R$A621)
 $A5F6 CP $60        ; Compare A with $60 - $60 and up
 $A5F8 JP NC,$A83A   ; If A is $60 or more, jump on to the routine "Message
                     ; bytes $60 and up: a common word" (#R$A83A)
 $A5FB CP $40        ; Compare A with $40 - A literal character?
 $A5FD JR C,$A61C    ; If A is below $40, skip ahead to #R$A61C
 $A5FF AND $1F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 of A (mask $1F) - Packed
                     ; letters: how many
 $A601 INC A         ; Add one to A
 $A602 LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $A603 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A605 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A606 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A607 LD C,$00      ; Load C with $00
*$A609 CALL $A990    ; Unpack one...
 $A60C CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7) - ...and print it
 $A60F DJNZ $A609    ; Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back to
                     ; #R$A609
 $A611 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A612 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A614 LD A,C        ; Copy C into A
 $A615 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A616 JR NZ,$A5ED   ; If A is not zero, go back to #R$A5ED
 $A618 DEC IX        ; Step IX back one byte
 $A61A JR $A5ED      ; Go back to #R$A5ED
*$A61C CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7) - Print the character
 $A61F JR $A5ED      ; Go back to #R$A5ED

; Run control code A
;
; Through the table at #R$A55D (see there).
;
;  Input:A The control code (below $20)
;        IX The message position
; Output:F Z to stop or NZ to go on, from the handler
c$A621 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack - Control code A: HL = the handler
                     ; from the table at #R$A55D, two bytes each
 $A622 LD E,A        ; Copy A into E
 $A623 LD D,$00      ; Load D with $00
 $A625 LD HL,$A55D   ; Point HL at "Routines for the message control codes
                     ; $00-$1F" (#R$A55D)
 $A628 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A629 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A62A LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E
 $A62B INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A62C LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $A62D EX DE,HL      ; Swap DE and HL
 $A62E POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A62F CP $1D        ; Compare A with $1D - Codes below $1D: call it and carry
                     ; on with the same message
 $A631 JR NC,$A5C3   ; If A is $1D or more, go back to #R$A5C3
 $A633 CALL $A5C3    ; Call #R$A5C3 (inside "Print the message at HL") - $1D
                     ; and above end the message: call it, then Z goes on to
                     ; #R$A5ED, NZ to #R$A5EA
 $A636 JR Z,$A5ED    ; If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$A5ED
 $A638 JR $A5EA      ; Go back to #R$A5EA

; Codes $08 and $0D: print the code itself
;
; Prints A - the control code, so a backspace or a newline - through the
; word-wrapping printer (#R$A9B7), and carries on (XOR A). Code $08 is #R$A736,
; the same three instructions.
;
;  Input:A The code to print
; Output:F Z (XOR A)
c$A63A CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7) - (Print the character in A and
                     ; succeed)
 $A63D XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A63E RET           ; Return to the caller

; Codes $1D and $1E (and $A647): finish a sentence
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A647.
; .
; Jumped to rather than called (#R$A62F). D = $60 ($1D) or $30 ($1E), or,
; entered at #R$A647, D from a word reference just printed by #R$A887. Then:
; with D bit 6 set, a newline (#R$A2A2); with bit 4 set, a full stop and a
; newline. Read from the code.
;
;  Input:D Bit 6: no full stop; bit 4: full stop and new line
; Output:F From the new line
;        A Changed
c$A63F LD D,$60      ; Load D with $60 - Code $1D: D = $60 (end of sentence,
                     ; new line)
 $A641 JR $A64A      ; Skip ahead to #R$A64A
 $A643 LD D,$30      ; Load D with $30 - Code $1E: D = $30 (full stop and new
                     ; line)
 $A645 JR $A64A      ; Skip ahead to #R$A64A
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$A5CD.
*$A647 CALL $A887    ; Entry: print the word at DE (#R$A887), D left as it was
*$A64A LD A,$2E      ; A = '.'
 $A64C BIT 6,D       ; Test bit 6 of D - D bit 6 set: no full stop, straight to
                     ; the new line
 $A64E JR NZ,$A657   ; If bit 6 of D is set, skip ahead to #R$A657
 $A650 BIT 4,D       ; Test bit 4 of D - D bit 4 set: print the full stop
                     ; (#R$A9B7)
 $A652 CALL NZ,$A9B7 ; If bit 4 of D is set, call the routine "Print character
                     ; A, a word at a time (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A655 BIT 4,D       ; ...and a newline (#R$A2A2)
*$A657 CALL NZ,$A2A2 ; Then start a new line (#R$A2A2) if the bit was set

; Code $1F: end of message
;
; XOR A and return: the interpreter stops.
;
; Output:A 0
;        F Z (stop)
c$A65A XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; Neither: also the handler for code $1F, which does
                     ; nothing
 $A65B RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $12: does nothing
;
; A bare RET.
;
; Output:No registers changed
c$A65C RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $00: print the thing whose address is the next parameter
;
; Clears #R$A022($A028) and takes the next word from the message's parameters
; (#R$A66C); if it is not zero, prints that thing's name (#R$A7BA, #R$D405).
;
;  Input:(message parameters) The next word: the thing to name
; Output:F Z
;        A 0
c$A65D XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Clear
                     ; #R$A022($A028)
 $A65E LD ($A028),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A028) in "Game variables"
 $A661 CALL $A66C    ; Call the routine "Code $01: print the next parameter as
                     ; a word (and:..." (#R$A66C) - Take the next message
                     ; parameter (#R$A66C)
 $A664 EX DE,HL      ; Swap DE and HL
 $A665 LD A,H        ; Copy H into A
 $A666 OR L          ; Set in A the bits that are set in L
 $A667 CALL NZ,$A7BA ; If the result is not zero, call the routine "Print the
                     ; name of the thing at HL (as #R$D405)" (#R$A7BA) - If it
                     ; is not zero, print it (#R$A7BA)
 $A66A XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A66B RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $01: print the next parameter as a word (and: fetch the next parameter)
;
; DE = the next word of the parameters the caller pushed before printing the
; message, at #R$A59D, which moves on by two. Returns NZ, and a handler that
; returns NZ has DE printed as a dictionary word reference (#R$A633, #R$A5EA) -
; so as code $01 it prints the parameter as a word. The other codes call it to
; read their parameters.
;
;  Input:$A59D The parameter pointer
; Output:DE The next parameter word
;        HL The pointer, moved on two
;        F NZ
c$A66C LD HL,($A59D) ; The message printer's parameter pointer (#R$A59D)
 $A66F LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E - DE = the next
                     ; parameter word; the pointer moves on two bytes
 $A670 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A671 LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $A672 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A673 LD ($A59D),HL ; Store HL in "Where the message's stack parameters end"
                     ; (#R$A59D)
 $A676 OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $A678 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $02: jump within the message
;
; IX moves on by the two-byte offset that follows, less one. Read from the
; code.
;
;  Input:IX The position of the code
; Output:IX Moved on by the offset
c$A679 LD E,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into E - DE = the word
                     ; at IX+1/+2 (a length)
 $A67C LD D,(IX+$02) ; Read byte +2 of the record at IX into D
 $A67F DEC DE        ; Step DE back one byte - IX moves on by that length less
                     ; one
 $A680 ADD IX,DE     ; Add DE to IX

; Code $1C: does nothing
;
; XOR A and return.
;
; Output:A 0
;        F Z
c$A682 XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A683 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $1A: carry on with the message at the next parameter
;
; IX = the next parameter (#R$A66C): the rest of the printing comes from there.
;
;  Input:(message parameters) The next word: the new message
; Output:IX The new message position
;        A 0
;        F Z
c$A684 CALL $A66C    ; Take the next message parameter (#R$A66C)
 $A687 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack - IX = it (a record or word list)
 $A688 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A68A XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; Z, A = 0
 $A68B RET           ; Return to the caller

; Codes $0F, $14-$17: only in one part of the day
;
; Each asks #R$C00C what part of the day it is (1-5) and, unless it is the
; code's own (code $0F: 5, $14: 4, $15: 3, $16: 2, $17: 1), skips the next
; element of the message (#R$A6B2, #R$A6BB). So a message can say different
; things by day and by night.
;
;  Input:IX The position of the code
; Output:F Z; the next element is printed only in the code's own part of the
; .        day, otherwise it is skipped
;        A 0 when skipped
c$A68C CALL $C00C    ; Call the routine "What part of the day is it? (A = 1-5)"
                     ; (#R$C00C) - Code $0F: the part of the day (#R$C00C) is
                     ; 5?
 $A68F CP $05        ; Compare A with $05
 $A691 RET Z         ; If A equals $05, return
 $A692 JR $A6B2      ; Skip ahead to #R$A6B2
 $A694 CALL $C00C    ; Call the routine "What part of the day is it? (A = 1-5)"
                     ; (#R$C00C) - Code $14: is it 4?
 $A697 CP $04        ; Compare A with $04
 $A699 RET Z         ; If A equals $04, return
 $A69A JR $A6B2      ; Skip ahead to #R$A6B2
 $A69C CALL $C00C    ; Call the routine "What part of the day is it? (A = 1-5)"
                     ; (#R$C00C) - Code $15: is it 3?
 $A69F CP $03        ; Compare A with $03
 $A6A1 RET Z         ; If A equals $03, return
 $A6A2 JR $A6B2      ; Skip ahead to #R$A6B2
 $A6A4 CALL $C00C    ; Call the routine "What part of the day is it? (A = 1-5)"
                     ; (#R$C00C) - Code $16: is it 2?
 $A6A7 CP $02        ; Compare A with $02
 $A6A9 RET Z         ; If A equals $02, return
 $A6AA JR $A6B2      ; Skip ahead to #R$A6B2
 $A6AC CALL $C00C    ; Call the routine "What part of the day is it? (A = 1-5)"
                     ; (#R$C00C) - Code $17: is it 1?
 $A6AF CP $01        ; Compare A with $01
 $A6B1 RET Z         ; If A equals $01, return
*$A6B2 INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte - Not that part: skip the
                     ; element that follows (#R$A6BB)
 $A6B4 CALL $A6BB    ; Call the routine "Skip one message element at IX"
                     ; (#R$A6BB)
 $A6B7 DEC IX        ; Step IX back one byte
 $A6B9 XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; and print nothing
 $A6BA RET           ; Return to the caller

; Skip one message element at IX
;
; A word reference (bit 7) is two bytes, codes $02 and $0B are three,
; everything else one. Read from the code.
;
;  Input:IX An element of a message
; Output:IX The next element
c$A6BB LD A,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into A - Skip
                     ; one message element. Bit 7 set: a two-byte word
                     ; reference...
 $A6BE BIT 7,A       ; Test bit 7 of A
 $A6C0 JR NZ,$A6CC   ; If bit 7 of A is set, skip ahead to #R$A6CC
 $A6C2 CP $02        ; Compare A with $02 - ...codes 2 and $0B take two operand
                     ; bytes (three in all)...
 $A6C4 JR Z,$A6CA    ; If A equals $02, skip ahead to #R$A6CA
 $A6C6 CP $0B        ; Compare A with $0B
 $A6C8 JR NZ,$A6CE   ; If A differs from $0B, skip ahead to #R$A6CE
*$A6CA INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
*$A6CC INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
*$A6CE INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte - ...any other code is a
                     ; single byte
 $A6D0 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $0A: the count, then the choice
b$A6D1 DEFB $00,$00  ; Code $0A: the count, then the choice

; Code $0A: one of the next n elements, at random
;
; The byte after the code is n; a random number from 1 to n (#R$D2A4 on n - 1,
; plus 1) picks which of the following n elements is printed (#R$A5CD), the
; others being skipped (#R$A6BB). The count and the choice are kept at #R$A6D1
; and #R$A6D1($A6D2).
;
;  Input:IX The position of the code (the byte after it is n)
; Output:IX At the end of the n elements
;        A 0
;        F Z
c$A6D3 INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte - The count n that follows
                     ; the code
 $A6D5 LD A,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into A
 $A6D8 LD ($A6D1),A  ; Store A in "Code $0A: the count, then the choice"
                     ; (#R$A6D1) - #R$A6D1 counts the elements down from n
 $A6DB DEC A         ; Subtract one from A
 $A6DC CALL $D2A4    ; Call the routine "A random number from 0 to A" (#R$D2A4)
                     ; - A random number from 0 to n - 1 (#R$D2A4), plus one:
                     ; the element to print (#R$A6D1($A6D2))
 $A6DF INC A         ; Add one to A
 $A6E0 LD ($A6D2),A  ; Store A in #R$A6D1($A6D2) in "Code $0A: the count, then
                     ; the choice"
 $A6E3 INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
*$A6E5 LD A,($A6D2)  ; Read #R$A6D1($A6D2) in "Code $0A: the count, then the
                     ; choice" into A - Is this the chosen element?
 $A6E8 LD HL,$A6D1   ; Point HL at "Code $0A: the count, then the choice"
                     ; (#R$A6D1)
 $A6EB CP (HL)       ; Compare A with the byte at "Code $0A: the count, then
                     ; the choice" (#R$A6D1)
 $A6EC JR NZ,$A6F5   ; If A differs from the byte at "Code $0A: the count, then
                     ; the choice" (#R$A6D1), skip ahead to #R$A6F5
 $A6EE PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $A6F0 CALL $A5CD    ; Call the routine "Interpret one message element at IX"
                     ; (#R$A5CD) - Yes: print it (the interpreter, #R$A5CD)
 $A6F3 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
*$A6F5 CALL $A6BB    ; Call the routine "Skip one message element at IX"
                     ; (#R$A6BB) - Either way skip over it (#R$A6BB)
 $A6F8 LD HL,$A6D1   ; Point HL at "Code $0A: the count, then the choice"
                     ; (#R$A6D1)
 $A6FB DEC (HL)      ; Subtract one from the byte at "Code $0A: the count, then
                     ; the choice" (#R$A6D1) - Until all n have been passed
 $A6FC JR NZ,$A6E5   ; If the byte at "Code $0A: the count, then the choice"
                     ; (#R$A6D1) is not zero, go back to #R$A6E5
 $A6FE DEC IX        ; Step IX back one byte - Back one byte, ready for the
                     ; next token
 $A700 XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A701 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $03: print the word reference in $A00D
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A00A($A00D).
; .
; DE = (#R$A00A($A00D)), returned NZ so that the interpreter prints it as a
; word.
;
; Output:DE The word reference at $A00D
;        F NZ
c$A702 LD DE,($A00D) ; Read #R$A00A($A00D) in "Game variables" into DE
 $A706 OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $A708 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $04: print the next parameter as a word or number
;
; Takes the next parameter (#R$A66C) and prints it with #R$A80E.
;
;  Input:(message parameters) The next word
; Output:A 0
;        F Z
c$A709 CALL $A66C    ; Take the next message parameter (#R$A66C)
 $A70C CALL $A80E    ; Name it (#R$A80E)
 $A70F XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; Z, A = 0
 $A710 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $05: print the case statement at the next parameter
;
; IX = the next parameter, a statement record of the case table (#R$9965),
; printed by #R$BF1C: 'MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER' in Lestrade's 'Well can
; you prove that ... yet, Holmes ?'. Read from the code.
;
;  Input:(message parameters) The next word: a case statement
; Output:A 0
;        F Z
c$A711 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - Code $05: the case statement at
                     ; the next parameter (#R$A66C) in IX
 $A713 CALL $A66C    ; Call the routine "Code $01: print the next parameter as
                     ; a word (and:..." (#R$A66C)
 $A716 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A717 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A719 CALL $BF1C    ; Call the routine "Print a case statement: thing,
                     ; relation, thing" (#R$BF1C) - Print it (#R$BF1C)
 $A71C POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A71E XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A71F RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $06: name the actor
;
; Clears #R$A022($A028) and names the actor in #R$A032($A036) (#R$A82F).
;
; Output:A 0
;        F Z
c$A720 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Code $06: name
                     ; the current actor (#R$A032($A036)) with #R$A022($A028) =
                     ; 0 (#R$A82F)
 $A721 LD ($A028),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A028) in "Game variables"
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$A2E4.
*$A724 LD A,($A036)  ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A
 $A727 CALL $A82F    ; Call the routine "Name actor A ($FF: SOMEONE)" (#R$A82F)
 $A72A XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A72B RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $07: name the direct object
;
; Sets #R$A022($A028) and names the direct object in #R$A032($A034) (#R$A751:
; #R$A4BC, #R$A7C5).
;
; Output:A 0
;        F Z
c$A72C LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - Code $07: name the direct object
                     ; (#R$A032($A034)) with #R$A022($A028) = 1
 $A72E LD ($A028),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A028) in "Game variables"
 $A731 LD A,($A034)  ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A
 $A734 JR $A751      ; Skip ahead to #R$A751

; Code $08: print the code itself
;
; As #R$A63A.
;
;  Input:A The code to print
; Output:A 0
;        F Z
c$A736 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7) - Code $08: print the character
                     ; in A
 $A739 XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A73A RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $09: name the indirect object
;
; As code $07, for #R$A032($A035).
;
; Output:A 0
;        F Z
c$A73B LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - Code $09: name the indirect object
                     ; (#R$A032($A035)) with #R$A022($A028) = 1
 $A73D LD ($A028),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A028) in "Game variables"
 $A740 LD A,($A035)  ; Read #R$A032($A035) in "Game variables" into A
 $A743 JR $A751      ; Skip ahead to #R$A751

; Name room or thing A
;
; Entered with carry from #R$A3DE($A3DF)/#R$A411($A412): carry clear names a
; room (#R$A4AB, #R$A7BA), carry set - and #R$A751, used by codes $07 and $09 -
; a thing (#R$A4BC, #R$A7C5).
;
;  Input:A The room or thing number
;        F Carry set for a room, clear for a thing
; Output:A 0
;        HL Kept
c$A745 JR NC,$A751   ; No carry (from the caller's compare): a thing - go to
                     ; #R$A751
 $A747 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - Carry: a room: HL = its name
                     ; words (#R$A4AB), print them (#R$A7BA)
 $A748 CALL $A4AB    ; Call the routine "HL = room A's record + 1" (#R$A4AB)
 $A74B CALL $A7BA    ; Call the routine "Print the name of the thing at HL (as
                     ; #R$D405)" (#R$A7BA)
 $A74E POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A74F XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A750 RET           ; Return to the caller
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$A72C, #R$A73B and #R$A82F.
*$A751 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - A thing: HL = its name words
                     ; (#R$A4BC), print them (#R$A7C5)
 $A752 CALL $A4BC    ; Call the routine "HL = thing A's record + 7" (#R$A4BC)
 $A755 CALL $A7C5    ; Call the routine "Print the name of the thing at HL (as
                     ; #R$D3E8)" (#R$A7C5)
 $A758 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A759 XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; Return A = 0
 $A75A RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $0B: print a message at an offset from here
;
; HL = IX + the two-byte offset that follows; prints the message there
; (#R$A5C4) and steps over the offset: a sub-message.
;
;  Input:IX The position of the code ($0B), followed by a 16-bit offset
; Output:IX Past the offset
;        A 0
;        F Z
c$A75B PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - Code $0B: IX is the control code;
                     ; HL = IX + the offset in its next two bytes
 $A75D POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A75E PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A75F LD E,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into E
 $A762 LD D,(IX+$02) ; Read byte +2 of the record at IX into D
 $A765 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A766 CALL $A5C4    ; Call the routine "Interpret message bytes until the end
                     ; of the message" (#R$A5C4) - Print that message
                     ; (#R$A5C4), then move IX past the operand
 $A769 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A76B INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $A76D INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $A76F XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $A770 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $19: print the message at the next parameter
;
; If the parameter is not zero (#R$A5C4).
;
;  Input:(message parameters) The next word: a message, or 0
; Output:A 0
;        F Z
c$A771 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - Code $19: take the next message
                     ; parameter (#R$A66C)...
 $A773 CALL $A66C    ; Call the routine "Code $01: print the next parameter as
                     ; a word (and:..." (#R$A66C)
 $A776 EX DE,HL      ; Swap DE and HL - ...and if it is not zero, print the
                     ; message it points to (#R$A5C4)
 $A777 LD A,L        ; Copy L into A
 $A778 OR H          ; Set in A the bits that are set in H
 $A779 CALL NZ,$A5C4 ; If the result is not zero, call the routine "Interpret
                     ; message bytes until the end of the message" (#R$A5C4)
 $A77C POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A77E XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; Done, A = 0
 $A77F RET           ; Return to the caller

; Codes $0C and $0E: YOUR or HIS
;
; DE = $09F2 if the actor in #R$A032($A036) (code $0C) or the direct object in
; #R$A032($A034) (code $0E) is the player (0), $0414 otherwise; returns NZ so
; that the interpreter prints DE as a word reference (#R$A633 -> #R$A5EA):
; $09F2 is YOUR and $0414 is HIS (looked up with source/dictionary.py).
;
;  Input:$A036 The actor (code $0C); $A034 the direct object (code $0E)
; Output:DE $09F2 (YOUR) for the player, $0414 (HIS) for anyone else
;        F NZ
c$A780 LD A,($A036)  ; Code $0C: whose? A = the actor (#R$A032($A036))
*$A783 LD DE,$0414   ; Load DE with $0414 - DE = $0414 (HIS), returned NZ when
                     ; A is not zero
 $A786 AND A         ; Clear the carry flag for the caller (A is unchanged)
 $A787 RET NZ        ; If A is not zero, return
 $A788 LD DE,$09F2   ; Load DE with $09F2 - The player (A = 0): DE = $09F2
                     ; (YOUR), NZ
 $A78B OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $A78D RET           ; Return to the caller
 $A78E LD A,($A034)  ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A - Code
                     ; $0E: the same for the direct object (#R$A032($A034))
 $A791 JR $A783      ; Go back to #R$A783

; Codes $10, $11 and $13: name someone, then ARE or IS
;
; Names the actor ($10), the direct object ($11) or the person in the next
; parameter ($13) with #R$A82F, then DE = $007A if it was the player and $0485
; if not - ARE and IS - for the interpreter to print: 'you are', 'Watson is'.
; Read from the code; words looked up with source/dictionary.py.
;
;  Input:$A036 The actor (code $10); $A034 the direct object (code $11); the
; .            next parameter (code $13)
; Output:DE ARE ($007A) for the player, IS ($0485) otherwise
;        F NZ for the player; the flags of the compare otherwise
c$A793 LD A,($A036)  ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A - Keep
                     ; the current actor in A'
 $A796 EX AF,AF'     ; Swap AF with the shadow copy
 $A797 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
*$A798 LD ($A028),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A028) in "Game variables" -
                     ; #R$A022($A028) = 0 (the thing is the actor); for
                     ; #R$A7AC, 1 (a thing named by the command)
 $A79B EX AF,AF'     ; Swap AF with the shadow copy
 $A79C PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A79D CALL $A82F    ; Call the routine "Name actor A ($FF: SOMEONE)" (#R$A82F)
                     ; - Name them (#R$A82F): a thing's name words, or YOU
                     ; ($080E) for $FF
 $A7A0 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A7A1 AND A         ; Z (the player) leaves DE = ARE ($007A)...
 $A7A2 LD DE,$0485   ; Load DE with $0485 - ...otherwise IS ($0485)
 $A7A5 RET NZ        ; If A is not zero, return
 $A7A6 LD DE,$007A   ; Load DE with $007A
 $A7A9 OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $A7AB RET           ; Return to the caller
 $A7AC LD A,($A034)  ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A - Entry
                     ; for code $11: the direct object (#R$A032($A034))
*$A7AF EX AF,AF'     ; Swap AF with the shadow copy
 $A7B0 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01
 $A7B2 JR $A798      ; Go back to #R$A798
 $A7B4 CALL $A66C    ; Call the routine "Code $01: print the next parameter as
                     ; a word (and:..." (#R$A66C) - Entry for code $13: the
                     ; person named in the next parameter (#R$A66C)
 $A7B7 LD A,D        ; Copy D into A
 $A7B8 JR $A7AF      ; Go back to #R$A7AF

; Print the name of the thing at HL (as $D405)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$D405.
; .
; With IY = HL.
;
;  Input:HL A thing's name words
; Output:The name printed ($D405); IY kept
c$A7BA PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack - IY = HL; print the thing's name
                     ; words (#R$D405)
 $A7BC PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A7BD POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A7BF CALL $D405    ; Call the routine "Print a thing's name with its
                     ; adjectives (IY = its..." (#R$D405)
 $A7C2 POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A7C4 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Print the name of the thing at HL (as $D3E8)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$D3E8.
; .
; With IY = HL.
;
;  Input:HL A thing's name words
; Output:The name printed ($D3E8); IY kept
c$A7C5 PUSH IY       ; Save IY on the stack - IY = HL; print the name words at
                     ; IY (#R$D3E8)
 $A7C7 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A7C8 POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A7CA CALL $D3E8    ; Call the routine "Print the name words at IY" (#R$D3E8)
 $A7CD POP IY        ; Restore IY from the stack
 $A7CF RET           ; Return to the caller

; Print an article, or note whether the word is YOU
;
; Sets #R$A7EA to 1 unless DE is a word reference to YOU ($09EE). When DE is
; not a word reference at all (bit 7 of D clear) it is an article code: #R$A7EB
; prints THE, A, AN or SOME from #R$A24D - or from #R$A255, all THE but SOME,
; when #R$A022($A027) or #R$A2AA is set. Also used by #R$D0E1($D0E3),
; #R$D3F0($D3F1) and #R$D40D($D40E). Read from the code.
;
;  Input:DE A word reference or an article code
; Output:$A7EA 1 unless the word is YOU
;        F Z if the word is YOU
c$A7D0 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - #R$A7EA = 0: the
                     ; word is not YOU
 $A7D1 LD ($A7EA),A  ; Store A in "#R$A7D0: set when the word was not YOU"
                     ; (#R$A7EA)
 $A7D4 BIT 7,D       ; Test bit 7 of D - Bit 7 of D clear: print the article by
                     ; code (#R$A7EB)
 $A7D6 JR Z,$A7EB    ; If bit 7 of D is clear, skip ahead to the routine "Print
                     ; the article for article code DE" (#R$A7EB)
 $A7D8 LD A,E        ; Copy E into A - Is the word reference $09EE (YOU)? Then
                     ; print nothing
 $A7D9 LD HL,$09EE   ; Load HL with $09EE
 $A7DC CP L          ; Compare A with L
 $A7DD JR NZ,$A7E4   ; If A differs from L, skip ahead to #R$A7E4
 $A7DF LD A,D        ; Copy D into A
 $A7E0 AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
 $A7E2 CP H          ; Compare A with H
 $A7E3 RET Z         ; If A equals H, return
*$A7E4 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - Otherwise note a word that is not YOU
                     ; (#R$A7EA = 1) and return
 $A7E6 LD ($A7EA),A  ; Store A in "#R$A7D0: set when the word was not YOU"
                     ; (#R$A7EA)
 $A7E9 RET           ; Return to the caller

; $A7D0: set when the word was not YOU
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A7D0.
b$A7EA DEFB $00      ; #R$A7D0: set when the word was not YOU

; Print the article for article code DE
;
; THE, A, AN or SOME from #R$A24D - or from #R$A255 when #R$A022($A027) or
; #R$A2AA is set - picked by bits 4-5 of D (#R$A887 prints it). Reached from
; #R$A7D0 when DE is not a word reference. Read from the code.
;
;  Input:D The article code in its top nibble
; Output:The article (THE, A, AN or SOME) printed
;        DE Kept
c$A7EB LD HL,$A24D   ; Indefinite table #R$A24D: THE, A, AN, SOME
 $A7EE LD A,($A027)  ; Read #R$A022($A027) in "Game variables" into A - Unless
                     ; #R$A022($A027) or #R$A2AA is set...
 $A7F1 LD E,A        ; Copy A into E
 $A7F2 LD A,($A2AA)  ; Read "Use THE: a flag for the article printer (#R$A7F2)"
                     ; (#R$A2AA) into A
 $A7F5 OR E          ; Set in A the bits that are set in E
 $A7F6 JR Z,$A7FB    ; If the result is zero, skip ahead to #R$A7FB
 $A7F8 LD HL,$A255   ; ...then table #R$A255: THE, THE, THE, SOME (a definite
                     ; narration)
*$A7FB LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - The article code is the top nibble of D;
                     ; two bytes an entry
 $A7FC RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A7FD RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A7FE RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A7FF AND $1E       ; Keep only bits 1, 2, 3, 4 of A (mask $1E)
 $A801 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A802 LD E,A        ; Copy A into E
 $A803 LD D,$00      ; Load D with $00
 $A805 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A806 LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E - DE = the word; print
                     ; it (#R$A887)
 $A807 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A808 LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $A809 CALL $A887    ; Call #R$A887 (inside "Print the dictionary word that HL
                     ; refers to")
 $A80C POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A80D RET           ; Return to the caller

; Print word reference DE (code $04's printer)
;
; Through #R$A7D0, then as a dictionary word (#R$A887) with flag nibble $5
; unless it is empty; ORs #R$A7EA into #R$A022($A026).
;
;  Input:DE A word reference
; Output:The word printed, as a noun ($A887)
;        $A026 Marked if the word is not YOU
c$A80E PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack - Print the word's preceding
                     ; space/capital handling first (#R$A7D0)
 $A80F CALL $A7D0    ; Call the routine "Print an article, or note whether the
                     ; word is YOU" (#R$A7D0)
 $A812 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A813 LD A,$50      ; Load A with $50 - Class $50 (a noun) is put in the top
                     ; nibble of D
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$D405.
*$A815 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A816 LD A,D        ; Copy D into A
 $A817 AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
 $A819 LD D,A        ; Copy A into D
 $A81A POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A81B OR D          ; Set in A the bits that are set in D
 $A81C LD D,A        ; Copy A into D
 $A81D AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F) - Nothing
                     ; there (D and E zero apart from the class)? Return
 $A81F OR E          ; Set in A the bits that are set in E
 $A820 RET Z         ; If the result is zero, return
 $A821 CALL $A887    ; Print the word (#R$A887)
 $A824 LD A,($A7EA)  ; Read "#R$A7D0: set when the word was not YOU" (#R$A7EA)
                     ; into A - Note the print state of the word's last letter
                     ; (#R$A7EA) in #R$A022($A026)
 $A827 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A828 LD HL,$A026   ; Point HL at #R$A022($A026) in "Game variables"
 $A82B OR (HL)       ; Set in A the bits that are set in the byte at
                     ; #R$A022($A026) in "Game variables"
 $A82C LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to
 $A82D POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A82E RET           ; Return to the caller

; Name actor A ($FF: SOMEONE)
;
; A thing name (#R$A751) unless A is $FF, which prints SOMEONE ($080E).
;
;  Input:A The actor number ($FF is SOMEONE)
; Output:The name printed
c$A82F CP $FF        ; Actor $FF?
 $A831 JP NZ,$A751   ; No: name the thing (#R$A751)
 $A834 LD DE,$080E   ; Load DE with $080E - Yes: print the word $080E (#R$A887)
 $A837 JP $A887      ; Jump on to #R$A887

; Message bytes $60 and up: a common word
;
; Byte - $60 indexes the two-byte table at #R$67B7; the word reference it holds
; (with $50 added to the high byte) is printed like any other (#R$A5EA).
; #R$A840 does the lookup and is also used by #R$B7D5($B7D6).
;
;  Input:A The byte, $60 or more
; Output:The common word printed
;        F NZ
c$A83A CALL $A840    ; Call #R$A840 (inside "Message bytes $60 and up: a common
                     ; word") - Convert the common-word byte (#R$A840) and
                     ; print it as a word reference (#R$A5EA)
 $A83D JP $A5EA      ; Go back to #R$A5EA
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$B77F.
*$A840 SUB $60       ; Subtract $60 from A - Index = A - $60; the list of words
                     ; is at #R$67B7, two bytes each
 $A842 LD E,A        ; Copy A into E
 $A843 LD D,$00      ; Load D with $00
 $A845 LD HL,$67B7   ; Point HL at "Message bytecode: everything the game
                     ; prints" (#R$67B7)
 $A848 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A849 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $A84A LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E
 $A84B INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A84C LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $A84D ADD A,$50     ; Add $50 to A - High byte + $50: class $50, as for a noun
 $A84F LD D,A        ; Copy A into D
 $A850 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Code $1B: the all-capitals flag (read at $AA59)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$AA59.
b$A851 DEFB $00      ; Code $1B: the flag it flips

; Code $1B: capitals on/off
;
; Toggles #R$A851. While it is set every letter is printed as a capital
; (#R$AA59) instead of the usual sentence case. It is not how the game's
; refusals come out in capitals: with a breakpoint here, 'YOU CANNOT GO WEST.'
; and 'I DO NOT KNOW THE WORD...' were printed without reaching it, so they
; take another route to the screen. Which messages use code $1B was not found.
; Read from the code.
;
; Output:A 0
;        $A851 Toggled
c$A852 LD A,($A851)  ; Read "Code $1B: the all-capitals flag (read at #R$AA59)"
                     ; (#R$A851) into A - Toggle the capital flag #R$A851
 $A855 XOR $01       ; Flip in A the bits that are set in $01
 $A857 LD ($A851),A  ; Store A in "Code $1B: the all-capitals flag (read at
                     ; #R$AA59)" (#R$A851)
 $A85A XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; A = 0
 $A85B RET           ; Return to the caller

; Word suffixes
;
; Nine 4-byte entries, zero-padded, chosen by the low nibble of a dictionary
; word's flags ($F = no suffix; see dictionary.py). Code 8 is a delete, so
; suffix 3 turns SPY into SPIES and suffix 7 RIDE into RIDING. #R$A93A($A93B)
; indexes it.
; .
; CORRECTED: the automatic split had this as code.
b$A85C DEFB $53,$00,$00,$00 ; Suffix 0: "S"
 $A860 DEFB $45,$53,$00,$00 ; Suffix 1: "ES"
 $A864 DEFB $49,$45,$53,$00 ; Suffix 2: "IES"
 $A868 DEFB $08,$49,$45,$53 ; Suffix 3: "<delete>IES"
 $A86C DEFB $44,$00,$00,$00 ; Suffix 4: "D"
 $A870 DEFB $49,$4E,$47,$00 ; Suffix 5: "ING"
 $A874 DEFB $45,$44,$00,$00 ; Suffix 6: "ED"
 $A878 DEFB $08,$49,$4E,$47 ; Suffix 7: "<delete>ING"
 $A87C DEFB $4C,$59,$00,$00 ; Suffix 8: "LY"

; Print the dictionary word that HL refers to
;
; HL points at a 2-byte word reference (low 12 bits: offset into the
; dictionary; high nibble: flags). Prints nothing when output is switched off
; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) = 0: the text belongs to something the
; player cannot see) or the reference is 0. Otherwise prints a space, finds the
; word through the per-letter index at #R$5D80, prints it (#R$A95B) and its
; suffix. Called by the object-name printers at #R$A39E($A39F)-#R$A3F7($A3F8)
; and by #R$D5E4($D5E5). Read from the code, not traced in detail.
; .
; .
; .
; Line comments added from reading the code; the flag-nibble rule for the
; suffix ($50/$40/$10) is read from the branches, not yet shown by playing.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Take the 12-bit offset and the flag nibble from the two bytes at HL; print
; nothing if output is off (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C)) or the offset is
; 0.
; .
; 2. Print a space, then find the letter whose records contain the offset by
; searching the per-letter index at #R$5D80.
; .
; 3. From that letter's first record, spell each record (#R$A95B) until the
; offset is reached; the spelling is built at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42)
; from its kept letters, new packed letters and suffix.
; .
; 4. Print the spelling a letter at a time through the word-wrap printer,
; applying capitals and the flag nibble's rules.
;
;  Input:HL A word reference (two bytes)
; Output:HL Past the reference
;        The word printed
c$A880 LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E - DE = the 2-byte word
                     ; reference at HL: low 12 bits the dictionary offset...
 $A881 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A882 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $A883 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A884 AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F) - ...high
                     ; nibble the flags; HL moves on past it
 $A886 LD D,A        ; Copy A into D
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$A2E4, #R$A37D, #R$A5CD,
; #R$A63F, #R$A7EB, #R$A80E, #R$A82F, #R$BF1C, #R$CB7B, #R$D113, #R$D526,
; #R$D5D3, #R$D60B and #R$D68C.
*$A887 LD A,($5C3C)  ; Read the output-switch byte (TV_FLAG)
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C)) into A - Output
                     ; switched off (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) = 0)?
 $A88A AND A         ; Clear the carry flag for the caller (A is unchanged)
 $A88B RET Z         ; Yes: print nothing
 $A88C LD ($A022),DE ; Remember the reference in #R$A022
 $A890 LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - Offset zero: no word, print nothing
 $A891 AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
 $A893 OR E          ; Set in A the bits that are set in E
 $A894 RET Z         ; If the result is zero, return
 $A895 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - Save the registers the dictionary
                     ; search will use
 $A897 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A898 PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack
 $A899 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $A89A LD A,$20      ; Load A with $20 - Print a space first (#R$A9B7)
 $A89C CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A89F LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - Flag nibble into C
 $A8A0 AND $F0       ; Keep only bits 4, 5, 6, 7 of A (mask $F0)
 $A8A2 LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $A8A3 CP $70        ; Flag $70?
 $A8A5 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - Then set #R$A022($A026) to 1
 $A8A7 JR NZ,$A8AC   ; If A differs from $70, skip ahead to #R$A8AC
 $A8A9 LD ($A026),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A026) in "Game variables"
*$A8AC LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - Offset alone into HL
 $A8AD AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
 $A8AF LD D,A        ; Copy A into D
 $A8B0 EX DE,HL      ; Swap DE and HL
 $A8B1 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A8B2 LD A,H        ; Copy H into A
 $A8B3 OR C          ; Set in A the bits that are set in C
 $A8B4 LD H,A        ; Copy A into H
 $A8B5 LD ($A022),HL ; Store HL in #R$A022 in "Game variables" - #R$A022 =
                     ; offset with the flag nibble
 $A8B8 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A8B9 PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack
 $A8BA LD IX,$5D80   ; IX = the per-letter index at #R$5D80 (2 bytes a letter)
 $A8BE LD B,$5F      ; Load B with $5F - B counts letters from 'a' (the code
                     ; before 'a' is $60)
*$A8C0 INC B         ; Add one to B
 $A8C1 LD A,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into A - Find the last
                     ; letter whose first record starts at or before the offset
                     ; (high byte, then low)
 $A8C4 CP H          ; Compare A with H
 $A8C5 JR C,$A8CF    ; If A is below H, skip ahead to #R$A8CF
 $A8C7 JR NZ,$A8D9   ; If A differs from H, skip ahead to #R$A8D9
 $A8C9 LD A,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into A
 $A8CC CP L          ; Compare A with L
 $A8CD JR NC,$A8D9   ; If A is L or more, skip ahead to #R$A8D9
*$A8CF INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $A8D1 INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $A8D3 LD A,B        ; Copy B into A
 $A8D4 CP $7B        ; Compare A with $7B - Ran past 'z' ($7B)
 $A8D6 JR NZ,$A8C0   ; If A differs from $7B, go back to #R$A8C0
 $A8D8 INC B         ; Add one to B
*$A8D9 JR Z,$A8E0    ; If B has become zero, skip ahead to #R$A8E0
*$A8DB DEC IX        ; Step IX back one byte - Step back to the previous letter
 $A8DD DEC IX        ; Step IX back one byte
 $A8DF DEC B         ; Subtract one from B
*$A8E0 LD E,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into E - DE =
                     ; where that letter's records start
 $A8E3 LD D,(IX+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into D
 $A8E6 LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - Zero means no word begins with that
                     ; letter: step back again
 $A8E7 OR E          ; Set in A the bits that are set in E
 $A8E8 JR Z,$A8DB    ; If the result is zero, go back to #R$A8DB
 $A8EA LD A,B        ; Copy B into A - Print the first letter itself (#R$A9B7)
 $A8EB CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A8EE LD BC,$5DBF   ; Load BC with #R$5DBE($5DBF), the amount that is added
                     ; next - Dictionary base #R$5DBE($5DBF) turns offsets into
                     ; addresses
 $A8F1 ADD HL,BC     ; Add BC to HL
 $A8F2 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A8F3 EX DE,HL      ; Swap DE and HL
 $A8F4 ADD HL,BC     ; Add BC to HL
*$A8F5 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - Unpack records one at a time
                     ; (#R$A95B, front-coded) from the letter's first record up
                     ; to the wanted word, leaving its spelling in
                     ; #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42)
 $A8F6 AND A         ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that
                     ; follows is a plain one (a compare)
 $A8F7 SBC HL,DE     ; Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller,
                     ; zero = equal)
 $A8F9 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack - (compare with the wanted
                     ; word's address)
 $A8FA PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A8FB CALL $A95B    ; Call the routine "Unpack a dictionary record's letters
                     ; (front-coded)" (#R$A95B)
 $A8FE POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A8FF JR NZ,$A8F5   ; Not there yet: next record
 $A901 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack - Restore HL, BC
 $A902 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A903 LD A,C        ; The flag nibble decides whether the suffix is added
                     ; (#R$A92F):
 $A904 CP $50        ; Compare A with $50 - $50: never
 $A906 JR Z,$A91C    ; If A equals $50, skip ahead to #R$A91C
 $A908 CP $40        ; Compare A with $40 - $40: always
 $A90A JR Z,$A919    ; If A equals $40, skip ahead to #R$A919
 $A90C CP $10        ; Compare A with $10 - $10: if the direct object
                     ; #R$A032($A034) is non-zero; any other: if the actor
                     ; #R$A032($A036) is
 $A90E LD A,($A034)  ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A
 $A911 JR Z,$A916    ; If A equals $10, skip ahead to #R$A916
 $A913 LD A,($A036)  ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A
*$A916 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A917 JR Z,$A91C    ; Zero: no suffix
*$A919 CALL $A92F    ; Add the dictionary suffix (#R$A92F)
*$A91C LD HL,$5C42   ; Point HL at the BASIC workspace
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42)) - Print the unpacked
                     ; spelling at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42) up to its 0
                     ; terminator, a character at a time
*$A91F LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $A920 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A921 JR Z,$A929    ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$A929
 $A923 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $A926 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A927 JR $A91F      ; Go back to #R$A91F
*$A929 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack - Restore the registers and
                     ; return
 $A92A POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A92B POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A92C POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A92E RET           ; Return to the caller

; Add a dictionary word's suffix to the spelling at IX
;
; The low nibble of (HL) picks one of the four-letter suffixes at #R$A85C (15 =
; none), copied to IX and ended with 0. Read from the code.
;
;  Input:HL A dictionary record
;        IX The end of the spelling
; Output:IX At the end of the suffix, ended with 0
c$A92F LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - Low nibble of the
                     ; record's byte picks a suffix
 $A930 AND $0F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
 $A932 CP $0F        ; Compare A with $0F - 15: none
 $A934 RET Z         ; If A equals $0F, return
 $A935 RLCA          ; Rotate A left one place (bit 7 goes to carry and bit 0)
                     ; - Times 4: an index into the table at #R$A85C
 $A936 RLCA          ; Rotate A left one place (bit 7 goes to carry and bit 0)
 $A937 LD L,A        ; Copy A into L
 $A938 LD H,$00      ; Load H with $00
 $A93A LD BC,$A85C   ; Load BC with #R$A85C (-22436 as a signed offset), the
                     ; amount that is added next
 $A93D ADD HL,BC     ; Add BC to HL
 $A93E LD B,$04      ; Load B with $04 - Copy four letters to IX...
*$A940 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $A941 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A942 LD (IX+$00),A ; Store A in the first byte of the record at IX
 $A945 INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $A947 DJNZ $A940    ; Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back to
                     ; #R$A940
 $A949 LD (IX+$00),B ; Store B in the first byte of the record at IX - ...and
                     ; end with 0
 $A94C RET           ; Return to the caller

; Spell dictionary record HL into the buffer at $5C42
;
; Addresses named in the title: #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42).
; .
; Its letters (#R$A95B), then its suffix (#R$A92F).
;
;  Input:HL A dictionary record
; Output:The spelling at $5C42
;        HL, IX Kept
c$A94D PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack - Spell dictionary record HL: the
                     ; front part first (#R$A95B), then a suffix if there is
                     ; one (#R$A92F)
 $A94F PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A950 CALL $A95B    ; Call the routine "Unpack a dictionary record's letters
                     ; (front-coded)" (#R$A95B)
 $A953 EX (SP),HL    ; Swap HL with the word on top of the stack
 $A954 CALL $A92F    ; Call the routine "Add a dictionary word's suffix to the
                     ; spelling at IX" (#R$A92F)
 $A957 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A958 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $A95A RET           ; Return to the caller

; Unpack a dictionary record's letters (front-coded)
;
; The dictionary is front-coded: bits 6-4 of a record's count byte say how many
; letters to keep from the word before (IX simply starts that far into
; #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42)), bits 3-0 how many new letters follow,
; packed five bits each (#R$A990). Records with bit 7 set carry two extra
; bytes. Read from the code; it is the format source/dictionary.py decodes.
;
;  Input:HL A dictionary record
; Output:The record's spelling at $5C42, ended with 0
;        HL Past the record
c$A95B PUSH BC         ; IX = the spelling buffer
                       ; #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42); HL past the record's
                       ; first byte
 $A95C LD IX,$5C42     ; The spelling goes to #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42)
                       ; on (a ROM system-variable area the game reuses)
 $A960 INC HL          ; A = the count byte; a record with bit 7 set has two
                       ; more bytes before the letters
 $A961 LD A,(HL)       ; Read the byte HL points at into A - The second byte:
                       ; bit 7 set means two more bytes follow (a suffix
                       ; reference)
 $A962 BIT 7,A         ; Test bit 7 of A
 $A964 INC HL          ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A965 JR Z,$A969      ; If bit 7 of A is clear, skip ahead to #R$A969
 $A967 INC HL          ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A968 INC HL          ; Step HL on to the next byte
*$A969 PUSH AF         ; Bits 6-4: letters kept from the word before - just
                       ; skip over them
 $A96A AND $70         ; Keep only bits 4, 5, 6 of A (mask $70) - Bits 4-6: how
                       ; many letters are kept from the previous word - IX
                       ; steps over them
 $A96C JR Z,$A974      ; If bits 4, 5, 6 of A were all clear, skip ahead to
                       ; #R$A974
*$A96E INC IX          ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $A970 SUB $10         ; Subtract $10 from A
 $A972 JR NZ,$A96E     ; If the two values differ, go back to #R$A96E
*$A974 LD C,$00        ; C = the bit position in the packed letters
 $A976 POP AF          ; Bits 3-0: new letters to unpack
 $A977 AND $0F         ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F) - The low
                       ; nibble: how many new letters follow, five bits each
                       ; (#R$A990)
 $A979 JR Z,$A986      ; If bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A were all clear, skip ahead to
                       ; #R$A986
 $A97B LD B,A          ; Copy A into B
*$A97C CALL $A990      ; Call the routine "Unpack the next five-bit letter (C =
                       ; bit position)" (#R$A990) - Unpack one and store it
 $A97F LD (IX+$00),A   ; Store A in the first byte of the record at IX
 $A982 INC IX          ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $A984 DJNZ $A97C      ; Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back to
                       ; #R$A97C
*$A986 LD (IX+$00),$00 ; End the spelling with 0
 $A98A LD A,C          ; Copy C into A - If the letters ended part-way through
                       ; a byte, step HL past it
 $A98B POP BC          ; Restore BC from the stack
 $A98C AND A           ; Clear the carry flag for the caller (A is unchanged)
 $A98D RET Z           ; If A is zero, return
 $A98E INC HL          ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A98F RET             ; Return to the caller

; Unpack the next five-bit letter (C = bit position)
;
; Returns 'a' + n - 1 style codes ($60 + n). Read from the code.
;
;  Input:HL The packed text
;        C The bit position (0 to 7)
; Output:A The letter code ($60 + n)
;        HL, C Moved on five bits
c$A990 PUSH DE       ; HL = the packed text, C = the bit position (0-7) in the
                     ; byte at HL
 $A991 LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D - DE = this byte and
                     ; the next, big-endian
 $A992 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $A993 LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E
 $A994 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $A995 LD A,C        ; Copy C into A - Shift left C places to bring the letter
                     ; to the top...
 $A996 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A997 JR Z,$A9A0    ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$A9A0
*$A999 SLA E         ; Shift E left (double it)
 $A99B RL D          ; Rotate D left through the carry
 $A99D DEC A         ; Subtract one from A
 $A99E JR NZ,$A999   ; If A is not zero, go back to #R$A999
*$A9A0 LD A,C        ; Copy C into A - Advance by five bits: C + 5, with a byte
                     ; on (HL + 1) when it passes 8
 $A9A1 ADD A,$05     ; Add $05 to A
 $A9A3 CP $08        ; Compare A with $08
 $A9A5 JR C,$A9AA    ; If A is below $08, skip ahead to #R$A9AA
 $A9A7 SUB $08       ; Subtract $08 from A
 $A9A9 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
*$A9AA LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $A9AB LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - The top five bits of D (RRCA x3, AND
                     ; $1F)...
 $A9AC POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $A9AD RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A9AE RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A9AF RRCA          ; Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
 $A9B0 AND $1F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 of A (mask $1F)
 $A9B2 ADD A,$60     ; Add $60 to A - ...plus $60: the letter's code, $60 (a)
                     ; upwards
 $A9B4 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Word buffer pointer for $A9B7
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$A9B7.
; .
; Where the next character of the word being collected goes; the buffer starts
; at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B).
w$A9B5 DEFW $5C0B    ; Next free byte in the word buffer

; Print character A, a word at a time (word wrap)
;
; Every character the message interpreter prints comes here. Unless quoted
; printing is on (#R$A022($A027): straight to #R$C1E1), characters are
; collected in the buffer at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B), a backspace (8)
; taking the last one off again - which is how suffixes such as '<delete>IES'
; work. A space, a carriage return or a quote mark ends the word: if the word
; is longer than the count at #R$C1F5 allows, a new line is started first, and
; then the word is sent to #R$AA14 character by character. #R$AA14 turns
; letters to lower case and drops spaces at the start of a line (#R$A022($A025)
; = 0 there). Read from the code.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. If quoted printing is on (#R$A022($A027)), send the character straight out
; (#R$C1E1).
; .
; 2. A space, carriage return or quote ends a word. Any other character is
; added to the word buffer at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B); a delete removes
; the last letter.
; .
; 3. When a word ends: if it does not fit on the rest of the line (#R$C1F5),
; start a new line first. Then print the word, and then the character that
; ended it.
;
;  Input:A The character to print
; Output:All registers kept
c$A9B7 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack - Quoted printing on?
 $A9B8 LD A,($A027)  ; Read #R$A022($A027) in "Game variables" into A
 $A9BB AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $A9BC JR NZ,$AA10   ; If A is not zero, jump on to #R$AA10 - Yes: print it as
                     ; it is
 $A9BE POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A9BF PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A9C0 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $A9C1 LD HL,($A9B5) ; The end of the word so far
 $A9C4 CP $20        ; Compare A with $20 - A space...
 $A9C6 JR Z,$A9DF    ; If A equals $20, skip ahead to #R$A9DF
 $A9C8 CP $0D        ; Compare A with $0D - ...a carriage return...
 $A9CA JR Z,$A9DF    ; If A equals $0D, skip ahead to #R$A9DF
 $A9CC CP $22        ; Compare A with $22 - ...or a quote mark ends the word
 $A9CE JR Z,$A9DF    ; If A equals $22, skip ahead to #R$A9DF
 $A9D0 CP $08        ; Compare A with $08 - A delete...
 $A9D2 JR NZ,$A9D7   ; If A differs from $08, skip ahead to #R$A9D7
 $A9D4 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte - ...takes the last letter back
 $A9D5 JR $A9D9      ; Skip ahead to #R$A9D9
*$A9D7 LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to - Otherwise add the
                     ; letter
 $A9D8 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
*$A9D9 LD ($A9B5),HL ; Store HL in "Word buffer pointer for #R$A9B7" (#R$A9B5)
 $A9DC POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $A9DD POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $A9DE RET           ; Return to the caller
*$A9DF PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack - An empty word?
 $A9E0 LD DE,$5C0B   ; Point DE at the tape header workspace
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B))
 $A9E3 AND A         ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that
                     ; follows is a plain one (a compare)
 $A9E4 SBC HL,DE     ; Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller,
                     ; zero = equal)
 $A9E6 JR Z,$AA0B    ; If the two values are equal, skip ahead to #R$AA0B
 $A9E8 LD A,($C1F5)  ; Read the characters left on the line (#R$C1F5) into A -
                     ; Does the word fit?
 $A9EB CP L          ; Compare A with L
 $A9EC PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $A9ED JR NC,$A9F4   ; If A is L or more, skip ahead to #R$A9F4
 $A9EF LD A,$0D      ; Load A with $0D - No: new line first
 $A9F1 CALL $AA14    ; Call the routine "Send one character to the screen
                     ; layer" (#R$AA14)
*$A9F4 PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack - Send the word out
 $A9F5 LD B,L        ; Copy L into B
 $A9F6 LD HL,$5C0B   ; Point HL at the tape header workspace
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B))
 $A9F9 LD ($A9B5),HL ; Store HL in "Word buffer pointer for #R$A9B7" (#R$A9B5)
*$A9FC LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $A9FD CALL $AA14    ; Call the routine "Send one character to the screen
                     ; layer" (#R$AA14)
 $AA00 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $AA01 DJNZ $A9FC    ; Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back to
                     ; #R$A9FC
 $AA03 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $AA04 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $AA05 JR NZ,$AA0B   ; If the zero flag is clear, skip ahead to #R$AA0B
 $AA07 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $AA08 LD ($A025),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A025) in "Game variables"
*$AA0B POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack - ...then the character that
                     ; ended it
 $AA0C POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $AA0D POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $AA0E JR $AA14      ; Skip ahead to the routine "Send one character to the
                     ; screen layer" (#R$AA14)
*$AA10 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $AA11 JP $C1E1      ; Jump on to the routine "Print gatekeeper: check two
                     ; flags, then dispatch" (#R$C1E1)

; Send one character to the screen layer
;
; Letters become lower case; a space at the start of a line is dropped;
; carriage returns, quote marks and full stops get special handling (the rest:
; a full stop or quote mark makes the next letter a capital, and #R$A851 makes
; them all capitals).
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. A capital letter becomes lower case; everything else is passed on as it
; is.
; .
; 2. A space at the start of a line is dropped (#R$A022($A025) is zero until
; the first real character of the line).
; .
; 3. A carriage return starts a new line. A quote mark gets a space put in
; front of it. A quote mark or a full stop sets #R$A022($A026), so the next
; letter will be a capital.
; .
; 4. A lower-case letter becomes a capital if the all-capitals flag #R$A851 is
; set or #R$A022($A026) is set; #R$A022($A026) is cleared as it is used.
; .
; 5. Pass the character to the screen layer (#R$C1E1).
;
; Input:A The character
c$AA14 CP $41        ; Compare A with $41 - Capital letters become small ones
 $AA16 JR C,$AA1E    ; If A is below $41, skip ahead to #R$AA1E
 $AA18 CP $5B        ; Compare A with $5B
 $AA1A JR NC,$AA1E   ; If A is $5B or more, skip ahead to #R$AA1E
 $AA1C OR $20        ; Set bit 5 of A
*$AA1E PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $AA1F LD A,($A025)  ; Read #R$A022($A025) in "Game variables" into A -
                     ; #R$A022($A025) = 0 at the start of a line: a leading
                     ; space is dropped
 $AA22 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $AA23 JR NZ,$AA2A   ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$AA2A
 $AA25 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $AA26 CP $20        ; Compare A with $20
 $AA28 RET Z         ; If A equals $20, return
 $AA29 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
*$AA2A LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01
 $AA2C LD ($A025),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A025) in "Game variables"
 $AA2F POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $AA30 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $AA31 CP $0D        ; Compare A with $0D - A carriage return starts a new line
 $AA33 JR NZ,$AA3B   ; If A differs from $0D, skip ahead to #R$AA3B
 $AA35 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $AA36 LD ($A025),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A025) in "Game variables"
 $AA39 JR $AA6D      ; Jump on to #R$AA6D
*$AA3B CP $22        ; Compare A with $22 - A quote mark gets a space before
                     ; it,
 $AA3D JR NZ,$AA46   ; If A differs from $22, skip ahead to #R$AA46
 $AA3F LD A,$20      ; Load A with $20
 $AA41 CALL $C1E1    ; Call the routine "Print gatekeeper: check two flags,
                     ; then dispatch" (#R$C1E1)
 $AA44 JR $AA4A      ; Skip ahead to #R$AA4A
*$AA46 CP $2E        ; Compare A with $2E - and it, like a full stop, makes the
                     ; next letter a capital (#R$A022($A026))
 $AA48 JR NZ,$AA51   ; If A differs from $2E, skip ahead to #R$AA51
*$AA4A LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01
 $AA4C LD ($A026),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A026) in "Game variables"
 $AA4F JR $AA6D      ; Skip ahead to #R$AA6D
*$AA51 CP $61        ; Compare A with $61 - A letter: a capital if #R$A851 (all
                     ; capitals, code $1B) or #R$A022($A026) says so
 $AA53 JR C,$AA6D    ; If A is below $61, skip ahead to #R$AA6D
 $AA55 CP $7B        ; Compare A with $7B - A letter below $7B...
 $AA57 JR NC,$AA6D   ; If A is $7B or more, skip ahead to #R$AA6D
 $AA59 LD A,($A851)  ; Read "Code $1B: the all-capitals flag (read at #R$AA59)"
                     ; (#R$A851) into A - ...is capitalised if the capital flag
                     ; #R$A851 is set, or #R$A022($A026) (set after a full stop
                     ; and by some word flags)...
 $AA5C AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $AA5D JR NZ,$AA65   ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$AA65
 $AA5F LD A,($A026)  ; Read #R$A022($A026) in "Game variables" into A
 $AA62 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $AA63 JR Z,$AA6D    ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$AA6D
*$AA65 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - ...clearing
                     ; #R$A022($A026),
 $AA66 LD ($A026),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A026) in "Game variables"
 $AA69 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack - ...and its case bit is
                     ; dropped (AND $5F)
 $AA6A AND $5F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 of A (mask $5F)
 $AA6C PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
*$AA6D POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $AA6E JP $C1E1      ; Out through the screen layer (#R$C1E1)

; Shared action records, used through objects' own action lists
;
; 24 addresses of action records, ended by $0000. An object's own action list
; (see the entity records at #R$8CBA) maps a verb number to an index here;
; #R$C960 looks the index up when the target object has an entry for the verb
; being carried out. That lets every door share one OPEN and one CLOSE, every
; chair one SIT IN, and so on.
; .
; Which objects use which entries was read from all 110 entity records
; (objects.py); entries 1-3, 13-15 and 22 are not used by any object's action
; list and may be reached some other way - not yet found.
; .
; CORRECTED HISTORY: this table was first taken for the verb dispatch table
; (wrong - see #R$9F38), then 'TAKE is entry 5' was claimed and withdrawn.
; Entry 5 is in fact TAKE OFF.
; .
; People have action lists too, after a 00 entry in their records (see
; #R$C933): the local police man maps GO TO and GO FURIOUSLY TO to entries 1
; and 2, the cabbies TAKE TO, GO TO and GO FURIOUSLY TO to 13, 14 and 15,
; Tricia Fender has entry 3 and Mrs Jones has entry 22 - which is how the
; entries no object uses are reached.
w$AA71 DEFW $9A99                           ; Shared action 1: GO TO for the
                                            ; police cab (the local police
                                            ; man's own list)
 $AA73 DEFW $9ACB                           ; Shared action 2: GO FURIOUSLY TO
                                            ; for the police cab (the local
                                            ; police man's own list)
 $AA75 DEFW $E1A3                           ; Shared action 3: not used by any
                                            ; object list
 $AA77 DEFW $E022                           ; Shared action 4: WEAR (used by
                                            ; the China man's disguise)
 $AA79 DEFW $E003                           ; Shared action 5: TAKE OFF (used
                                            ; by the China man's disguise)
 $AA7B DEFW $DE9D                           ; Shared action 6: OPEN (used by
                                            ; every door)
 $AA7D DEFW $DEAF                           ; Shared action 7: CLOSE (used by
                                            ; every door)
 $AA7F DEFW $E432                           ; Shared action 8: GET OUT OF /
                                            ; CLIMB OUT OF (armchairs, sofa,
                                            ; cabs, trains)
 $AA81 DEFW $E40A                           ; Shared action 9: GET INTO / CLIMB
                                            ; INTO / SIT IN (armchairs, sofa,
                                            ; trains)
 $AA83 DEFW $E328                           ; Shared action 10: COUNT (the
                                            ; money objects)
 $AA85 DEFW $E2C7                           ; Shared action 11: HAIL / GET (the
                                            ; two hansom cabs)
 $AA87 DEFW $E3EA                           ; Shared action 12: GET INTO /
                                            ; CLIMB INTO a cab (reuses the SIT
                                            ; IN body)
 $AA89 DEFW $E08D                           ; Shared action 13: TAKE TO for a
                                            ; hansom cab (the cabbies' own
                                            ; lists)
 $AA8B DEFW $9AF4                           ; Shared action 14: GO TO for a
                                            ; hansom cab (the cabbies' own
                                            ; lists)
 $AA8D DEFW $9AE1                           ; Shared action 15: GO FURIOUSLY TO
                                            ; for a hansom cab (the cabbies'
                                            ; own lists)
 $AA8F DEFW $E0A0                           ; Shared action 16: EXAMINE CLOSELY
                                            ; the deep stream (verb
                                            ; #R$E135(20); CORRECTED, was
                                            ; "EXAMINE")
 $AA91 DEFW $E0F2                           ; Shared action 17: EXAMINE CLOSELY
                                            ; the drawer (verb #R$E135(20);
                                            ; CORRECTED, was "EXAMINE")
 $AA93 DEFW $E104                           ; Shared action 18: EXAMINE CLOSELY
                                            ; the bookcase (verb #R$E135(20);
                                            ; CORRECTED, was "EXAMINE")
 $AA95 DEFW $E0E0                           ; Shared action 19: EXAMINE CLOSELY
                                            ; the trash pile (verb #R$E135(20);
                                            ; CORRECTED, was "EXAMINE")
 $AA97 DEFW $DF7E                           ; Shared action 20: TAKE / GET /
                                            ; TAKE OUT OF the burnt note
 $AA99 DEFW $DFA7                           ; Shared action 21: OPEN a wall
                                            ; safe
 $AA9B DEFW $E17B                           ; Shared action 22: not used by any
                                            ; object list
 $AA9D DEFW $DFF0                           ; Shared action 23: WEAR (used by
                                            ; the old man's disguise)
 $AA9F DEFW $DFEA                           ; Shared action 24: TAKE OFF (used
                                            ; by the old man's disguise)
 $AAA1 DEFW $0000                           ; End marker
 $AAA3 DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00 ; {Unused (zeros)
 $AAAB DEFB $00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00,$00     ; }

; Parse the next sentence from the word list
;
; Called for the player's typed line (#R$D78E($D78F), part of the player's
; script step #R$D77E) and for the words of a remark (#R$B8BE($B8BF)). Sorts
; tokens into the parse record (IY) - the verb and its particles (#R$AB91),
; objects (#R$AC24) - and dispatches on punctuation: a full stop finishes the
; sentence (#R$AD57), a comma starts another object or sentence (#R$ACCC), a
; special word (class $90) goes to #R$AFB3. NZ if it does not make sense
; (#R$AF67). Read from the code; only its outline was traced.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Clear the sentence count and start with every kind of word allowed (E =
; $FF).
; .
; 2. Decide the sentence's mode (#R$A022($A024)). If a line of sentences is
; still being worked through it is an ordinary command (mode 1). Otherwise try
; to read a noun phrase first (#R$AC24): a sentence that opens with one and has
; no preposition is a statement such as MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER (mode 2,
; subject kept in the parse node); anything else is a command (mode 1) and
; reading starts again from the beginning.
; .
; 3. Read the verb and its particles (#R$AB91). Carry means the words make no
; sense.
; .
; 4. A special word (token class $90) goes to #R$AFB3. A comma ($A0) starts
; another sentence (#R$ACCC).
; .
; 5. Read the objects: each noun phrase (#R$AC24) is filed in the parse node
; (#R$ACA6), and this repeats as long as noun phrases follow.
; .
; 6. A full stop ($B0) completes the sentence (#R$AD57). In a statement, any
; other word must be a relation word (#R$ABF3), which ends the subject and
; carries on with the object.
; .
; 7. Anything else makes no sense: return NZ (#R$AF67).
;
;  Input:IY The parse node for the sentence
; Output:F NZ if the sentence makes no sense (A = 2 for a stray full stop)
c$AAB2 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - #R$A01A and the
                     ; sentence count #R$A012 start at 0
 $AAB3 LD ($A01A),A  ; Store A in #R$A01A in "Game variables"
 $AAB6 LD ($A012),A  ; Store A in #R$A012 in "Game variables"
 $AAB9 LD E,$FF      ; Load E with $FF - E: flags for what may come next; D:
                     ; the token class last read
