; Remember a choice (A) and the 3 bytes at $BA19
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$BA13($BA19).
;
;  Input:A The kind of match
; Output:$BA60 The kind
;        $BA61 1
;        $BA62 The three bytes from $ BA19
c$BB46 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack - Entry used at #R$BA7C: set
                     ; #R$BA60($BA61) = 1 and store the kind in #R$BA60
 $BB47 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01
 $BB49 LD ($BA61),A  ; Store A in #R$BA60($BA61) in "More variables for
                     ; #R$BA67-#R$BB5C"
 $BB4C POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $BB4D LD ($BA60),A  ; The choice
 $BB50 LD HL,$BA19   ; Point HL at #R$BA13($BA19) in "Variables used by the
                     ; code at #R$BA20-#R$BF58" - Copy 3 bytes to
                     ; #R$BA60($BA62)
 $BB53 LD DE,$BA62   ; Point DE at #R$BA60($BA62) in "More variables for
                     ; #R$BA67-#R$BB5C"
 $BB56 LD BC,$0003   ; Load BC with $0003
 $BB59 LDIR          ; Copy BC bytes from where HL points to where DE points,
                     ; counting up
 $BB5B RET           ; Return to the caller

; Jump to the handler of the record at ($BA65)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$BA60($BA65).
; .
; The handler address is the record's +3/+4.
;
;  Input:$BA65 The record found
; Output:Continues at the record's handler (+3/+4)
c$BB5C LD IX,($BA65) ; IX = the record found (#R$BA60($BA65))
 $BB60 LD L,(IX+$03) ; Read byte +3 of the record at IX into L - HL = its
                     ; handler at +3/+4
 $BB63 LD H,(IX+$04) ; Read byte +4 of the record at IX into H
 $BB66 JP (HL)       ; Jump to it

; Lestrade's reply to something the player says
;
; Parses the remark into a statement the same way as Watson's reply (#R$BA67)
; and checks it against the case table: a statement whose evidence is complete
; is proven (#R$BCC9) and Lestrade says so; otherwise it is marked as waiting
; and he says he does not believe the player has the evidence (#R$72A6,
; #R$72BD).
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator (see the case table at #R$9965): 'I do not believe
; that you have the evidence to prove that Mrs Brown killed herself' without
; the evidence, 'So Mrs Brown killed herself, excellent Holmes, another
; brilliant conclusion.' with it.
; .
; Line comments added from reading the code and cover the first part only (to
; #R$BC0E); which exact reply each address holds was not rendered.
; .
; Played, 9 November (a fresh replay of the winning route to Scotland Yard at
; 1:58pm on Tuesday, then sentences typed to Inspector Lestrade; the reply and
; the branch taken are given for each): MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF - 'So Mrs
; Brown killed herself, excellent Holmes, another brilliant conclusion'
; (#R$BBE5, the statement is proven); MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER -
; 'Brilliant Holmes, I do not know how you do it' and Mrs Jones is led away;
; BASIL KILLED MRS BROWN, BASIL HAS THE PLANS and THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL
; ROAD before the evidence is in - 'I do not believe that you have the evidence
; to prove that ...' (#R$BBC4, #R$72A6); TRICIA FENDER HAD PLANS - 'I do not
; see the importance of that'; BASIL KILLED WATSON, WATSON KILLED MRS BROWN,
; WHAT HAPPENED and THE PLANS ARE IN THE DRAWER - the stock reply 'That may
; well be, Holmes, I will think about it' (#R$BB87, #R$725F); a sentence with a
; word the game does not know is refused before it gets here (I DO NOT KNOW THE
; WORD). A plain HELLO does not come here at all: Lestrade answers 'Good
; afternoon' through #R$9A80.
; .
; Branches reached later the same day (tools/experiments/tails_test.py to
; tails_test6.py, same set-up: the player put in Lestrade's room, then remarks
; typed): BASIL KILLED - a statement that stops after the verb - goes to
; #R$BBBE and Lestrade says 'What' (#R$7243). MAJOR FFOULKES VISITED THE OPIUM
; DEN with the Major's confession already poked in (bit 1 of #R$996F($9972))
; and nothing else waiting goes through the summary at #R$BBC9, finds nothing
; unprovable and nothing newly provable (#R$BBD3), and Lestrade says 'Thats
; fine Holmes but what does it prove' (#R$727E at #R$BBDE). The same remark
; after TRICIA FENDER KILLED MRS JONES has been told (and left waiting) finds
; one newly provable statement (#R$BBD9): 'That is well and good, but I do not
; see how it proves that Tricia Fender killed Mrs Jones' (#R$72BD). After MAJOR
; FFOULKES IS INNOCENT is left waiting the same remark proves it and Lestrade
; says 'Very good Holmes, I shall return to Leather head tomorrow...', through
; #R$BBE5.
; .
; Still not seen: #R$BBFB-#R$BC17, which names a statement with flags bit 4
; that is still waiting. Only the four flags-$B4 statements (Tricia Fender or
; Phipps killed Mrs Brown or Mrs Jones) have that bit, and each has a child
; record that asks only that thing 0, the player, has been seen. They were
; always found provable, even in a run that cleared bit 7 of the player's +6
; before the check (#R$BBD9 was taken instead), so no sentence that can be
; typed has been found to reach it, and I have not worked out why the poke made
; no difference. Those 12 of the routine's 93 instructions are read from the
; code; the other 81 have run, 66 of them in the stored routes.
;
;  Input:IY The remark's sentence node
;        A 0
; Output:A reply printed
c$BB67 LD ($BA1D),A   ; Store A in #R$BA1B($BA1D) in "Variables used by the
                      ; code at #R$BA20-#R$BF58" - A = 0 on entry: clear
                      ; #R$BA1B($BA1D) and #R$BA1B($BA1C) (the count of
                      ; statements matched)
 $BB6A LD ($BA1C),A   ; Store A in #R$BA1B($BA1C) in "Variables used by the
                      ; code at #R$BA20-#R$BF58"
*$BB6D CALL $BD7E     ; Parse the next statement out of the remark (#R$BD7E)
 $BB70 JP C,$BBBE     ; Carry: no sense, reply #R$7243 (#R$BBBE)
 $BB73 CALL $BC21     ; Call #R$BC21 (inside "Lestrade's reply to something the
