; Player's turn: turn the sentence into a command (RUN at $993C)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$993C.
; .
; Part of the player's own script (see #R$993C): parses the sentence at #R$9FF2
; into a verb and objects (#R$B191) and copies them into the plan registers
; (#R$9FD6-#R$9FD8), where the script's DO picks them up. With ALL
; (#R$A01A($A01C), kept in #R$D6B8 between turns) the sentence is kept so that
; the next turn takes the next thing; otherwise its node is freed, with any BUT
; nodes after it. If the sentence does not parse, an ALL that has run out of
; things fails quietly (#R$DC15, with #R$A032($A033) = $FF); anything else sets
; #R$D7E0 so that the rest of the line is thrown away (#R$D7E1). Read from the
; code.
; .
; Tried 9 November: TAKE ALL (the 'ALL has run out' failure at #R$D865), TAKE
; ALL BUT LAMP (the BUT chain #R$D84F-#R$D856) and DROP ALL BUT LAMP both
; reached the paths that the stored routes do not. This record is one step of
; the loop in the player's own plan script at #R$9921: DO LOOK once, then read
; a line (#R$D6BF), split it into words (#R$D715), check there is a word
; (#R$D769), parse a sentence (#R$D77E), check there is a sentence (#R$D7D9),
; turn it into a command (#R$D7FA) and DO it. When you wear the china man's
; disguise the same script runs for him; the counts below include both.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 15,323 times in 14 of them, run by you,
; china man. Every one of its 53 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py plus the targeted tests described above).
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Carry the ALL flag over from the last turn and take the sentence at
; #R$9FF2.
; .
; 2. Parse it into a verb and objects (#R$B191). If that fails, set the failure
; flag and finish.
; .
; 3. Put the verb and objects in the plan registers (#R$9FD6, #R$9FD7), and
; note where the ALL search got to.
; .
; 4. If ALL is still going keep the sentence; otherwise unhook and free its
; node.
;
;  Input:$9FF2 The sentence
; Output:$9FD6 The verb
;        $9FD7 The objects
;        F Z if the sentence could not be made into a command
c$D7FA DEFB $00,$00   ; Action record header: flags $00; byte 1 $00
 $D7FC XOR A          ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $D7FD LD A,($D6B8)   ; Read "A flag of the player's script step, and spare..."
                      ; (#R$D6B8) into A - Carry the ALL flag over from the
                      ; last turn
 $D800 LD ($A01C),A   ; Store A in #R$A01A($A01C) in "Game variables"
 $D803 LD IY,($9FF2)  ; Read #R$9FF2 in "Game variables" into IY - IY = the
                      ; sentence; where the ALL search had got to (#R$D6BB ->
                      ; #R$B17D($B17E))
 $D807 LD HL,($D6BB)  ; Read #R$D6BB in "A flag of the player's script step,
                      ; and..." into HL
 $D80A LD ($B17E),HL  ; Store HL in #R$B17D($B17E) in "Parser variables"
 $D80D CALL $B191     ; Parse it into a verb and objects (#R$B191)
 $D810 LD A,$01       ; Load A with $01 - (failures now count: #R$A00A($A00B) =
                      ; 1)
 $D812 LD ($A00B),A   ; Store A in #R$A00A($A00B) in "Game variables"
 $D815 JP Z,$D85B     ; If the zero flag is set, jump on to #R$D85B
 $D818 LD A,($A01C)   ; Read #R$A01A($A01C) in "Game variables" into A -
                      ; Parsed: keep the ALL flag for next turn
 $D81B LD ($D6B8),A   ; Store A in "A flag of the player's script step, and
                      ; spare..." (#R$D6B8)
 $D81E LD A,$01       ; Load A with $01 - The next letter printed is a capital
                      ; (#R$A022($A026))
 $D820 LD ($A026),A   ; Store A in #R$A022($A026) in "Game variables"
 $D823 LD A,($A033)   ; Read #R$A032($A033) in "Game variables" into A - The
                      ; verb and objects into the plan registers
 $D826 LD ($9FD6),A   ; Store A in the plan's verb (#R$9FD6)
 $D829 LD HL,($A034)  ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into HL
 $D82C LD ($9FD7),HL  ; Store HL in the plan's direct object (#R$9FD7)
 $D82F LD HL,($B17E)  ; Read #R$B17D($B17E) in "Parser variables" into HL -
                      ; Note where the ALL search got to
 $D832 LD ($D6BB),HL  ; Store HL in #R$D6BB in "A flag of the player's script
                      ; step, and..."
*$D835 LD A,($D6B8)   ; Read "A flag of the player's script step, and spare..."
                      ; (#R$D6B8) into A - ALL still going: keep the sentence
 $D838 AND A          ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                      ; carry)
 $D839 JR NZ,$D858    ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$D858
 $D83B LD IX,($9FF2)  ; Read #R$9FF2 in "Game variables" into IX - Otherwise
                      ; unhook and free its node...
*$D83F LD L,(IX-$02)  ; Read byte -2 of the record at IX into L
 $D842 LD H,(IX-$01)  ; Read byte -1 of the record at IX into H
 $D845 LD ($9FF2),HL  ; Store HL in #R$9FF2 in "Game variables"
 $D848 CALL $A1C7     ; Call the routine "Free node IX back to the heap"
                      ; (#R$A1C7)
 $D84B LD A,L         ; Copy L into A
 $D84C OR H           ; Set in A the bits that are set in H
 $D84D JR Z,$D858     ; If the result is zero, skip ahead to #R$D858
 $D84F PUSH HL        ; Save HL on the stack - ...and any nodes after it marked
                      ; with bit 6 (BUT)
 $D850 POP IX         ; Restore IX from the stack
 $D852 BIT 6,(IX+$01) ; Test bit 6 of byte +1 of the record at IX
 $D856 JR NZ,$D83F    ; If bit 6 of byte +1 of the record at IX is set, go back
                      ; to #R$D83F
*$D858 OR $01         ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                      ; that back to the caller - Succeed
 $D85A RET            ; Return to the caller
*$D85B XOR A          ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Did not parse:
                      ; the ALL is over
 $D85C LD ($D6B8),A   ; Store A in "A flag of the player's script step, and
                      ; spare..." (#R$D6B8)
 $D85F LD A,($A01C)   ; Read #R$A01A($A01C) in "Game variables" into A - It was
                      ; an ALL: no verb (#R$A032($A033) = $FF), drop the
                      ; sentence and fail quietly
 $D862 AND A          ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                      ; carry)
 $D863 JR Z,$D870     ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$D870
 $D865 LD A,$FF       ; Load A with $FF
 $D867 LD ($A033),A   ; Store A in #R$A032($A033) in "Game variables"
 $D86A CALL $D835     ; Call #R$D835 (inside "Player's turn: turn the sentence
                      ; into a...")
 $D86D JP $DC15       ; Jump on to the routine "Fail: A = 0, HL = 0, Z set"
                      ; (#R$DC15)
*$D870 LD A,$01       ; Load A with $01 - Otherwise drop the rest of the line
                      ; (#R$D7E0) and end the word list there
 $D872 LD ($D7E0),A   ; Store A in "A flag for #R$D7E1" (#R$D7E0)
 $D875 PUSH HL        ; Save HL on the stack
 $D876 LD HL,($D6BD)  ; Read #R$D6BD in "A flag of the player's script step,
                      ; and..." into HL
 $D879 LD (HL),$C0    ; Store $C0 at the address HL points to
 $D87B POP HL         ; Restore HL from the stack
 $D87C RET            ; Return to the caller

; Free the current sentence's word list ($9FF0)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$9FF0.
; .
; Clears the pointer and hands the list to #R$A1AF. Read from the code.
;
; Output:$9FF0 Empty; its nodes freed
c$D87D LD IX,($9FF0) ; Read words the player has (#R$9FF0) into IX - The
                     ; sentence list (#R$9FF0) becomes empty; free all its
                     ; nodes (#R$A1AF)
 $D881 LD HL,$0000   ; Load HL with $0000
 $D884 LD ($9FF0),HL ; Store HL in words the player has (#R$9FF0)
 $D887 JP $A1AF      ; Go back to the routine "Free every node of the list
                     ; starting at IX" (#R$A1AF)

; Free the rest of the typed line's word list ($9FF2)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$9FF2.
; .
; As #R$D87D.
;
; Output:$9FF2 Empty; its nodes freed
c$D88A LD IX,($9FF2) ; Read #R$9FF2 in "Game variables" into IX - The same for
                     ; the rest of the line (#R$9FF2)
 $D88E LD HL,$0000   ; Load HL with $0000
 $D891 LD ($9FF2),HL ; Store HL in #R$9FF2 in "Game variables"
 $D894 JP $A1AF      ; Go back to the routine "Free every node of the list
                     ; starting at IX" (#R$A1AF)

; The line editor's inside-quotes flag
;
; 1 outside quotation marks, 0 inside: set to 1 at the start of each line
; (#R$D8BE) and flipped by each quote mark typed or rubbed out (#R$D8F6,
; #R$D908). At ENTER an open quotation is closed for you (#R$D949). CORRECTED:
; was taken for a NOP at the head of #R$D898.
b$D897 DEFB $00      ; Flag

; Read a line from the keyboard, with a time limit
;
; The pre-check of the player's first script step (#R$D6BF). Sets the limit
; (#R$A03A($A03B) = 3,000 polls), switches quoted printing on while the line is
; typed, and runs the line editor (#R$D8AD). If the player stops typing for too
; long the editor gives up, the step fails, and the player's script does WAIT
; HERE instead (#R$9948) - so the clock and the other characters carry on while
; the player thinks. Read from the code.
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator: leaving the prompt with no key pressed (the
; harness answers every keyboard poll with 'no key') gives '> You wait.' and
; the clock runs on from 8:02 to 8:05, then again to 8:10, with no command
; typed.
;
; Output:F Z if nothing was typed in time; NZ if a line was typed
c$D898 LD HL,$0BB8   ; Load HL with $0BB8 - The time limit (#R$A03A($A03B)) =
                     ; $0BB8 = 3000
 $D89B LD ($A03B),HL ; Store HL in #R$A03A($A03B) in "Game variables"
 $D89E LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - #R$A022($A027) = 1 while the line is
                     ; read (#R$D8AD)...
 $D8A0 LD ($A027),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A027) in "Game variables"
 $D8A3 CALL $D8AD    ; Call the routine "The line editor" (#R$D8AD)
 $D8A6 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $D8A7 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $D8A8 LD ($A027),A  ; Store A in #R$A022($A027) in "Game variables" - ...and 0
                     ; afterwards, keeping the result
 $D8AB POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $D8AC RET           ; Return to the caller

; The line editor
;
; Prints the '> ' prompt and reads up to 128 characters into
; #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00) through #R$A536: DELETE rubs out (and keeps
; track of quote marks, #R$D897), $18 clears the line (#R$D9B7), letters,
; digits and a few punctuation marks are accepted, ENTER ends the line
; (#R$D949). A time-out from #R$A536 ends it with nothing (#R$D9AC). Read from
; the code.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Print the prompt '> ' and set up the buffer at
; #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00); B counts down from 128, with bit 7 set while
; nothing has been typed.
; .
; 2. Wait for a key with the time limit (#R$A536). If time runs out, give up:
; the player will wait.
; .
; 3. On an empty line, '@' repeats the last command (#R$D99E). The first key of
; a line may be a cursor key, which is turned into a one-key move (#R$D978).
; .
; 4. Rub-out ($18) clears the whole line; DELETE removes one character, and
; removing a quote mark flips the inside-quotes flag.
; .
; 5. Letters are taken. A quote mark flips the flag (closing a quotation first
; ends it with a full stop). Space, full stop, comma, slash, the pound sign,
; hyphen, colon and digits are taken. Anything else is ignored.
; .
; 6. ENTER closes any open quotation, ends the line with a full stop (#R$D968)
; and a carriage return, and returns NZ.
;
; Output:F NZ if a line was typed, Z if the wait timed out
;        HL The end of the line in the buffer at $5B00
c$D8AD LD A,$3E      ; Load A with $3E - Print the prompt '> '
 $D8AF CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D8B2 LD A,$20      ; Load A with $20
 $D8B4 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D8B7 LD HL,$5B00   ; Load HL with #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00) - The line
                     ; goes to #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00); B counts down
                     ; from $80 (bit 7 set: nothing typed yet)
 $D8BA LD B,$80      ; Load B with $80
*$D8BC LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - #R$D897 = 1: not inside quotes; C = 0:
                     ; no key yet
 $D8BE LD ($D897),A  ; Store A in "The line editor's inside-quotes flag"
                     ; (#R$D897)
 $D8C1 LD C,$00      ; Load C with $00
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$D99E.
*$D8C3 CALL $A536    ; Call the routine "Wait for a key, but not for ever"
                     ; (#R$A536) - Wait for a key, with the time limit
                     ; (#R$A536); time up: give up the line
 $D8C6 JP Z,$D9AC    ; If the zero flag is set, jump on to #R$D9AC
 $D8C9 BIT 7,B       ; Test bit 7 of B - On an empty line '@' repeats the last
                     ; command (#R$D99E)
 $D8CB JR Z,$D8D2    ; If bit 7 of B is clear, skip ahead to #R$D8D2
 $D8CD CP $40        ; Compare A with $40
 $D8CF JP Z,$D99E    ; If A equals $40, jump on to the routine "The '@' key on
                     ; an empty line: do the last command..." (#R$D99E)
*$D8D2 BIT 0,C       ; Test bit 0 of C - The first key of a line may be a
                     ; cursor key: a one-key move (#R$D978)
 $D8D4 CALL Z,$D978  ; If bit 0 of C is clear, call the routine "A cursor key
                     ; on an empty line is a compass move" (#R$D978)
 $D8D7 LD C,$01      ; Load C with $01
 $D8D9 CP $18        ; Compare A with $18 - $18 ('_', made so by GET-KEY): rub
                     ; out the whole line and start again
 $D8DB JR NZ,$D8E2   ; If A differs from $18, skip ahead to #R$D8E2
 $D8DD CALL $D9B7    ; Call the routine "Rub out the typed line" (#R$D9B7)
 $D8E0 JR $D8BC      ; Go back to #R$D8BC
*$D8E2 CP $08        ; Compare A with $08 - $08 (DELETE): back one character,
                     ; unless the line is empty
 $D8E4 JR NZ,$D900   ; If A differs from $08, skip ahead to #R$D900
 $D8E6 BIT 7,B       ; Test bit 7 of B
 $D8E8 JR NZ,$D8C3   ; If bit 7 of B is set, go back to #R$D8C3
 $D8EA LD A,$08      ; Load A with $08
 $D8EC CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D8EF INC B         ; Add one to B
 $D8F0 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $D8F1 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - Rubbing out a quote
                     ; mark flips the inside-quotes flag back
 $D8F2 CP $22        ; Compare A with $22
 $D8F4 JR NZ,$D8C3   ; If A differs from $22, go back to #R$D8C3
 $D8F6 LD A,($D897)  ; Read "The line editor's inside-quotes flag" (#R$D897)
                     ; into A
 $D8F9 XOR $01       ; Flip in A the bits that are set in $01
 $D8FB LD ($D897),A  ; Store A in "The line editor's inside-quotes flag"
                     ; (#R$D897)
 $D8FE JR $D8C3      ; Go back to #R$D8C3
*$D900 CP $40        ; Compare A with $40 - Letters ($40 up) are always taken
 $D902 JR NC,$D93C   ; If A is $40 or more, jump on to #R$D93C
 $D904 CP $22        ; Compare A with $22 - A quote mark flips the flag;
                     ; closing a quotation first ends it with a full stop
                     ; (#R$D968)
 $D906 JR NZ,$D917   ; If A differs from $22, skip ahead to #R$D917
 $D908 LD A,($D897)  ; Read "The line editor's inside-quotes flag" (#R$D897)
                     ; into A
 $D90B XOR $01       ; Flip in A the bits that are set in $01
 $D90D LD ($D897),A  ; Store A in "The line editor's inside-quotes flag"
                     ; (#R$D897)
 $D910 CALL NZ,$D968 ; If the result is not zero, call the routine "End the
                     ; typed line with a full stop if it has none" (#R$D968)
 $D913 LD A,$22      ; Load A with $22
 $D915 JR $D93C      ; Skip ahead to #R$D93C
*$D917 CP $20        ; Compare A with $20 - Space, ENTER, . , / ` - : are
                     ; taken...
 $D919 JR Z,$D93C    ; If A equals $20, skip ahead to #R$D93C
 $D91B CP $0D        ; Compare A with $0D - Carriage return: end of line
                     ; (#R$D949)
 $D91D JR Z,$D949    ; If A equals $0D, jump on to #R$D949
 $D91F CP $2E        ; Compare A with $2E - A '.' ends a word (#R$D93C)...
 $D921 JR Z,$D93C    ; If A equals $2E, skip ahead to #R$D93C
 $D923 CP $2C        ; Compare A with $2C - ...and so do ','...
 $D925 JR Z,$D93C    ; If A equals $2C, skip ahead to #R$D93C
 $D927 CP $2F        ; Compare A with $2F
 $D929 JR Z,$D93C    ; If A equals $2F, skip ahead to #R$D93C
 $D92B CP $60        ; Compare A with $60 - ...the pound sign ($60)...
 $D92D JR Z,$D93C    ; If A equals $60, skip ahead to #R$D93C
 $D92F CP $2D        ; Compare A with $2D
 $D931 JR Z,$D93C    ; If A equals $2D, skip ahead to #R$D93C
 $D933 CP $3A        ; Compare A with $3A - ...and ':'
 $D935 JR Z,$D93C    ; If A equals $3A, skip ahead to #R$D93C
 $D937 CALL $DAE8    ; Call the routine "Is A a digit? (carry if not)"
                     ; (#R$DAE8) - ...and digits (#R$DAE8); anything else is
                     ; ignored
 $D93A JR C,$D8C3    ; If the carry flag is set, go back to #R$D8C3
*$D93C DEC B         ; Subtract one from B - Store and print it, if the
                     ; 128-character line has room
 $D93D INC B         ; Add one to B
 $D93E JR Z,$D946    ; If B has become zero, skip ahead to #R$D946
 $D940 LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to
 $D941 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D944 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $D945 DEC B         ; Subtract one from B
*$D946 JP $D8C3      ; Go back to #R$D8C3
*$D949 LD A,($D897)  ; Read "The line editor's inside-quotes flag" (#R$D897)
                     ; into A - ENTER: close an open quotation (#R$D95C),
 $D94C AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $D94D CALL Z,$D95C  ; If A is zero, call the routine "Close a quotation: a
                     ; full stop if needed, then the..." (#R$D95C)
 $D950 CALL $D968    ; Call the routine "End the typed line with a full stop if
                     ; it has none" (#R$D968) - end with a full stop (#R$D968)
                     ; and a carriage return
 $D953 LD A,$0D      ; Load A with $0D
 $D955 LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to
 $D956 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D959 OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller - NZ: a line was typed
 $D95B RET           ; Return to the caller

; Close a quotation: a full stop if needed, then the closing quote mark
;
; Puts '.' (#R$D968) and then '"' into the line buffer at HL, printing each.
; Read from the code.
;
;  Input:HL The end of the line buffer
;        B The count
; Output:HL Past the closing quote
;        B One fewer
c$D95C CALL $D968    ; (#R$D968)
 $D95F LD A,$22      ; A closing quote mark...
 $D961 LD (HL),A     ; ...stored at HL
 $D962 CALL $A9B7    ; ...and printed
 $D965 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte - HL on, B down
 $D966 DEC B         ; Subtract one from B
 $D967 RET           ; Return to the caller

; End the typed line with a full stop if it has none
;
; Read from the code.
;
;  Input:HL Past the last character typed
;        B The count
; Output:HL Past the end
;        B One fewer if a full stop was added
c$D968 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte - The last character typed: a full
                     ; stop? Then done
 $D969 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $D96A CP $2E        ; Compare A with $2E
 $D96C JR Z,$D976    ; If A equals $2E, skip ahead to #R$D976
 $D96E INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte - Else put one after it,
                     ; count it, and echo it (#R$A9B7)
 $D96F DEC B         ; Subtract one from B
 $D970 LD A,$2E      ; Load A with $2E
 $D972 LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to
 $D973 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
*$D976 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte - Step HL past the end
 $D977 RET           ; Return to the caller

; A cursor key on an empty line is a compass move
;
; Key codes $08, $09, $0A and '[' become W, E, S and N - stored in the line,
; printed, and followed at once by ENTER. GET-KEY (#R$C41F) turns cursor-up
; ($0B) into '[' and DELETE ($0C) into $08, so on a Spectrum CAPS SHIFT with 5,
; 6, 7 or 8 (or DELETE) walks west, south, north or east with one key.
; Confirmed in the emulator: after OPEN PLAIN DOOR, the single key '[' gave 'N.
; You go north. You are in a small closet...', $0A gave 'S. You go south.', $08
; gave 'W. YOU CANNOT GO WEST.'
;
;  Input:A The key
;        HL The line buffer
; Output:A $0D (ENTER) with N, E, S or W typed for a cursor key; the key itself
; .        for any other
;        HL Moved on
;        B One fewer
c$D978 CP $09        ; Compare A with $09 - Cursor right: E
 $D97A JR Z,$D996    ; If A equals $09, skip ahead to #R$D996
 $D97C CP $08        ; Compare A with $08 - Cursor left (or DELETE, made $08 by
                     ; GET-KEY): W
 $D97E JR Z,$D99A    ; If A equals $08, skip ahead to #R$D99A
 $D980 CP $0A        ; Compare A with $0A - Cursor down: S
 $D982 JR Z,$D992    ; If A equals $0A, skip ahead to #R$D992
 $D984 CP $5B        ; Compare A with $5B - Cursor up (made '[' by GET-KEY): N;
                     ; any other key: back
 $D986 RET NZ        ; If A differs from $5B, return
 $D987 LD A,$4E      ; Load A with $4E
*$D989 LD (HL),A     ; Store A at the address HL points to - Put the letter in
                     ; the line and print it
 $D98A INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $D98B CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D98E DEC B         ; One more character in the line
 $D98F LD A,$0D      ; Load A with $0D - and press ENTER
 $D991 RET           ; Return to the caller
*$D992 LD A,$53      ; Load A with $53
 $D994 JR $D989      ; Go back to #R$D989
*$D996 LD A,$45      ; Load A with $45
 $D998 JR $D989      ; Go back to #R$D989
*$D99A LD A,$57      ; Load A with $57
 $D99C JR $D989      ; Go back to #R$D989

; The '@' key on an empty line: do the last command again
;
; Reached from the line editor (#R$D8CF) when '@' is typed on an empty line.
; Unless a typed line still has sentences waiting (#R$A01A($A01B), when the key
; is ignored), sets #R$D714 and ends the input. The word-list builder at
; #R$D715 sees #R$D714 and, instead of tokenizing the line again, reuses the
; tokens already at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B81)($5B81) - the previous command's.
; (#R$D9AC, shared with the time-out path, prints two backspaces.) Confirmed in
; the emulator: after NORTH into the closet, '@' gave 'YOU CANNOT GO NORTH.'
; twice.
;
; Output:F NZ with $D714 set to 1, unless something has already been typed
;        A The key
c$D99E LD A,($A01B)  ; Read #R$A01A($A01B) in "Game variables" into A -
                     ; Anything already typed (#R$A01A($A01B) set)? Then '@' is
                     ; an ordinary character (#R$D8C3)
 $D9A1 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $D9A2 JP NZ,$D8C3   ; If A is not zero, go back to #R$D8C3
 $D9A5 LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01 - #R$D714 = 1: use the last word list
                     ; again
 $D9A7 LD ($D714),A  ; Store A in "Flag: repeat the last command ('@')"
                     ; (#R$D714)
 $D9AA OR $01        ; Set bit 0 of A
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$D8AD.
*$D9AC PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack - Rub out the '@' just printed: two
                     ; backspaces ($08)
 $D9AD LD A,$08      ; Load A with $08
 $D9AF CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D9B2 CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D9B5 POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $D9B6 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Rub out the typed line
;
; Prints a backspace ($08), steps HL back and counts B up, until B reaches $80
; (at once if bit 7 is already set). Read from the code; it is what #R$A536
; uses to wipe an unfinished line when the time limit runs out.
;
;  Input:HL The line end
;        B The count (bit 7 set means finished)
; Output:HL Back to the start
;        B Up to $80
c$D9B7 BIT 7,B       ; Test bit 7 of B - B bit 7 set: finished
 $D9B9 RET NZ        ; If bit 7 of B is set, return
 $D9BA LD A,$08      ; Load A with $08 - Print a backspace (8) (#R$A9B7)
 $D9BC CALL $A9B7    ; Call the routine "Print character A, a word at a time
                     ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)
 $D9BF INC B         ; Add one to B - B up, HL back: repeat
 $D9C0 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $D9C1 JR $D9B7      ; Go back to the routine "Rub out the typed line"
                     ; (#R$D9B7)

; The tokenizer's current dictionary candidate
;
; The address of the dictionary record being compared, stored at #R$D9E4 for
; each candidate and read back at #R$DA22 when the best one has been chosen.
; CORRECTED: was 'not traced'. Read from the code.
b$D9C3 DEFW $0000    ; The dictionary record being compared

; Tokenizer: read and classify the next word from input
;
; Confirmed, by single-stepping through a real call while typing a real command
; (see dictionary_notes.md), to be the tokenizer's genuine entry point: skips
; leading spaces, checks for an empty line or a special character, then (for an
; ordinary word) calls the per-letter dictionary index lookup (#R$DA34), the
; word-expansion machinery (#R$A94D), and the comparison routine (#R$DA7D) in
; turn, repeating for each candidate word stored under the typed word's first
; letter.
; .
; Abbreviations, tried in the emulator: EX LAMP, EXA LAMP and EXAM LAMP all
; examine the oil lamp, EXAMINE LAM too, and INVE takes stock; but EXAMINE LA
; finds the LABEL and EXAMINE L, TA LAMP and T LAMP give 'WHAT ?', while words
; with letters added (EXAMINATION, EXAMINEXYZ) are not recognised. So a typed
; word is matched as the start of a dictionary word (#R$DA7D counts the letters
; that agree, keeping the best in #R$DC13($DC14); the typed length is #R$DC13),
; the first word under its letter winning a tie.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Skip spaces and remember where the word starts (#R$A022($A029)).
; .
; 2. A carriage return gives an end-of-line token ($C0).
; .
; 3. Characters that are not letters (the pound sign, digits, full stop, comma,
; quote) are read as their own tokens (#R$DAC6).
; .
; 4. Otherwise look up the dictionary words under the first letter (#R$DA34),
; spell each one out (#R$A94D) and compare it with the typed word (#R$DA7D),
; keeping the best match.
; .
; 5. A word that matches returns its class and word reference; no match gives a
; $D0 token.
;
;  Input:HL The typed line
; Output:A, D The token class ($C0 end of line, $D0 not understood, $F0 number,
; .         and so on)
;        BC The token's value
;        HL Past the word
;        $A029 Where the word started
c$D9C5 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
*$D9C6 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - Skip spaces
 $D9C7 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $D9C8 CP $20        ; Compare A with $20
 $D9CA JR Z,$D9C6    ; If A equals $20, go back to #R$D9C6
 $D9CC DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $D9CD LD ($A029),HL ; Remember where this word starts (#R$A022($A029))
 $D9D0 CP $0D        ; Compare A with $0D - End of the line: a $C0 token
 $D9D2 JR Z,$DA06    ; If A equals $0D, jump on to #R$DA06
 $D9D4 CALL $DAC6    ; Call the routine "Read a token that is not a word:
                     ; money, a number or..." (#R$DAC6) - A special character
                     ; (#R$DAC6)? Its own token
 $D9D7 JR Z,$DA2F    ; If the zero flag is set, jump on to #R$DA2F
 $D9D9 CALL $DA34    ; Call the routine "Look up this word's first letter in
                     ; the dictionary..." (#R$DA34) - Find the dictionary words
                     ; under its first letter (#R$DA34); none: a $D0 token
                     ; (unknown word)
 $D9DC JR Z,$DA0A    ; If the zero flag is set, jump on to #R$DA0A
 $D9DE PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $D9E0 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $D9E1 LD ($DC14),A  ; Best match so far: none
*$D9E4 LD ($D9C3),DE ; Store DE in "The tokenizer's current dictionary
                     ; candidate" (#R$D9C3) - Next candidate: spell it out
                     ; (#R$A94D)
*$D9E8 LD DE,($DA32) ; Read "The tokenizer's word pointer" (#R$DA32) into DE
 $D9EC AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $D9ED PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $D9EE SBC HL,DE     ; Subtract DE and the carry from HL
 $D9F0 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $D9F1 JR Z,$DA12    ; If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$DA12
 $D9F3 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $D9F4 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $D9F5 CALL $A94D    ; Call the routine "Spell dictionary record HL into the
                     ; buffer at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42)" (#R$A94D)
 $D9F8 LD A,(DE)     ; Read the byte DE points at into A - Skip records of
                     ; class $D0
 $D9F9 AND $F0       ; Keep only bits 4, 5, 6, 7 of A (mask $F0)
 $D9FB CP $D0        ; Compare A with $D0
 $D9FD JR Z,$D9E8    ; If A equals $D0, go back to #R$D9E8
 $D9FF CALL $DA7D    ; Call the routine "Compare the typed word against one
                     ; dictionary..." (#R$DA7D) - Compare it with the typed
                     ; word (#R$DA7D); Z at the end of this letter's words
 $DA02 JR Z,$DA12    ; If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$DA12
 $DA04 JR $D9E4      ; Go back to #R$D9E4
*$DA06 LD A,$C0      ; Load A with $C0 - $C0: end of the line
 $DA08 JR $DA0C      ; Skip ahead to #R$DA0C
*$DA0A LD A,$D0      ; Load A with $D0 - $D0: not a word the game knows
*$DA0C LD BC,$0000   ; Load BC with $0000
 $DA0F POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $DA10 LD D,A        ; Copy A into D
 $DA11 RET           ; Return to the caller
*$DA12 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack - Fewer than four letters
                     ; agree: they must be all the letters typed
 $DA13 LD A,($DC14)  ; Read #R$DC13($DC14) in "The number reader's working
                     ; bytes" into A
 $DA16 CP $04        ; Compare A with $04
 $DA18 JR NC,$DA21   ; If A is $04 or more, skip ahead to #R$DA21
 $DA1A LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $DA1B LD A,($DC13)  ; Read "The number reader's working bytes" (#R$DC13) into
                     ; A
 $DA1E CP B          ; Compare A with B
 $DA1F JR NZ,$DA0A   ; If A differs from B, go back to #R$DA0A
*$DA21 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - BC = the chosen word's offset
                     ; from the dictionary base #R$5DBE($5DBF), A = its class
 $DA22 LD HL,($D9C3) ; Read "The tokenizer's current dictionary candidate"
                     ; (#R$D9C3) into HL
 $DA25 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $DA26 AND $F0       ; Keep only bits 4, 5, 6, 7 of A (mask $F0)
 $DA28 LD BC,$5DBF   ; Point BC at #R$5DBE($5DBF)
 $DA2B SBC HL,BC     ; Subtract BC and the carry from HL
 $DA2D EX (SP),HL    ; Swap HL with the word on top of the stack
 $DA2E POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
*$DA2F POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $DA30 LD D,A        ; Copy A into D
 $DA31 RET           ; Return to the caller

; The tokenizer's word pointer
;
; Stored by #R$DA54($DA55) (LD (#R$DA32),HL). Read from the code.
b$DA32 DEFB $00,$00  ; The tokenizer's word pointer

; Look up this word's first letter in the dictionary index
;
; Takes the current word's first character directly from the input buffer,
; masks it to 5 bits (giving 1-26 for any letter without needing a separate
; packing step - 'A' is $41, whose low 5 bits are 1; 'Z' is $5A, whose low 5
; bits are 26), and uses that to index a 26-entry table at #R$5D80 of 16-bit
; offsets, relative to a second base at #R$5DBE($5DBF), to find the first
; candidate word stored under that letter.
; .
; Confirmed real and correctly understood since early in this project (the
; table's entries are clean and monotonically increasing across all 26
; letters), but its connection to real gameplay wasn't confirmed until much
; later - see dictionary_notes.md for the single-step trace that finally
; confirmed this is genuinely on the path a real typed command takes, not
; merely a plausible-looking table sitting unused.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. The first letter (A = 1 to Z = 26) indexes the table of offsets at
; #R$5D80.
; .
; 2. DE is where that letter's words start in the dictionary. The next letter
; that has words marks where they end (#R$DA32).
; .
; 3. Return Z if there are no words under the letter.
;
;  Input:HL The word
; Output:DE The first dictionary record for the letter
;        $DA32 Where its words end
;        F Z if there are none
c$DA34 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - The first letter (A
                     ; = 1 ... Z = 26) indexes the table of offsets at #R$5D80
 $DA35 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DA36 AND $1F       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 of A (mask $1F)
 $DA38 RLCA          ; Rotate A left one place (bit 7 goes to carry and bit 0)
 $DA39 LD E,A        ; Copy A into E
 $DA3A LD D,$00      ; Load D with $00
 $DA3C PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $DA3D POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $DA3F LD HL,$5D80   ; Point HL at "Dictionary index: where each letter's words
                     ; start" (#R$5D80)
 $DA42 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $DA43 LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E - DE = where this
                     ; letter's words start in the dictionary
 $DA44 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DA45 LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $DA46 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DA47 PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
*$DA48 LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E - The next letter that
                     ; has words marks where they end (#R$DA32)
 $DA49 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DA4A LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $DA4B INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DA4C LD A,D        ; Copy D into A
 $DA4D OR E          ; Set in A the bits that are set in E
 $DA4E JR Z,$DA48    ; If the result is zero, go back to #R$DA48
 $DA50 LD HL,$5DBF   ; Point HL at #R$5DBE($5DBF)
 $DA53 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $DA54 LD ($DA32),HL ; Store HL in "The tokenizer's word pointer" (#R$DA32)
 $DA57 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $DA58 LD A,D        ; Copy D into A - No words under this letter: Z
 $DA59 OR E          ; Set in A the bits that are set in E
 $DA5A RET Z         ; If the result is zero, return
 $DA5B LD HL,$5DBF   ; Point HL at #R$5DBE($5DBF)
 $DA5E ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $DA5F LD DE,$5C0B   ; Point DE at the tape header workspace
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B)) - Copy the typed word
                     ; to #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B) in lower case, as the
                     ; dictionary spells
 $DA62 LD B,$01      ; Load B with $01
*$DA64 LD A,(IX+$00) ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into A
 $DA67 CP $40        ; Compare A with $40
 $DA69 JR C,$DA74    ; If A is below $40, skip ahead to #R$DA74
 $DA6B OR $20        ; Set bit 5 of A
 $DA6D LD (DE),A     ; Store A at the address DE points to
 $DA6E INC DE        ; Step DE on to the next byte
 $DA6F INC IX        ; Step IX on to the next byte
 $DA71 INC B         ; Add one to B
 $DA72 JR $DA64      ; Go back to #R$DA64
*$DA74 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - End it with 0;
                     ; #R$DC13 = its length + 1
 $DA75 LD (DE),A     ; Store A at the address DE points to
 $DA76 LD A,B        ; Copy B into A
 $DA77 LD ($DC13),A  ; Store A in "The number reader's working bytes" (#R$DC13)
 $DA7A OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $DA7C RET           ; Return to the caller

; Compare the typed word against one dictionary candidate
;
; Compares the typed word (already expanded/normalised into a scratch buffer)
; against one candidate's own expanded spelling, up to the length of whichever
; is shorter - i.e. genuine abbreviation/prefix matching, not requiring an
; exact full-length match. On a match, also follows a synonym chain (a bit in
; the next word's own flag byte marks 'this is the last synonym in the group';
; otherwise a 16-bit offset follows, pointing at another word to also accept as
; equivalent).
; .
; CONFIRMED WITH REAL DATA, correcting an earlier, wrongly-abandoned conclusion
; in this project (see dictionary_notes.md): breakpointing this exact address
; while typing TAKE shows the typed buffer holding 'ake' (the leading 't'
; already consumed by the per-letter index lookup above) compared in turn
; against three expanded dictionary candidates - 'able', 'akeS', 'alkS' -
; almost certainly TABLE, TAKES and TALKS. 'ake' against 'akeS' is exactly a
; prefix match, real evidence (not just a plausible story) that this
; comparison, and the word-expansion machinery that produced 'akeS' from a
; stored stem plus a suffix-table entry, are genuinely how the game recognises
; typed words.
;
;  Input:$5C0B The typed word
;        $5C42 The candidate word
; Output:The best match so far updated ($DC14)
c$DA7D PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $DA7E PUSH DE       ; Save DE on the stack
 $DA7F LD A,($DC13)  ; Read "The number reader's working bytes" (#R$DC13) into
                     ; A - Already matched every typed letter with an earlier
                     ; word: skip this one
 $DA82 LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $DA83 LD A,($DC14)  ; Read #R$DC13($DC14) in "The number reader's working
                     ; bytes" into A
 $DA86 CP B          ; Compare A with B
 $DA87 JR NC,$DAA5   ; If A is B or more, skip ahead to #R$DAA5
 $DA89 LD B,$01      ; Load B with $01 - Count the letters of the typed word
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B)) that agree with this
                     ; word (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42))
 $DA8B LD DE,$5C0B   ; Point DE at the tape header workspace
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B))
 $DA8E LD HL,$5C42   ; Point HL at the BASIC workspace
                     ; (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42))
*$DA91 LD A,(DE)     ; Read the byte DE points at into A
 $DA92 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $DA93 JR Z,$DA9D    ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$DA9D
 $DA95 CP (HL)       ; Compare A with the byte HL points at
 $DA96 JR NZ,$DA9D   ; If A differs from the byte HL points at, skip ahead to
                     ; #R$DA9D
 $DA98 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DA99 INC DE        ; Step DE on to the next byte
 $DA9A INC B         ; Add one to B
 $DA9B JR $DA91      ; Go back to #R$DA91
*$DA9D LD A,($DC14)  ; Read #R$DC13($DC14) in "The number reader's working
                     ; bytes" into A - As good as the best so far? Then this
                     ; word becomes the best (#R$DC13($DC14))
 $DAA0 CP B          ; Compare A with B
 $DAA1 JR Z,$DAA9    ; If A equals B, skip ahead to #R$DAA9
 $DAA3 JR C,$DAA9    ; If A is below B, skip ahead to #R$DAA9
*$DAA5 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack
 $DAA6 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $DAA7 XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
 $DAA8 RET           ; Return to the caller
*$DAA9 LD A,B        ; Copy B into A
 $DAAA LD ($DC14),A  ; Store A in #R$DC13($DC14) in "The number reader's
                     ; working bytes"
*$DAAD POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack - A record whose count byte
                     ; has bit 7 set holds the offset of another record in its
                     ; next two bytes: follow such links
 $DAAE PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack
 $DAAF INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DAB0 BIT 7,(HL)    ; Test bit 7 of the byte HL points at
 $DAB2 DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $DAB3 JR Z,$DAC1    ; If bit 7 of the byte HL points at is clear, skip ahead
                     ; to #R$DAC1
 $DAB5 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DAB6 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DAB7 LD E,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into E
 $DAB8 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DAB9 LD D,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into D
 $DABA LD HL,$5DBF   ; Point HL at #R$5DBE($5DBF)
 $DABD ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $DABE EX (SP),HL    ; Swap HL with the word on top of the stack
 $DABF JR $DAAD      ; Go back to #R$DAAD
*$DAC1 POP DE        ; Restore DE from the stack - NZ: more words to try
 $DAC2 POP HL        ; Restore HL from the stack
 $DAC3 OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $DAC5 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Read a token that is not a word: money, a number or punctuation
;
; A '£' (character $60) starts an amount of money (#R$DB67); a digit starts a
; time or a number (#R$DAEF); a full stop, comma or quote mark gives the
; punctuation tokens $B0, $A0 and $90. Read from the code.
;
;  Input:A The first character of the word
;        HL The word
; Output:A The token class; BC its value; HL past it, for money, a number, full
; .        stop, comma or quote
;        F NZ for any other character
c$DAC6 CP $60        ; Compare A with $60 - A pound sign ($60 in the game's
                     ; character set): read an amount of money (#R$DB67)
 $DAC8 JP Z,$DB67    ; If A equals $60, jump on to the routine "Read an amount
                     ; of pre-decimal money" (#R$DB67)
 $DACB CALL $DAE8    ; A digit? (#R$DAE8)
 $DACE JP NC,$DAEF   ; Yes: read a number (#R$DAEF)
 $DAD1 LD B,$B0      ; Load B with $B0 - A full stop: class $B0 (end of a
                     ; command)
 $DAD3 CP $2E        ; Compare A with $2E
 $DAD5 JR Z,$DAE2    ; If A equals $2E, skip ahead to #R$DAE2
 $DAD7 LD B,$A0      ; Load B with $A0 - A comma: class $A0
 $DAD9 CP $2C        ; Compare A with $2C
 $DADB JR Z,$DAE2    ; If A equals $2C, skip ahead to #R$DAE2 - A double quote:
                     ; class $90 (starts or ends a quotation)
 $DADD CP $22        ; Compare A with $22
 $DADF RET NZ        ; If A differs from $22, return - Anything else: carry on
                     ; with the caller (RET NZ)
 $DAE0 LD B,$90      ; Load B with $90
*$DAE2 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte - Past the character; class
                     ; in A, value 0
 $DAE3 LD A,B        ; Copy B into A
 $DAE4 LD BC,$0000   ; Load BC with $0000
 $DAE7 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Is A a digit? (carry if not)
;
; Used by the routines at #R$D8AD, #R$DAC6, #R$DB67 and #R$DBF7.
;
;  Input:A A character
; Output:F Carry clear if it is a digit
c$DAE8 CP $30        ; Compare A with $30 - Carry clear if A is a digit ('0' to
                     ; '9')
 $DAEA RET C         ; If A is below $30, return
 $DAEB CP $3A        ; Compare A with $3A
 $DAED CCF           ; Flip the carry flag
 $DAEE RET           ; Return to the caller

; Read a time, or a number that may turn out to be money
;
; A number followed by ':' (minutes), 'A' or 'P' (then 'M') is a time: B = the
; hour with bit 7 set and bit 6 for AM, C = the minutes with bit 6 for PM.
; Anything else goes to the money reader at #R$DB84 (so '2/6' and '3S' need no
; '£'). Returns A = $F0 for a number token, or $D0 and BC = 0 if it makes no
; sense.
;
;  Input:HL The number
; Output:A $F0 (a number) or $D0 (not understood)
;        B, C The time, or 0
;        HL Past it
c$DAEF CALL $DBF7    ; Call the routine "Read a decimal number at HL into A"
                     ; (#R$DBF7) - B = the first number (#R$DBF7)
 $DAF2 LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $DAF3 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - Followed by ':', 'A'
                     ; or 'P' it is a time; otherwise money (#R$DB56)
 $DAF4 CP $3A        ; Compare A with $3A
 $DAF6 JR Z,$DB05    ; If A equals $3A, skip ahead to #R$DB05
 $DAF8 CP $41        ; Compare A with $41
 $DAFA JR Z,$DB05    ; If A equals $41, skip ahead to #R$DB05
 $DAFC CP $50        ; Compare A with $50
 $DAFE JR Z,$DB05    ; If A equals $50, skip ahead to #R$DB05
 $DB00 CALL $DB56    ; Call the routine "Is A a separator (full stop, comma or
                     ; space)? Z if so" (#R$DB56)
 $DB03 JR NZ,$DB84   ; If the zero flag is clear, jump on to #R$DB84
*$DB05 LD A,B        ; Copy B into A - The hour must be below 60 - the same
                     ; test as the minutes; bit 7 marks it as a time
 $DB06 CP $3C        ; Compare A with $3C
 $DB08 JR NC,$DB4F   ; If A is $3C or more, jump on to #R$DB4F
 $DB0A SET 7,B       ; Set bit 7 of B
 $DB0C LD C,$00      ; Load C with $00
 $DB0E LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - ':' and the minutes
                     ; in C
 $DB0F CALL $DB56    ; Call the routine "Is A a separator (full stop, comma or
                     ; space)? Z if so" (#R$DB56)
 $DB12 JR Z,$DB60    ; If the zero flag is set, jump on to #R$DB60
 $DB14 CP $3A        ; Compare A with $3A
 $DB16 JR NZ,$DB21   ; If A differs from $3A, skip ahead to #R$DB21
 $DB18 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DB19 CALL $DBF7    ; Call the routine "Read a decimal number at HL into A"
                     ; (#R$DBF7)
 $DB1C LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $DB1D CP $3C        ; Compare A with $3C
 $DB1F JR NC,$DB4F   ; If A is $3C or more, jump on to #R$DB4F
*$DB21 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - 'A': bit 6 of the
                     ; hour; 'P': bit 6 of the minutes
 $DB22 CALL $DB56    ; Call the routine "Is A a separator (full stop, comma or
                     ; space)? Z if so" (#R$DB56)
 $DB25 JR Z,$DB60    ; If the zero flag is set, jump on to #R$DB60
 $DB27 CP $41        ; Compare A with $41
 $DB29 JR NZ,$DB30   ; If A differs from $41, skip ahead to #R$DB30
 $DB2B SET 6,B       ; Set bit 6 of B
 $DB2D INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DB2E JR $DB37      ; Skip ahead to #R$DB37
*$DB30 CP $50        ; Compare A with $50
 $DB32 JR NZ,$DB4F   ; If A differs from $50, skip ahead to #R$DB4F
 $DB34 SET 6,C       ; Set bit 6 of C
 $DB36 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
*$DB37 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - then 'M'
 $DB38 CP $4D        ; Compare A with $4D
 $DB3A JR NZ,$DB4F   ; If A differs from $4D, skip ahead to #R$DB4F
*$DB3C INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte - Skip full stops; the time
                     ; must end the word
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$DB67.
*$DB3D LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $DB3E CP $2E        ; Compare A with $2E
 $DB40 JR Z,$DB3C    ; If A equals $2E, go back to #R$DB3C
 $DB42 CALL $DB59    ; Call #R$DB59 (inside "Is A a separator (full stop, comma
                     ; or...")
 $DB45 JR Z,$DB60    ; If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$DB60
 $DB47 CP $0D        ; Compare A with $0D
 $DB49 JR Z,$DB5F    ; If A equals $0D, skip ahead to the routine "Step HL back
                     ; one and return Z with A = $F0" (#R$DB5F)
 $DB4B CP $22        ; Compare A with $22
 $DB4D JR Z,$DB5F    ; If A equals $22, skip ahead to the routine "Step HL back
                     ; one and return Z with A = $F0" (#R$DB5F)
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$DB67.
*$DB4F XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Not a time: a $D0
                     ; token (not understood)
 $DB50 LD A,$D0      ; Load A with $D0
 $DB52 LD BC,$0000   ; Load BC with $0000
 $DB55 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Is A a separator (full stop, comma or space)? Z if so
;
; Used by the routine at #R$DAEF.
;
;  Input:A A character
; Output:F Z if it is a full stop, comma or space
c$DB56 CP $2E        ; Compare A with $2E - Full stop...
 $DB58 RET Z         ; If A equals $2E, return
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$DAEF.
*$DB59 CP $2C        ; Compare A with $2C - ...comma...
 $DB5B RET Z         ; If A equals $2C, return
 $DB5C CP $20        ; Compare A with $20 - ...or space: Z
 $DB5E RET           ; Return to the caller

; Step HL back one and return Z with A = $F0
;
; Read from the code.
;
; Output:HL Back one
;        A $F0
;        F Z
c$DB5F DEC HL        ; Step HL back one
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$DAEF.
*$DB60 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Z with A = $F0 (a
                     ; time was read)
 $DB61 LD A,$F0      ; Load A with $F0
 $DB63 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Pounds, shillings and pence
;
; The three parts of an amount being read, added up at #R$DBC0.
b$DB64 DEFB $00      ; Pounds
 $DB65 DEFB $00      ; Shillings
 $DB66 DEFB $00      ; Pence

; Read an amount of pre-decimal money
;
; '£n' gives pounds; then 'nS' is shillings, 'nD' pence, and 'n/m' shillings
; and pence ('n/-' for none). The total is worked out in pence - (pounds x 20 +
; shillings) x 12 + pence - and packed into BC seven bits per byte with bit 7
; of both bytes set, which is what tells a sum of money from a time
; (requirement code 6, PAY TO, wants bit 7 set in the low byte; code 5, WAIT
; UNTIL, wants it clear).
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/money_test.py): '£1' reads as
; #R$81EF($81F0) (240 pence), '2/6' as #R$809A($809E) (30), '3S' as
; #R$80A2($80A4) (36), '1/-' as #R$808A($808C) (12), and '9:30AM' as
; #R$C91C($C91E); the game echoes them back as 'You pay £1 to Watson.', '2/6',
; '3/-' and '1/-'.
;
;  Input:HL The text at the pound sign
; Output:A $F0 (an amount) or $D0 (not understood)
;        BC The sum in the packed form
;        HL Past the amount
c$DB67 INC HL        ; After the '£'
 $DB68 CALL $DBF7    ; Call the routine "Read a decimal number at HL into A"
                     ; (#R$DBF7) - Pounds
 $DB6B LD ($DB64),A  ; Store A in "Pounds, shillings and pence" (#R$DB64)
 $DB6E XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $DB6F LD ($DB65),A  ; Store A in #R$DB65 in "Pounds, shillings and pence"
 $DB72 LD ($DB66),A  ; Store A in #R$DB66 in "Pounds, shillings and pence"
 $DB75 INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DB76 LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $DB77 CALL $DAE8    ; Call the routine "Is A a digit? (carry if not)"
                     ; (#R$DAE8)
 $DB7A DEC HL        ; Step HL back one byte
 $DB7B JR C,$DBC0    ; If the carry flag is set, jump on to #R$DBC0
 $DB7D INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DB7E CALL $DBF7    ; Call the routine "Read a decimal number at HL into A"
                     ; (#R$DBF7)
 $DB81 LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $DB82 JR $DB88      ; Skip ahead to #R$DB88
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$DAEF.
*$DB84 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - No pounds
 $DB85 LD ($DB64),A  ; Store A in "Pounds, shillings and pence" (#R$DB64)
*$DB88 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $DB89 LD ($DB65),A  ; Store A in #R$DB65 in "Pounds, shillings and pence"
 $DB8C LD ($DB66),A  ; Store A in #R$DB66 in "Pounds, shillings and pence"
 $DB8F LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - S: shillings
 $DB90 CP $53        ; Compare A with $53
 $DB92 JR NZ,$DB9A   ; If A differs from $53, skip ahead to #R$DB9A
 $DB94 LD A,B        ; Copy B into A
 $DB95 LD ($DB65),A  ; Store A in #R$DB65 in "Pounds, shillings and pence"
*$DB98 JR $DBBF      ; Skip ahead to #R$DBBF
*$DB9A CP $44        ; Compare A with $44 - D: pence
 $DB9C JR NZ,$DBA4   ; If A differs from $44, skip ahead to #R$DBA4
 $DB9E LD A,B        ; Copy B into A
 $DB9F LD ($DB66),A  ; Store A in #R$DB66 in "Pounds, shillings and pence"
 $DBA2 JR $DB98      ; Go back to #R$DB98
*$DBA4 CP $2F        ; Compare A with $2F - /: shillings, then pence or -
 $DBA6 JR NZ,$DB4F   ; If A differs from $2F, go back to #R$DB4F
 $DBA8 LD A,B        ; Copy B into A
 $DBA9 LD ($DB65),A  ; Store A in #R$DB65 in "Pounds, shillings and pence"
 $DBAC INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
 $DBAD LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A
 $DBAE CP $2D        ; Compare A with $2D
 $DBB0 JR Z,$DBBF    ; If A equals $2D, skip ahead to #R$DBBF
 $DBB2 CALL $DAE8    ; Call the routine "Is A a digit? (carry if not)"
                     ; (#R$DAE8)
 $DBB5 JR C,$DB4F    ; If the carry flag is set, go back to #R$DB4F
 $DBB7 CALL $DBF7    ; Call the routine "Read a decimal number at HL into A"
                     ; (#R$DBF7)
 $DBBA LD ($DB66),A  ; Store A in #R$DB66 in "Pounds, shillings and pence"
 $DBBD JR $DBC0      ; Skip ahead to #R$DBC0
*$DBBF INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte
*$DBC0 PUSH HL       ; Save HL on the stack - Total in pence: pounds x 20...
 $DBC1 LD A,($DB64)  ; Read "Pounds, shillings and pence" (#R$DB64) into A
 $DBC4 LD L,A        ; Copy A into L
 $DBC5 LD H,$00      ; Load H with $00
 $DBC7 ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $DBC8 ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $DBC9 LD E,L        ; Copy L into E
 $DBCA LD D,H        ; Copy H into D
 $DBCB ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $DBCC ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $DBCD ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $DBCE LD A,($DB65)  ; Read #R$DB65 in "Pounds, shillings and pence" into A -
                     ; ...plus shillings...
 $DBD1 LD E,A        ; Copy A into E
 $DBD2 LD D,$00      ; Load D with $00
 $DBD4 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $DBD5 ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit) - ...times 12...
 $DBD6 ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $DBD7 LD E,L        ; Copy L into E
 $DBD8 LD D,H        ; Copy H into D
 $DBD9 ADD HL,HL     ; Double HL (shift it left one bit)
 $DBDA ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $DBDB LD A,($DB66)  ; Read #R$DB66 in "Pounds, shillings and pence" into A -
                     ; ...plus pence
 $DBDE LD E,A        ; Copy A into E
 $DBDF LD D,$00      ; Load D with $00
 $DBE1 ADD HL,DE     ; Add DE to HL
 $DBE2 EX (SP),HL    ; Swap HL with the word on top of the stack
 $DBE3 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $DBE4 LD A,B        ; Copy B into A - Too much?
 $DBE5 CP $40        ; Compare A with $40
 $DBE7 JP NC,$DB4F   ; If A is $40 or more, go back to #R$DB4F
 $DBEA SLA C         ; Shift C left (double it) - Pack it: 7 bits a byte, bit 7
                     ; set in both
 $DBEC RL B          ; Rotate B left through the carry
 $DBEE SRL C         ; Shift C right (halve it)
 $DBF0 SET 7,C       ; Set bit 7 of C
 $DBF2 SET 7,B       ; Set bit 7 of B
 $DBF4 JP $DB3D      ; Go back to #R$DB3D

; Read a decimal number at HL into A
;
; Used by the routines at #R$DAEF and #R$DB67.
;
;  Input:HL A decimal number
; Output:A The number
;        HL Past it
;        BC Kept
c$DBF7 PUSH BC       ; Save BC on the stack - Save BC; C = the number so far, 0
 $DBF8 LD C,$00      ; Load C with $00
*$DBFA LD A,(HL)     ; Read the byte HL points at into A - Next character at
                     ; HL: a digit (#R$DAE8 sets carry if not)?
 $DBFB CALL $DAE8    ; Call the routine "Is A a digit? (carry if not)"
                     ; (#R$DAE8)
 $DBFE JR C,$DC10    ; Not a digit: finish
 $DC00 SUB $30       ; Subtract $30 from A - Digit value into B
 $DC02 LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $DC03 RLC C         ; Rotate C left - C = C x 10 + digit: C x 2 in A...
 $DC05 LD A,C        ; Copy C into A
 $DC06 RLC C         ; Rotate C left - ...C x 8 added...
 $DC08 RLC C         ; Rotate C left
 $DC0A ADD A,C       ; Add C to A
 $DC0B ADD A,B       ; Add B to A - ...plus the digit
 $DC0C LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $DC0D INC HL        ; Step HL on to the next byte - Next character
 $DC0E JR $DBFA      ; Go back to #R$DBFA
*$DC10 LD A,C        ; Copy C into A - A = the number
 $DC11 POP BC        ; Restore BC from the stack
 $DC12 RET           ; Return to the caller

; The number reader's working bytes
;
; Written at #R$DA77($DA78), read at #R$DA1B($DA1C) and #R$DA7F($DA80). Read
; from the code.
b$DC13 DEFB $00,$00  ; The number reader's working bytes

; Fail: A = 0, HL = 0, Z set
;
; The common 'no' exit of action-record checks (the destination check at
; #R$9AA3 refuses through it). Read from the code.
;
; Output:A 0
;        HL 0
;        F Z (failed)
c$DC15 XOR A         ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Fail: A = 0, HL =
                     ; 0 (Z set)
 $DC16 LD L,A        ; Copy A into L
 $DC17 LD H,A        ; Copy A into H
 $DC18 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Action record: default for verb 35 (LOOK THROUGH)
;
; LOOK THROUGH <door>: after the pre-check at #R$DC6C, finds the exit through
; the door (#R$D46C); if it leads to a room (+2), moves the actor there for a
; moment (#R$DD0B), describes the room with 'YOU CAN SEE' instead of 'YOU ARE'
; (#R$D0DC = 1, #R$D1B2) and moves it back. Tried in the emulator 2026-11-08:
; LOOK THROUGH WINDOWS gets YOU CANNOT LOOK THROUGH THE WINDOW, LOOK THROUGH
; DOOR asks WHICH DOOR ?. The record's first seven bytes are header and
; arguments, not code.
; .
; Tested 9 November (tools/experiments/untraced_tests.py, in the sitting room):
; LOOK THROUGH WINDOW gets 'YOU CANNOT LOOK THROUGH THE WINDOW', LOOK THROUGH
; YOUR DOOR with the door shut gets 'YOU CANNOT LOOK THROUGH YOUR DOOR', and
; after OPEN YOUR DOOR it prints 'You look through your door. You can see a
; hall.' - this is the only way found to run the body, so the door must be
; open. No stored route uses LOOK THROUGH.
; .
; Every one of its 24 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py plus the targeted tests described above).
;
;  Input:$A034 The door
; Output:F NZ (done); the room beyond described as seen through the door
c$DC19 DEFB $1B,$20,$40,$28,$00,$00,$00 ; Action record header: flags $1B; bit
                                        ; 4: five extra bytes; byte 1 $20
 $DC20 CALL $DC6C                       ; Call #R$DC6C (inside "Action record:
                                        ; default for verb #R$DC57(28) (GO...")
                                        ; - Pre-check (#R$DC6C): fail if not a
                                        ; door
 $DC23 RET NZ                           ; If the zero flag is clear, return
 $DC24 LD A,($A034)                     ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game
                                        ; variables" into A - A = the door;
                                        ; find its exit (#R$D46C)
 $DC27 CALL $D46C                       ; Call the routine "Find the exit from
                                        ; the actor's room through door A..."
                                        ; (#R$D46C)
 $DC2A LD A,(IX+$02)                    ; Read byte +2 of the record at IX into
                                        ; A - +2: the room beyond (none: just
                                        ; succeed)
 $DC2D AND A                            ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it
                                        ; was and clears carry)
 $DC2E JR Z,$DC4B                       ; If A is zero, skip ahead to #R$DC4B
 $DC30 LD B,A                           ; Copy A into B
 $DC31 LD A,($A00A)                     ; Read the current actor's room
                                        ; (#R$A00A) into A - Remember the
                                        ; current room
 $DC34 PUSH AF                          ; Save AF on the stack
 $DC35 LD A,$01                         ; Load A with $01 - 'YOU CAN SEE'
                                        ; wording on (#R$D0DC)
 $DC37 LD ($D0DC),A                     ; Store A in "Set to use 'YOU CAN SEE'
                                        ; instead of 'YOU ARE ...'" (#R$D0DC)
 $DC3A CALL $DD0B                       ; Move the actor to the room beyond for
                                        ; a moment (#R$DD0B, B = the room)
 $DC3D LD A,($A00A)                     ; Read the current actor's room
                                        ; (#R$A00A) into A - Describe the room
                                        ; it is in now (#R$D1B2)
 $DC40 CALL $D1B2                       ; Call the routine "Describe room A
                                        ; briefly (a room seen before)"
                                        ; (#R$D1B2)
 $DC43 POP BC                           ; Restore BC from the stack
 $DC44 CALL $DD0B                       ; Call #R$DD0B (inside "Default action:
                                        ; move in a direction (verbs...") -
                                        ; Move back to the room it was in
 $DC47 XOR A                            ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
 $DC48 LD ($D0DC),A                     ; Store A in "Set to use 'YOU CAN SEE'
                                        ; instead of 'YOU ARE ...'" (#R$D0DC) -
                                        ; Wording back to normal
*$DC4B OR $01                           ; Make A non-zero, which clears the
                                        ; zero flag, and hand that back to the
                                        ; caller
 $DC4D RET                              ; Return to the caller
 $DC4E LD A,($A034)                     ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game
                                        ; variables" into A
 $DC51 CALL $D46C                       ; Call the routine "Find the exit from
                                        ; the actor's room through door A..."
                                        ; (#R$D46C)
 $DC54 JP $DCDD                         ; Jump on to #R$DCDD

; Action record: default for verb 28 (GO THROUGH)
;
; GO THROUGH <door>. The characters use it constantly - Chief Constable
; Straker, the cook, the gardener, Lestrade, the local policeman, Phipps and
; others walk through doors with it - and the player can too (tested 9
; November: with the door shut, GO THROUGH YOUR DOOR gets 'I SEE NO YOUR DOOR
; THAT YOU CAN GO THROUGH'; after OPEN YOUR DOOR it prints 'You go through your
; door.' and describes the hall). The verb becomes a GO in the door's
; direction. In the runs sampled the actor's room (+15) changed to the room on
; the other side of the door (Lestrade through the solid door, room 94 to 97;
; Straker through the large oak door, 63 to 64).
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 6,834 times in 14 of them, run by cook,
; gardener, inspector lestrade, chief constable straker and others. Every one
; of its 16 instructions was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:IX The exit through the door
; Output:The command turned into a movement; F From $DD07
c$DC57 DEFB $4B,$20,$4E,$DC ; Action record header: flags $4B; bit 6: pre-check
                            ; at #R$DC4E; byte 1 $20
 $DC5B LD A,(IX+$00)        ; IX = the exit through the door (pre-check
                            ; #R$DCA7($DCA8)): its direction (+0)...
 $DC5E LD ($A033),A         ; ...becomes the verb (#R$A032($A033)): the command
                            ; turns into GO <direction>
 $DC61 XOR A                ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - No direct
                            ; object (#R$A00A($A00F) = 0, #R$A032($A034) = $FF)
 $DC62 LD ($A00F),A         ; Store A in #R$A00A($A00F) in "Game variables"
 $DC65 DEC A                ; Subtract one from A
 $DC66 LD ($A034),A         ; Store A in #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables"
 $DC69 JP $DD07             ; Jump on to #R$DD07 - Carry on as the movement
                            ; action does (#R$DD07)
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$A2E4, #R$CAAF, #R$CB43,
; #R$DC19, #R$DC7B and #R$DF40.
*$DC6C LD A,($A036)         ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A
 $DC6F CP $00               ; Compare A with $00
 $DC71 RET Z                ; If A equals $00, return
 $DC72 CP $01               ; Compare A with $01
 $DC74 RET Z                ; If A equals $01, return
 $DC75 CP $64               ; Compare A with $64
 $DC77 RET Z                ; If A equals $64, return
 $DC78 OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller
 $DC7A RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Action record: default for verb 34 (LOOK)
;
; LOOK: a new line, then the full description of the room the player is in. It
; runs for the player and for the china man (the disguise), and does nothing
; for other actors (the pre-check at #R$DC6C passes only actors 0, 1 and $64 -
; you, the china man and the old man).
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 6,369 times in 14 of them, run by you,
; china man. Every one of its 7 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
; Output:F NZ; the room described in full ($D102) when the actor is accepted by
; .        $DC6C
c$DC7B DEFB $20,$10  ; Action record header: flags $20; bit 5: test #R$D078
                     ; first; byte 1 $10
 $DC7D CALL $DC6C    ; Call #R$DC6C (inside "Action record: default for verb
                     ; #R$DC57(28) (GO...") - Only the actors #R$DC6C accepts
                     ; (Z); anyone else passes
 $DC80 RET NZ        ; If the zero flag is clear, return
 $DC81 CALL $A2A2    ; Newline
 $DC84 LD A,($A00A)  ; Read the current actor's room (#R$A00A) into A - The
                     ; room the player is in (#R$A00A): describe it in full
                     ; (#R$D102)
 $DC87 CALL $D102    ; Call the routine "Describe room A in full (the first
                     ; visit)" (#R$D102)
 $DC8A OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller - Succeed
 $DC8C RET           ; Return to the caller

; Action record: default for verb 16 (DROP)
;
; Confirmed in the emulator 2026-11-08: TAKE LAMP sets the lamp's holder (+1)
; to 0, the player; DROP LAMP sets it to $FF, the holder of the actor, by
; copying it from the actor's record (#R$A012($A017)). Dropping is just being
; held by what holds you.
; .
; Seen: DROP CRUMPLED NOTE and DROP BURN NOTE by the player, DROP BODY by the
; local policeman - in each the dropped thing's holder (+1) went from 0 (the
; player) or the policeman to $FF (lying in the room).
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 30 times in 12 of them, run by you, local
; police man. Every one of its 17 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:$A013 The direct object's record
;        $A017 The actor's record
; Output:F NZ; the object's holder is now the actor's holder
c$DC8D DEFB $6A,$10,$B8,$DD ; Action record header: flags $6A; bit 5: test
                            ; #R$D078 first; bit 6: pre-check at #R$DDB8; byte
                            ; 1 $10
 $DC91 LD IX,($A013)        ; Read #R$A012($A013) in "Game variables" into IX -
                            ; IX = the direct object's record, IY = the actor's
                            ; (#R$A012($A017))
 $DC95 LD IY,($A017)        ; Read #R$A012($A017) in "Game variables" into IY
 $DC99 LD A,(IY+$01)        ; Read byte +1 of the record at IY into A - The
                            ; object's holder (+1) becomes the actor's holder
 $DC9C LD (IX+$01),A        ; Store A in byte +1 of the record at IX
 $DC9F OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller - Succeed
 $DCA1 RET                  ; Return to the caller
 $DCA2 CALL $D26C           ; Call the routine "Is the direct object carried by
                            ; the actor? (carry =..." (#R$D26C)
 $DCA5 JR C,$DCB6           ; If the carry flag is set, skip ahead to #R$DCB6
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$E3F5.
*$DCA7 LD A,($A036)         ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A -
                            ; Is the actor inside the object (#R$D278)?
 $DCAA LD HL,$A034          ; Point HL at #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables"
 $DCAD CALL $D278           ; Call #R$D278 (inside "Is thing A carried by the
                            ; actor, perhaps...")
 $DCB0 JP C,$DC15           ; If the carry flag is set, go back to the routine
                            ; "Fail: A = 0, HL = 0, Z set" (#R$DC15) - Yes:
                            ; refuse (#R$DC15)
 $DCB3 OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller
 $DCB5 RET                  ; Return to the caller
*$DCB6 LD HL,$DCBB          ; Point HL at "Refusal: "YOU ARE CARRYING THE ...""
                            ; (#R$DCBB)
 $DCB9 XOR A                ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the
                            ; caller
 $DCBA RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Refusal: "YOU ARE CARRYING THE ..."
;
; A pre-check that refuses returns HL = this address; the caller then runs it.
; Prints #R$68B7. CORRECTED: the automatic split had this as data.
;
; Output:HL The message printed ($A59F)
c$DCBB LD HL,$68B7   ; Point HL at the message "YOU ARE CARRYING THE OIL LAMP."
                     ; (#R$68B7)
 $DCBE JP $A59F      ; Go back to the routine "Print the message at HL"
                     ; (#R$A59F)

; Default action: take (TAKE, GET, CARRY, TAKE FROM, TAKE OUT OF)
;
; The body is three instructions: the target's container becomes the actor - so
; carrying something is the same containment link (#R$C86E's field +1) as
; sitting in a chair or a note being inside a safe. Confirmed: TAKE LAMP and
; GET LAMP both reach it, and INVENTORY afterwards lists the lamp.
;
;  Input:$A013 The target's record
;        $A036 The actor
; Output:F NZ; the target is now held by the actor
c$DCC1 DEFB $69,$10,$A2,$DC ; Action record header: flags $69; bit 5: test
                            ; #R$D078 first; bit 6: pre-check at #R$DCA2; byte
                            ; 1 $10
 $DCC5 LD IX,($A013)        ; IX = the target's own record
 $DCC9 LD A,($A036)         ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A -
                            ; Its container becomes the actor taking it
 $DCCC LD (IX+$01),A        ; Store A in byte +1 of the record at IX
 $DCCF OR $01               ; Succeed
 $DCD1 RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Pre-check for moving in a direction (verbs 1-10)
;
; Refuses (via #R$DC15, "YOU CANNOT GO ...") when there is no usable exit that
; way, when the actor is inside something (sitting in an armchair - GET OUT
; first), or when the thing in the way stops them (#R$DD35).
; .
; Confirmed by play for the player: every one of the 206 decoded exits behaves
; as this routine predicts (tools/walkall.py).
;
;  Input:$A033 The direction verb
; Output:F NZ if the actor can go; otherwise the move is refused through $DC15
;        IX The exit
c$DCD2 LD A,($A033)  ; The direction is the verb number
 $DCD5 CALL $D448    ; Call the routine "Find the actor's exit in a direction"
                     ; (#R$D448) - Find the exit
 $DCD8 CP $FF        ; Compare A with $FF
 $DCDA JP Z,$DC15    ; None: "YOU CANNOT GO ..."
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$DC19.
*$DCDD CALL $CBA7    ; Refuse if #R$B934 bit 6 is set ("SORRY, I CANNOT DO THAT
                     ; NOW")
 $DCE0 LD IY,($A017) ; The actor
 $DCE4 LD A,(IY+$01) ; Read byte +1 of the record at IY into A - Inside
                     ; something?
 $DCE7 CP $FF        ; Compare A with $FF
 $DCE9 JP NZ,$DC15   ; ...then they cannot walk off
 $DCEC LD A,(IX+$02) ; Read byte +2 of the record at IX into A - Destination
 $DCEF AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $DCF0 JP Z,$DC15    ; (0: refuse)
 $DCF3 LD A,(IX+$01) ; The thing in the way
 $DCF6 CALL $DD35    ; ...lets the actor through?
 $DCF9 JP NZ,$DC15   ; No: refuse
 $DCFC OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller - Yes
 $DCFE RET           ; Return to the caller
 $DCFF NOP           ; Do nothing (padding)
 $DD00 NOP           ; Do nothing (padding)
 $DD01 NOP           ; Do nothing (padding)
 $DD02 NOP           ; Do nothing (padding)

; Default action: move in a direction (verbs 1-10)
;
; Shared by all ten direction verbs. The pre-check (#R$DCD2) works out where
; the actor would end up; the body moves the actor and, when the actor is the
; one being followed by the display, updates #R$A00A.
; .
; The body also stores the destination and its room record at #R$DD01 and
; #R$DCFF - for ANY actor that moves, not just the player. After the boot
; sequence they hold room 16 (platform one of Aldergate station), left there by
; someone moving during the first turns. The 'what's here' code uses #R$DCFF,
; so a harness that teleports the player must set it as well, or the next room
; description starts with platform one.
; .
; Seen in the emulator: every compass move by every character, including the
; cook (EAST and WEST between rooms 65 and 68), the gardener (NORTHWEST,
; SOUTHWEST, NORTHEAST ...) and the player. In the sampled runs the mover's
; room (+15) is set to the destination and nothing else changes. #R$DD1D, if it
; ever ran, would turn a destination of $FF into room 0.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 21,243 times in 14 of them. 20 of its 21
; instructions were executed; the rest (#R$DD1D) never ran in any stored route,
; and were therefore read from the code only.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. The pre-check (#R$DCD2) finds the exit in the direction (the verb number),
; and refuses if there is none, the actor is inside something, the destination
; is 0 or the thing in the way will not let the actor through.
; .
; 2. The body sets the displayed room if the actor is the one followed,
; remembers the room entered (#R$DD01) and its record (#R$DCFF).
; .
; 3. The actor's own location (+15) becomes the new room, and the things it
; holds are updated (#R$D249).
;
;  Input:IX The exit found by the pre-check
; Output:F NZ; the actor is in the new room
c$DD03 DEFB $60,$20,$D2,$DC ; Action record header: flags $60; bit 5: test
                            ; #R$D078 first; bit 6: pre-check at #R$DCD2; byte
                            ; 1 $20
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$DC57.
*$DD07 LD A,(IX+$02)        ; Read byte +2 of the record at IX into A
 $DD0A LD B,A               ; Copy A into B
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$DC19.
*$DD0B LD A,($A036)         ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A -
                            ; The actor
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$9AF4, #R$9C41, #R$E2C7,
; #R$E497 and #R$E4C9.
*$DD0E PUSH AF              ; Save AF on the stack
 $DD0F LD HL,$A036          ; Point HL at #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" -
                            ; Is it the one the display follows?
 $DD12 CP (HL)              ; Compare A with the byte at #R$A032($A036) in
                            ; "Game variables"
 $DD13 LD A,B               ; Copy B into A
 $DD14 JR NZ,$DD19          ; If A differs from the byte at #R$A032($A036) in
                            ; "Game variables", skip ahead to #R$DD19
 $DD16 LD ($A00A),A         ; Store A in the current actor's room (#R$A00A) -
                            ; Yes: the displayed room changes
*$DD19 CP $FF               ; Compare A with $FF
 $DD1B JR NZ,$DD1E          ; If A differs from $FF, skip ahead to #R$DD1E
 $DD1D XOR A                ; Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
*$DD1E LD ($DD01),A         ; Store A in #R$DD01 in "Pre-check for moving in a
                            ; direction (verbs..." - Remember the room just
                            ; entered (#R$DD01) and its record (#R$DCFF)
 $DD21 CALL $D224           ; Call the routine "Find a room record" (#R$D224)
 $DD24 LD ($DCFF),IX        ; Store IX in #R$DCFF in "Pre-check for moving in a
                            ; direction (verbs..."
 $DD28 POP AF               ; Restore AF from the stack
 $DD29 CALL $D237           ; The actor's own record...
 $DD2C LD (IX+$0F),B        ; Store B in byte +15 of the record at IX -
                            ; ...location = the new room
 $DD2F CALL $D249           ; Call the routine "Move everything inside thing A,
                            ; at any depth, to room..." (#R$D249)
 $DD32 OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller
 $DD34 RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Can the actor get past the thing in an exit?
;
; Nothing in the way (A = 0) always lets the actor through. Otherwise the thing
; must be open - +5 bit 5, or bit 3 - and its size (+2) must be at least the
; actor's size (IY+2).
; .
; This is how guards work: the local police men at Browns and Jones front gates
; are themselves the 'door' of the exit they stand in. They are 'open' (bit 5)
; with size 69, smaller than the player (96) and Watson (96), so neither can
; pass, while Lestrade (32) could. The hidden door into the opium den (85) and
; the front door between Camden Street and the entrance hall (32) are narrower
; than the player too. Confirmed by play: with the player's size poked down
; from 96 to 8, all five of those exits let the player through.
;
;  Input:A The thing in the way (0 = nothing)
;        IY The actor
; Output:F Z set if the actor can pass
c$DD35 AND A         ; Nothing in the way...
 $DD36 RET Z         ; If A is zero, return - ...pass
 $DD37 PUSH IX       ; Save IX on the stack
 $DD39 CALL $D237    ; The thing's record
 $DD3C LD A,(IX+$05) ; Read the flags byte (+5) of the thing record at IX into
                     ; A - Open?
 $DD3F AND $28       ; Keep only bits 3, 5 of A (mask $28)
 $DD41 LD A,(IX+$02) ; Read the size byte (+2) of the thing record at IX into A
                     ; - Its size
 $DD44 POP IX        ; Restore IX from the stack
 $DD46 JR Z,$DD4F    ; Not open: refuse
 $DD48 CP (IY+$02)   ; At least the actor's size?
 $DD4B JR C,$DD4F    ; Too small: refuse
 $DD4D XOR A         ; Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller -
                     ; Pass (Z)
 $DD4E RET           ; Return to the caller
*$DD4F OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller - Refuse (NZ)
 $DD51 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Sub-script: RUN at $E460; RUN at $E7BE; default for verb 18 (ENTER); default
; for verb 27 (GO INTO)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$E460, #R$E7BE.
; .
; Entry points: #R$DD54 = body of record #R$DD52.
; .
; Decoded by tools/plan.py; see #R$CC78 for the language. Each sub-block is one
; instruction. Room and object numbers are as the game uses them (the reference
; pages list both). Labels for verbs 128-140 and for the common helper records
; (#R$E52F, #R$E57A, #R$E588, #R$E5A8, #R$DDBE, #R$F198) were read from those
; records' code, not tried one by one in the emulator.
; .
; In an instruction, 'plan dobj' and 'plan iobj' are not fixed objects: they
; mean the direct and indirect object currently held in the actor's plan
; registers (bytes +4 and +5 of its actor record), so the same step works on
; whatever the actor is dealing with - for the player, the objects of the
; command just typed. 'plan verb' is the verb register (+3), 'self' is the
; acting character, and '-' means none.
b$DD52 DEFB $A4,$00                     ; Action record header: flags $A4; bit
                                        ; 5: test #R$D078 first; bit 7: checks
                                        ; only; byte 1 $00 - checks-only: the
                                        ; script follows
 $DD54 DEFB $17,$61,$DD,$FD,$FF,$60,$DD ; RUN #R$DD61 (plan dobj, -) [fail
                                        ; #R$DD60]
 $DD5B DEFB $11,$FD,$60,$DD             ; DO plan dobj [fail #R$DD60]
 $DD5F DEFB $0B                         ; RETURN OK
 $DD60 DEFB $0A                         ; RETURN FAIL

; Action record: find the exit to a room next door (RUN at $DD54)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$DD54.
; .
; The pre-check takes the direct object as a room number, finds the exit from
; the actor's room to it (#R$D471) and refuses if there is none or if the exit
; is not a plain movement direction (a value below 11); the body stores that
; direction in plan register #R$9FD7. Used by the walk-to-a-room sub-script at
; #R$DD52, which then DOes the direction. Seen: Watson, Lestrade and Straker
; walking between rooms - the register held, for example, 1 (north), 2 (south)
; or 4 (west).
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 2,887 times in 14 of them, run by watson,
; chief constable straker, inspector lestrade, major percival ffoulkes. Every
; one of its 13 instructions was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:IX The exit found by the pre-check
; Output:$9FD7 The exit's direction
;        F NZ
c$DD61 DEFB $64,$00,$6E,$DD ; Action record header: flags $64; bit 5: test
                            ; #R$D078 first; bit 6: pre-check at #R$DD6E; byte
                            ; 1 $00
 $DD65 LD A,(IX+$00)        ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into A -
                            ; IX = the exit found by the pre-check; its
                            ; direction (+0) becomes the plan's direction
 $DD68 LD ($9FD7),A         ; Store A in the plan's direct object (#R$9FD7)
 $DD6B OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller - Succeed
 $DD6D RET                  ; Return to the caller
 $DD6E LD A,($A034)         ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A -
                            ; Pre-check: the direct object is a room; find the
                            ; exit to it next to the actor (#R$D471)
 $DD71 CALL $D471           ; Call #R$D471 (inside "Find the exit from the
                            ; actor's room through...")
 $DD74 CP $FF               ; Compare A with $FF
 $DD76 JP Z,$DC15           ; No such exit: refuse (#R$DC15)
 $DD79 LD A,(IX+$00)        ; Read the first byte of the record at IX into A -
                            ; Its direction must be below 11, a compass
                            ; direction (not UP or DOWN...)
 $DD7C CP $0B               ; Compare A with $0B
 $DD7E JP NC,$DC15          ; If A is $0B or more, go back to the routine
                            ; "Fail: A = 0, HL = 0, Z set" (#R$DC15) -
                            ; Otherwise refuse
 $DD81 OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller
 $DD83 RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Action record: remember what holds the direct object (RUN at $E476, $E7CF)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$E476, #R$E7CF.
; .
; Puts the direct object's holder (+1) into plan register #R$9FD6; the
; pre-check refuses if nothing holds it ($FF). Used by the follow loops of
; Watson (#R$E456) and Lestrade (#R$E72F): when the person they follow is in a
; cab or train, the register holds that vehicle and the next step is DO CLIMB
; INTO it. Seen: Watson climbing into the cab you climbed into (the register
; held that cab's number), Straker climbing into Lestrade's police cab (thing
; 109) and Lestrade into yours.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 175 times in 13 of them, run by watson,
; chief constable straker, inspector lestrade. Every one of its 10 instructions
; was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:$A013 The direct object's record
; Output:$9FD6 Its holder
;        F NZ; the pre-check refuses if it is held by nothing
c$DD84 DEFB $6D,$00,$91,$DD ; Action record header: flags $6D; bit 5: test
                            ; #R$D078 first; bit 6: pre-check at #R$DD91; byte
                            ; 1 $00
 $DD88 LD A,(IX+$01)        ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into A - The
                            ; direct object's holder (+1) goes into plan
                            ; register #R$9FD6
 $DD8B LD ($9FD6),A         ; Store A in the plan's verb (#R$9FD6)
 $DD8E OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller - Succeed
 $DD90 RET                  ; Return to the caller
 $DD91 LD IX,($A013)        ; Read #R$A012($A013) in "Game variables" into IX -
                            ; Pre-check: the direct object's holder...
 $DD95 LD A,(IX+$01)        ; Read byte +1 of the record at IX into A
 $DD98 AND A                ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and
                            ; clears carry) - ...must not be $FF (held by
                            ; nothing): refuse (#R$DC15) if it is
 $DD99 INC A                ; Add one to A
 $DD9A JP Z,$DC15           ; If A has become zero, go back to the routine
                            ; "Fail: A = 0, HL = 0, Z set" (#R$DC15)
 $DD9D RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Action record: default for verb 46 (TALK TO)
;
; TALK TO: passes the quoted words to #R$B86A with A = 1 when the person spoken
; to is an actor with a record, 0 otherwise, and succeeds. Seen when SAY TO
; LESTRADE, CABBIE, MRS JONES, TRICIA FENDER and WATSON were typed ('You talk
; to Watson.').
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 313 times in 13 of them, run by you,
; china man. Every one of its 9 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:$A034 The person spoken to
; Output:F NZ
c$DD9E DEFB $0D,$10  ; Action record header: flags $0D; byte 1 $10
 $DDA0 LD A,($A034)  ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A
 $DDA3 CALL $CC0E    ; Call the routine "Find the actor record whose id matches
                     ; C" (#R$CC0E) - Is the direct object an actor with a
                     ; record (#R$CC0E)? A = 0 if not, 1 if so
 $DDA6 CP $FF        ; Compare A with $FF
 $DDA8 LD A,$00      ; Load A with $00
 $DDAA JR Z,$DDAE    ; If A equals $FF, skip ahead to #R$DDAE
 $DDAC LD A,$01      ; Load A with $01
*$DDAE CALL $B86A    ; Hand the quoted words on (#R$B86A)
 $DDB1 OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller - Succeed
 $DDB3 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Action record: default for verb 49 (WAIT HERE)
;
; WAIT HERE: byte 1 is $50, and the player's WAIT moves the clock on by five
; minutes. Body and pre-check are the same three instructions at #R$DDB8:
; refuse if the actor is busy (#R$CBA7), otherwise succeed. The record at
; #R$DDBE (wait a given number of minutes) uses this code as its pre-check as
; well.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 104,825 times in 14 of them, run by steam
; train, you, phipps, german agent and others. Every one of its 3 instructions
; was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
; Output:F NZ
c$DDB4 DEFB $40,$50,$B8,$DD ; Action record header: flags $40; bit 6: pre-check
                            ; at #R$DDB8; byte 1 $50
 $DDB8 CALL $CBA7           ; Call the routine "Refuse if the character is
                            ; busy: 'SORRY, I CANNOT DO..." (#R$CBA7) - (Next
                            ; record's pre-check) only if the actor is not busy
                            ; (#R$CBA7)
 $DDBB OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller
 $DDBD RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Script helper: wait n minutes
;
; RUN #R$DDBE / n: the action takes n minutes (#R$A01A($A01F) = n), so the
; actor's next turn comes n minutes later. Pre-check #R$DDB8 as for WAIT HERE.
; Read from the code.
; .
; Used by: RUN at #R$EEBE; RUN at #R$EEE4; RUN at #R$EF04; RUN at #R$EF54; RUN
; at #R$EF75; RUN at #R$F0E9; RUN at #R$F22D; RUN at #R$F246; RUN at #R$F25B;
; RUN at #R$F268; RUN at #R$F278; RUN at #R$F28D; RUN at #R$F2AC.
;
;  Input:$A034 The number of minutes
; Output:$A01F The minutes
;        F NZ
c$DDBE DEFB $40,$00,$B8,$DD ; Action record header: flags $40; bit 6: pre-check
                            ; at #R$DDB8; byte 1 $00
 $DDC2 LD A,($A034)         ; n...
 $DDC5 LD ($A01F),A         ; ...is how long this takes
 $DDC8 OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller - Done
 $DDCA RET                  ; Return to the caller
 $DDCB NOP                  ; Do nothing (padding)
 $DDCC NOP                  ; Do nothing (padding)
 $DDCD NOP                  ; Do nothing (padding)
 $DDCE NOP                  ; Do nothing (padding)
 $DDCF NOP                  ; Do nothing (padding)

; Default action: WAIT UNTIL (verb 50)
;
; Works out how many minutes there are from now until the time typed (the
; number at #R$A02A($A02F): hour and minute in 6-bit fields, bit 6 of the hour
; marking am and bit 6 of the minute pm), converting both times to minutes past
; 12 o'clock (#R$DE48: hours x 60 + minutes, 12 counting as 0) and, when
; neither am nor pm was typed, taking whichever comes next; the result becomes
; the time the action takes (#R$A01A($A01F)), so the player's next turn simply
; comes that much later (see the main loop at #R$A064, which also lets ENTER
; cut the wait short). #R$DDCB-#R$DDCF hold the two times and the am/pm letter.
; Read from the code.
; .
; Tried in the emulator 2026-11-08, at 8:01am on Monday: WAIT UNTIL 9:00 sets
; the action time to 59 minutes ($89 $00 at #R$A02A($A02F): bit 7 marks a
; time); WAIT UNTIL 8 to 719 (8pm, the next 8 o'clock); WAIT UNTIL 8:30PM ($88
; $5E, bit 6 of the minutes) to 749; WAIT UNTIL 7AM ($C7 $00, bit 6 of the
; hour) to 1379, 7am the next day. WAIT UNTIL 8:30 PM, with a space, gets I DO
; NOT KNOW THE WORD "PM". All four waits are cut short at 8:34 by the news
; about Lestrade.
; .
; CORRECTED: the automatic split had most of this body as text and data.
; .
; How it works:
; .
; 1. Take the time typed (#R$A02A($A02F)) and work out the minutes past twelve
; for it (#R$DE48) and for the clock now.
; .
; 2. If am or pm was typed, the target is in that half of the day; otherwise it
; is in the same half as now, or the next.
; .
; 3. The difference is how long the action takes (#R$A01A($A01F)).
;
;  Input:$A02F The time typed
; Output:$A01F The minutes to wait
;        F NZ
c$DDD0 DEFB $45,$00,$B8,$DD ; Action record header: flags $45; bit 6: pre-check
                            ; at #R$DDB8; byte 1 $00
 $DDD4 LD HL,($A02F)        ; Read #R$A02A($A02F) in "Game variables" into HL -
                            ; HL = the typed time (#R$A02A($A02F)); drop bit 7
 $DDD7 RES 7,H              ; Clear bit 7 of H
 $DDD9 PUSH HL              ; Save HL on the stack
 $DDDA LD A,H               ; Copy H into A - Without the am/pm bits...
 $DDDB AND $3F              ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 of A (mask $3F)
 $DDDD LD H,A               ; Copy A into H
 $DDDE LD A,L               ; Copy L into A
 $DDDF AND $3F              ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 of A (mask $3F)
 $DDE1 LD L,A               ; Copy A into L
 $DDE2 CALL $DE48           ; Call #R$DE48 (inside "Default action: WAIT UNTIL
                            ; (verb #R$DDD0(50))") - ...in minutes past twelve
                            ; (#R$DE48): the target, at #R$DDCD
 $DDE5 LD ($DDCD),HL        ; Store HL in #R$DDCD in "Script helper: wait n
                            ; minutes"
 $DDE8 LD HL,($9FDF)        ; Read the minute (#R$9FDF) into HL - The clock
                            ; (#R$9FDF) in minutes: now, at #R$DDCB
 $DDEB CALL $DE48           ; Call #R$DE48 (inside "Default action: WAIT UNTIL
                            ; (verb #R$DDD0(50))")
 $DDEE LD ($DDCB),HL        ; Store HL in #R$DDCB in "Script helper: wait n
                            ; minutes"
 $DDF1 POP HL               ; Restore HL from the stack
 $DDF2 LD A,$61             ; Load A with $61 - am typed: the target is in the
                            ; morning (#R$DDCF = 'a')
 $DDF4 LD ($DDCF),A         ; Store A in #R$DDCF in "Script helper: wait n
                            ; minutes"
 $DDF7 BIT 6,H              ; Test bit 6 of H
 $DDF9 JR NZ,$DE19          ; If bit 6 of H is set, skip ahead to #R$DE19
 $DDFB LD A,$70             ; Load A with $70 - pm typed: in the afternoon
                            ; ('p')
 $DDFD LD ($DDCF),A         ; Store A in #R$DDCF in "Script helper: wait n
                            ; minutes"
 $DE00 BIT 6,L              ; Test bit 6 of L
 $DE02 JR NZ,$DE19          ; If bit 6 of L is set, skip ahead to #R$DE19
 $DE04 CALL $DE60           ; Call #R$DE60 (inside "Default action: WAIT UNTIL
                            ; (verb #R$DDD0(50))") - Neither: the same half of
                            ; the day as now (#R$9FE2)...
 $DE07 LD A,($9FE2)         ; Read 'a' or 'p' (#R$9FE2) into A
 $DE0A JR C,$DE0E           ; If the carry flag is set, skip ahead to #R$DE0E
 $DE0C JR NZ,$DE16          ; If the zero flag is clear, skip ahead to #R$DE16
*$DE0E CP $61               ; Compare A with $61 - ...unless that time has gone
                            ; (or is now): then the other half
 $DE10 LD A,$70             ; Load A with $70
 $DE12 JR Z,$DE16           ; If A equals $61, skip ahead to #R$DE16
 $DE14 LD A,$61             ; Load A with $61
*$DE16 LD ($DDCF),A         ; Store A in #R$DDCF in "Script helper: wait n
                            ; minutes"
*$DE19 LD HL,($DDCD)        ; Read #R$DDCD in "Script helper: wait n minutes"
                            ; into HL - HL = target - now
 $DE1C LD DE,($DDCB)        ; Read #R$DDCB in "Script helper: wait n minutes"
                            ; into DE
 $DE20 AND A                ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction
                            ; that follows is a plain one (a compare)
 $DE21 SBC HL,DE            ; Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was
                            ; smaller, zero = equal)
 $DE23 LD DE,$02D0          ; Load DE with $02D0 - DE = twelve hours
 $DE26 LD A,($9FE2)         ; Read 'a' or 'p' (#R$9FE2) into A
 $DE29 LD C,A               ; Copy A into C
 $DE2A LD A,($DDCF)         ; Read #R$DDCF in "Script helper: wait n minutes"
                            ; into A
 $DE2D LD B,A               ; Copy A into B
 $DE2E CALL $DE60           ; Call #R$DE60 (inside "Default action: WAIT UNTIL
                            ; (verb #R$DDD0(50))") - Later than now: add twelve
                            ; hours if it is in the other half
 $DE31 JR C,$DE3C           ; If the carry flag is set, skip ahead to #R$DE3C
 $DE33 JR Z,$DE3C           ; If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$DE3C
 $DE35 LD A,B               ; Copy B into A
 $DE36 CP C                 ; Compare A with C
 $DE37 JR Z,$DE42           ; If A equals C, skip ahead to #R$DE42
 $DE39 ADD HL,DE            ; Add DE to HL
 $DE3A JR $DE42             ; Skip ahead to #R$DE42
*$DE3C ADD HL,DE            ; Add DE to HL - Not later: add twelve hours, and
                            ; twelve more for the same half
 $DE3D LD A,B               ; Copy B into A
 $DE3E CP C                 ; Compare A with C
 $DE3F JR NZ,$DE42          ; If A differs from C, skip ahead to #R$DE42
 $DE41 ADD HL,DE            ; Add DE to HL
*$DE42 LD ($A01F),HL        ; Store HL in #R$A01A($A01F) in "Game variables" -
                            ; That is how long the wait takes (#R$A01A($A01F));
                            ; succeed
 $DE45 OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller
 $DE47 RET                  ; Return to the caller
*$DE48 PUSH DE              ; Save DE on the stack - HL = hours x 60 + minutes,
                            ; twelve o'clock counting as 0
 $DE49 PUSH BC              ; Save BC on the stack
 $DE4A LD C,L               ; Copy L into C
 $DE4B LD B,H               ; Copy H into B
 $DE4C LD HL,$0000          ; Load HL with $0000
 $DE4F LD DE,$003C          ; Load DE with $003C
 $DE52 LD A,B               ; Copy B into A
 $DE53 CP $0C               ; Compare A with $0C
 $DE55 JR Z,$DE5A           ; If A equals $0C, skip ahead to #R$DE5A
*$DE57 ADD HL,DE            ; Add DE to HL
 $DE58 DJNZ $DE57           ; Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back
                            ; to #R$DE57
*$DE5A LD B,$00             ; Load B with $00
 $DE5C ADD HL,BC            ; Add BC to HL
 $DE5D POP BC               ; Restore BC from the stack
 $DE5E POP DE               ; Restore DE from the stack
 $DE5F RET                  ; Return to the caller
*$DE60 PUSH HL              ; Save HL on the stack - Compare the target with
                            ; now (carry if earlier, Z if the same)
 $DE61 PUSH DE              ; Save DE on the stack
 $DE62 LD HL,($DDCD)        ; Read #R$DDCD in "Script helper: wait n minutes"
                            ; into HL
 $DE65 LD DE,($DDCB)        ; Read #R$DDCB in "Script helper: wait n minutes"
                            ; into DE
 $DE69 AND A                ; Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction
                            ; that follows is a plain one (a compare)
 $DE6A SBC HL,DE            ; Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was
                            ; smaller, zero = equal)
 $DE6C POP DE               ; Restore DE from the stack
 $DE6D POP HL               ; Restore HL from the stack
 $DE6E RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Action record: default for verb 33 (INVENTORY)
;
; INVENTORY: prints 'You are carrying' and the list of what the actor holds
; (#R$D4CC, #R$D51A), or 'nothing'. Played 9 November: after DROP ALL, 'You are
; carrying nothing' (the #R$DE86 branch); in the old man's disguise, 'An old
; man is carrying nothing'.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 46 times in 13 of them, run by you, china
; man. Every one of its 19 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py plus the targeted tests described above).
;
;  Input:$A036 The actor
; Output:F NZ; "YOU ARE CARRYING" and the list, or NOTHING, printed
c$DE6F DEFB $20,$10  ; Action record header: flags $20; bit 5: test #R$D078
                     ; first; byte 1 $10
 $DE71 CALL $A2A2    ; Call the routine "Print a newline (A kept)" (#R$A2A2)
 $DE74 LD HL,$68AD   ; Point HL at the message "YOU ARE CARRYING" (#R$68AD)
 $DE77 CALL $A59F    ; Call the routine "Print the message at HL" (#R$A59F)
 $DE7A LD A,($A036)  ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A
 $DE7D CALL $D4CC    ; Call the routine "Count the things in place B that
                     ; should be listed (B)" (#R$D4CC)
 $DE80 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $DE81 LD HL,$6900   ; Point HL at the message "NOTHING" (#R$6900)
 $DE84 JR NZ,$DE8C   ; If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$DE8C
 $DE86 CALL $A59F    ; Call the routine "Print the message at HL" (#R$A59F)
 $DE89 OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $DE8B RET           ; Return to the caller
*$DE8C LD C,A        ; Copy A into C
 $DE8D LD A,($A00A)  ; Read the current actor's room (#R$A00A) into A
 $DE90 LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $DE91 LD A,($A036)  ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A
 $DE94 CALL $D51A    ; Call the routine "List the things in a place, with
                     ; commas and AND" (#R$D51A)
 $DE97 CALL $A2A2    ; Call the routine "Print a newline (A kept)" (#R$A2A2)
 $DE9A OR $01        ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                     ; that back to the caller
 $DE9C RET           ; Return to the caller

; Shared OPEN action ($AA71 entry #6, CLOSE is #7)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$AA71.
; .
; Confirmed real by breakpointing #R$C960 (the #R$AA71 table lookup) during a
; real `OPEN PLAIN DOOR` and seeing it reliably look up index 6 - this address
; - not as a static coincidence but as a repeated, live hit during real command
; execution.
; .
; OPEN: `LD IX,(#R$A012($A013)); SET 5,(IX+5)` - sets bit 5 of the target
; record's field +5. CLOSE is the next record, #R$AA71 entry #7 at #R$DEAF (an
; earlier version of this entry wrongly called it a 'sub-entry' of #6): the
; same, but `RES 5,(IX+5)`. This confirms bit 5 of field +5 means 'is this
; thing open' - which also explains a flag the containment-chain walker
; (#R$C86E) was found checking several rounds earlier, without knowing what it
; meant.
; .
; The chunk after CLOSE (#R$DEC1) is not part of this entry: it is the default
; action record for PUT IN / PUT ON / DROP IN (verbs #R$DEC1(38), #R$DEC1(39)
; and #R$DEC1(17), via the #R$9F38 table) - see overlay_04_actions.py.
; .
; This is one entry in what's best understood as a small library of shared,
; generic action implementations that individual objects' own compact records
; can reference (rather than every openable object needing its own custom OPEN
; code) - see source/world_model_notes.md for how this reframes #R$AA71 as a
; whole, correcting the earlier 'verb dispatch table' guess.
;
;  Input:$A013 The target's record
; Output:F NZ; the target is open (+5 bit 5 set)
c$DE9D DEFB $38,$10,$80,$21,$00,$00,$00 ; Action record header: flags $38; bit
                                        ; 5: test #R$D078 first; bit 4: five
                                        ; extra bytes; byte 1 $10
 $DEA4 LD IX,($A013)                    ; IX -> the target object's record
 $DEA8 SET 5,(IX+$05)                   ; Mark it open
 $DEAC OR $01                           ; Make A non-zero, which clears the
                                        ; zero flag, and hand that back to the
                                        ; caller
 $DEAE RET                              ; Return to the caller

; Shared CLOSE action ($AA71 entry #7)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$AA71.
; .
; `LD IX,(#R$A012($A013)); RES 5,(IX+5)`: clears the target's 'open' bit.
; Confirmed live: CLOSE PLAIN DOOR reaches it through the door's own list
; (object 50, the pair at #R$90E4($90E7)).
;
;  Input:$A013 The target's record
; Output:F NZ; the target is closed (+5 bit 5 clear)
c$DEAF DEFB $38,$10,$00,$20,$00,$00,$00 ; Action record header: flags $38; bit
                                        ; 5: test #R$D078 first; bit 4: five
                                        ; extra bytes; byte 1 $10
 $DEB6 LD IX,($A013)                    ; IX -> the target object's record
 $DEBA RES 5,(IX+$05)                   ; Mark it closed
 $DEBE OR $01                           ; Make A non-zero, which clears the
                                        ; zero flag, and hand that back to the
                                        ; caller
 $DEC0 RET                              ; Return to the caller

; Default action: put something into something (PUT IN, PUT ON, DROP IN)
;
; The pre-check #R$DED9 sets IX to the thing being moved and IY to the
; container, then runs the fit check at #R$DEE4. The body moves the thing into
; the container's room and makes the container its new container.
;
;  Input:IX The thing being moved
;        IY The container
; Output:F NZ; the thing is in the container's room and held by it
c$DEC1 DEFB $7A,$18,$D9,$DE,$09,$00,$00,$40 ; {Action record header: flags $7A;
 $DEC9 DEFB $28                             ; bit 5: test #R$D078 first; bit 6:
                                            ; pre-check at #R$DED9; bit 4: five
                                            ; extra bytes; byte 1 $18}
 $DECA LD A,(IY+$0F)                        ; The thing goes to the container's
                                            ; room...
 $DECD LD (IX+$0F),A                        ; Store A in byte +15 of the record
                                            ; at IX
 $DED0 LD A,($A035)                         ; ...and the container now holds it
 $DED3 LD (IX+$01),A                        ; Store A in byte +1 of the record
                                            ; at IX
 $DED6 OR $01                               ; Succeed
 $DED8 RET                                  ; Return to the caller

; Pre-check for PUT IN: will the thing fit in the container?
;
; Sets IX to the thing being moved (#R$A012($A013)), IY to the container
; (#R$A012($A015)) and A to the container's id, then falls into the fit check
; at #R$DEE4 - which the SIT IN / GET INTO pre-check (#R$E3F5) also jumps into
; with different registers (IX = the actor, IY = the chair): a shared tail.
; .
; #R$DEE4 refuses if none of bits 0-2 of the container's field +4 are set (it
; cannot hold things). Otherwise it fits if capacity - size - what's already
; inside is at least 1, where capacity and size are both field +2 and #R$D2ED
; totals what is already inside.
; .
; Confirmed in the emulator, including the boundary: Holmes (size 96) gets into
; Watson's armchair (capacity 255) even with Watson in it (#R$D2ED returns 96):
; 255-96-96 = 63. Poking the armchair's capacity to 193 still lets him in (1 to
; spare); 192 refuses (0 to spare), and the refusal is reported as 'I SEE NO
; WATSONS ARMCHAIR THAT YOU CAN GET INTO'.
;
;  Input:$A013 The thing being put
;        $A015 The container
;        $A035 The container's id
; Output:IX The thing
;        IY The container
;        F NZ if it fits; Z if it will not
c$DED9 LD IX,($A013) ; IX = the thing being put somewhere
 $DEDD LD IY,($A015) ; IY = the container
 $DEE1 LD A,($A035)  ; A = the container's id
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$E3F5.
*$DEE4 LD B,A        ; B = the container's id. Can the container hold things?
 $DEE5 LD A,(IY+$04) ; Read the type byte (+4) of the thing record at IY into A
 $DEE8 AND $07       ; Keep only bits 0, 1, 2 of A (mask $07)
 $DEEA JP Z,$DC15    ; No: refuse
 $DEED LD A,(IY+$02) ; Capacity minus the size of the thing...
 $DEF0 SUB (IX+$02)  ; Subtract the size byte (+2) of the thing record at IX
                     ; from A
 $DEF3 JR C,$DF00    ; ...too big even for an empty container? Refuse
 $DEF5 PUSH AF       ; Save AF on the stack
 $DEF6 LD A,B        ; How much is already inside the container?
 $DEF7 CALL $D2ED    ; Call the routine "Total size (+2) of the things directly
                     ; in thing A" (#R$D2ED)
 $DEFA LD B,A        ; Copy A into B
 $DEFB POP AF        ; Restore AF from the stack
 $DEFC SUB B         ; Take that off too
 $DEFD JR C,$DF00    ; Not enough room: refuse
 $DEFF RET NZ        ; At least 1 to spare: it fits (NZ)
*$DF00 XOR A         ; Exactly full also refuses (Z)
 $DF01 RET           ; Return to the caller

; Action record: default for verb 26 (GIVE TO): hand the object over
;
; The pre-check (#R$DF24) asks whether the receiver (#R$A032($A035)) can carry
; the object as well as what it holds (#R$C8C6). The body makes the receiver
; the object's holder (+1), puts the object in the receiver's room (+15) and
; moves everything inside it there too (#R$D249). Read from the code; GIVE
; WATSON LAMP and GIVE YOUR MONEY TO WATSON both end with Watson holding it
; (emulator, 8 November).
; .
; Played: GIVE MONEY TO CABBIE hands over the entire purse (thing 27):
; INVENTORY shows no money afterwards and the cabbie keeps asking for his fare.
; Use PAY n TO instead (#R$E370).
;
;  Input:$A013 The object's record
;        $A015 The receiver's record
; Output:F NZ; the receiver holds the object, in the receiver's room
c$DF02 DEFB $6A,$1D,$24,$DF ; Action record header: flags $6A; bit 5: test
                            ; #R$D078 first; bit 6: pre-check at #R$DF24; byte
                            ; 1 $1D
 $DF06 LD IY,($A015)        ; Read the direct and indirect objects' records
                            ; (#R$A012($A015)) into IY - IY = the receiver's
                            ; record, IX = the object's
 $DF0A LD IX,($A013)        ; Read #R$A012($A013) in "Game variables" into IX
 $DF0E LD A,($A035)         ; Read #R$A032($A035) in "Game variables" into A -
                            ; The receiver holds it now
 $DF11 LD (IX+$01),A        ; Store A in byte +1 of the record at IX
 $DF14 LD A,(IY+$0F)        ; Read byte +15 of the record at IY into A - and it
                            ; is in the receiver's room
 $DF17 LD (IX+$0F),A        ; Store A in byte +15 of the record at IX
 $DF1A LD B,A               ; Copy A into B
 $DF1B LD A,($A034)         ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A -
                            ; with everything inside it (#R$D249)
 $DF1E CALL $D249           ; Call the routine "Move everything inside thing A,
                            ; at any depth, to room..." (#R$D249)
 $DF21 OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller - Succeed
 $DF23 RET                  ; Return to the caller
 $DF24 LD A,($A034)         ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A -
                            ; Pre-check: can the receiver carry it as well?
                            ; (#R$C8C6)
 $DF27 LD B,A               ; Copy A into B
 $DF28 LD A,($A035)         ; Read #R$A032($A035) in "Game variables" into A
 $DF2B JP $C8C6             ; Go back to the routine "Can the actor carry
                            ; object A as well as what it..." (#R$C8C6)
 $DF2E LD A,($A034)         ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A -
                            ; Pre-check: is the object held by a person
                            ; (#R$C8AF)?
 $DF31 CALL $C8AF           ; Call the routine "Is object A held, at any depth,
                            ; by a person?" (#R$C8AF)
 $DF34 JR Z,$DF3D           ; If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$DF3D
 $DF36 LD HL,$A036          ; Point HL at #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" -
                            ; By someone other than the actor: refuse
 $DF39 CP (HL)              ; Compare A with the byte at #R$A032($A036) in
                            ; "Game variables"
 $DF3A JP NZ,$DC15          ; If A differs from the byte at #R$A032($A036) in
                            ; "Game variables", go back to the routine "Fail: A
                            ; = 0, HL = 0, Z set" (#R$DC15)
*$DF3D OR $01               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and
                            ; hand that back to the caller - Pass
 $DF3F RET                  ; Return to the caller

; Action record: default for verb 19 (EXAMINE)
;
; The pre-check (#R$DF2E, shared with #R$E135) refuses an object that a person
; other than the actor holds (#R$C8AF). Tried in the emulator 2026-11-08:
; Watson holds the Daily Chronicle at the start, and EXAMINE CHRONICLE gets I
; SEE NO CHRONICLE THAT YOU CAN EXAMINE.
; .
; Body: sets #R$CAAE = 1, and for the actors #R$DC6C accepts (0, 1 and $64)
; prints the description (#R$DF5B) and a newline. Tried in the emulator
; 2026-11-08: EXAMINE LAMP -> 'You examine the oil lamp. You see an oil lamp.'
; - a thing with no description message (+13/+14 empty) gets 'You see' + its
; name + '.'.
; .
; Seen: the player's EXAMINE marks the thing as seen (+6 bit 6 goes from 0 to 1
; - the female corpse, the burning note); Lestrade's script also examines
; things (the deep stream, the sandstone bridge, the sofa, the bookcase, the
; corpse); the body prints a description only for you, the china man and the
; old man (#R$DC6C), so for him it prints nothing.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 242 times in 14 of them, run by inspector
; lestrade, you. Every one of its 21 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:$A034 The direct object
; Output:F NZ; $CAAE 1; the description printed
c$DF40 DEFB $78,$10,$2E,$DF,$80,$40,$00,$00 ; {Action record header: flags $78;
 $DF48 DEFB $00                             ; bit 5: test #R$D078 first; bit 6:
                                            ; pre-check at #R$DF2E; bit 4: five
                                            ; extra bytes; byte 1 $10}
 $DF49 LD A,$01                             ; Load A with $01 - #R$CAAE = 1
 $DF4B LD ($CAAE),A                         ; Store A in "Flag: the player's
                                            ; view has changed" (#R$CAAE)
 $DF4E CALL $DC6C                           ; Call #R$DC6C (inside "Action
                                            ; record: default for verb
                                            ; #R$DC57(28) (GO...") - Only for
                                            ; the actors #R$DC6C accepts (Z):
                                            ; anyone else passes Print the
                                            ; description (#R$DF5B) Newline
 $DF51 RET NZ                               ; If the zero flag is clear, return
 $DF52 CALL $DF5B                           ; Call #R$DF5B (inside "Action
                                            ; record: default for verb
                                            ; #R$DF40(19) (EXAMINE)")
 $DF55 CALL $A2A2                           ; Call the routine "Print a newline
                                            ; (A kept)" (#R$A2A2)
 $DF58 OR $01                               ; Make A non-zero, which clears the
                                            ; zero flag, and hand that back to
                                            ; the caller - Succeed
 $DF5A RET                                  ; Return to the caller
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$E104 and #R$E135.
*$DF5B LD A,($A034)                         ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game
                                            ; variables" into A - IX = the
                                            ; direct object's record
 $DF5E CALL $D237                           ; Call the routine "Resolve an id
                                            ; (in A) to its full entity record"
                                            ; (#R$D237)
 $DF61 SET 6,(IX+$06)                       ; Mark it as seen (+6 bit 6)
 $DF65 LD L,(IX+$0D)                        ; Read byte +13 of the record at IX
                                            ; into L - Its description message
                                            ; (+13/+14): print it if there is
                                            ; one
 $DF68 LD H,(IX+$0E)                        ; Read byte +14 of the record at IX
                                            ; into H
 $DF6B LD A,H                               ; Copy H into A
 $DF6C OR L                                 ; Set in A the bits that are set in
                                            ; L
 $DF6D JP NZ,$A59F                          ; If the result is not zero, go
                                            ; back to the routine "Print the
                                            ; message at HL" (#R$A59F)
 $DF70 LD HL,$68E1                          ; Point HL at the message "YOU SEE"
                                            ; (#R$68E1) - None: 'You see'
                                            ; (#R$68E1)...
 $DF73 CALL $A59F                           ; Call the routine "Print the
                                            ; message at HL" (#R$A59F)
 $DF76 CALL $D3D5                           ; ...its name (#R$D3D5)...
 $DF79 LD A,$2E                             ; Load A with $2E - ...and a full
                                            ; stop
 $DF7B JP $A9B7                             ; Go back to the routine "Print
                                            ; character A, a word at a time
                                            ; (word wrap)" (#R$A9B7)

; Sub-script: shared action 20, TAKE the burning note
;
; TAKE it (#R$DCC1), then #R$DF8B. Read from the script.
; .
; Entry points: #R$DF80 = body of record #R$DF7E.
; .
; Decoded by tools/plan.py; see #R$CC78 for the language. Each sub-block is one
; instruction. Room and object numbers are as the game uses them (the reference
; pages list both). Labels for verbs 128-140 and for the common helper records
; (#R$E52F, #R$E57A, #R$E588, #R$E5A8, #R$DDBE, #R$F198) were read from those
; records' code, not tried one by one in the emulator.
; .
; In an instruction, 'plan dobj' and 'plan iobj' are not fixed objects: they
; mean the direct and indirect object currently held in the actor's plan
; registers (bytes +4 and +5 of its actor record), so the same step works on
; whatever the actor is dealing with - for the player, the objects of the
; command just typed. 'plan verb' is the verb register (+3), 'self' is the
; acting character, and '-' means none.
; .
; Played: DROP BURN NOTE then a plain TAKE BURN NOTE runs this script - the
; countdown #R$9CA1 goes to $FF and the note is called SINGE; EXAMINE CLOSELY
; SINGE NOTE then counts as examining it. TAKE NOTE FROM FIREPLACE takes the
; note through a different action and leaves the countdown running. Note that
; typing BURNING NOTE is read as BURNT (the dictionary shares BURN), so the
; note must be called BURN NOTE until it is singed.
b$DF7E DEFB $88,$00             ; Action record header: flags $88; bit 7:
                                ; checks only; byte 1 $00 - checks-only: the
                                ; script follows
 $DF80 DEFB $07,$C1,$DC,$FD,$FF ; RUN #R$DCC1 TAKE (dobj) (plan dobj, -)
 $DF85 DEFB $07,$8B,$DF,$FD,$FF ; RUN #R$DF8B (plan dobj, -)
 $DF8A DEFB $0B                 ; RETURN OK

; Action record: singe the note when it is picked up (RUN at $DF85)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$DF85.
; .
; The tail of the shared TAKE script at #R$DF7E. If the burning note's
; countdown (#R$9CA1) is still running it is set to $FF (stopped) and the
; note's name word is changed from BURNING ($0157) to SINGED ($07D5), so the
; TAKEn note is a singed note you can examine. Seen on TAKE BURN NOTE: #R$9CA1
; went from 6 (or 4) to 255 and the name word from $0157 to $07D5.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 7 times in 7 of them, run by you. Every
; one of its 10 instructions was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
; Output:F From $E000 (NZ)
c$DF8B DEFB $08,$00  ; Action record header: flags $08; byte 1 $00
 $DF8D LD A,($9CA1)  ; Read #R$9CA1 in "After-turn routines for nine
                     ; characters" into A - The burning note's countdown
                     ; (#R$9CA1): already 0? Then nothing to change (#R$E000)
 $DF90 AND A         ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                     ; carry)
 $DF91 JR Z,$E000    ; If A is zero, jump on to #R$E000
 $DF93 LD A,$FF      ; Load A with $FF - Still burning: stop the countdown
                     ; ($FF)...
 $DF95 LD ($9CA1),A  ; Store A in #R$9CA1 in "After-turn routines for nine
                     ; characters"
 $DF98 LD IX,($A013) ; Read #R$A012($A013) in "Game variables" into IX - ...and
                     ; change the name word (+9/+10) of the direct object, the
                     ; note, to SINGE ($07D5)
 $DF9C LD HL,$07D5   ; Load HL with $07D5
 $DF9F LD (IX+$09),L ; Store L in byte +9 of the record at IX
 $DFA2 LD (IX+$0A),H ; Store H in byte +10 of the record at IX
 $DFA5 JR $E000      ; Jump on to #R$E000

; Sub-script: shared action 21, OPEN a wall safe
;
; #R$DFB6 (the safe's own check), then the ordinary OPEN (#R$DE9D). Read from
; the script.
; .
; Entry points: #R$DFAB = body of record #R$DFA7.
; .
; Decoded by tools/plan.py; see #R$CC78 for the language. Each sub-block is one
; instruction. Room and object numbers are as the game uses them (the reference
; pages list both). Labels for verbs 128-140 and for the common helper records
; (#R$E52F, #R$E57A, #R$E588, #R$E5A8, #R$DDBE, #R$F198) were read from those
; records' code, not tried one by one in the emulator.
; .
; In an instruction, 'plan dobj' and 'plan iobj' are not fixed objects: they
; mean the direct and indirect object currently held in the actor's plan
; registers (bytes +4 and +5 of its actor record), so the same step works on
; whatever the actor is dealing with - for the player, the objects of the
; command just typed. 'plan verb' is the verb register (+3), 'self' is the
; acting character, and '-' means none.
b$DFA7 DEFB $C8,$00,$1C,$E0     ; Action record header: flags $C8; bit 6:
                                ; pre-check at #R$E01C; bit 7: checks only;
                                ; byte 1 $00 - checks-only: the script follows
 $DFAB DEFB $07,$B6,$DF,$FD,$FF ; RUN #R$DFB6 (plan dobj, -)
 $DFB0 DEFB $07,$9D,$DE,$FD,$FF ; RUN #R$DE9D (plan dobj, -)
 $DFB5 DEFB $0B                 ; RETURN OK

; Action record: opening the wall safe on Monday (RUN at $DFAB)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$DFAB.
; .
; Part of the shared OPEN WALL SAFE script at #R$DFAB. If the direct object is
; thing $59 (the wall safe) and the day is Monday, control passes to #R$DFCC
; and Basil shoots you. Any other day it just succeeds. Seen: OPEN WALL SAFE on
; Monday (end4a_shot_wall_safe_monday) ended the game; on the other runs
; (Tuesday) nothing happened.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 21 times in 11 of them, run by you. Every
; one of its 7 instructions was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:$A034 The direct object
; Output:Continues at $E000, or at $DFCC on Monday for the wall safe
c$DFB6 DEFB $07,$F0  ; Action record header: flags $07; byte 1 $F0
 $DFB8 LD A,($A034)  ; Read #R$A032($A034) in "Game variables" into A - The
                     ; direct object (#R$A032($A034)) is thing $59?
 $DFBB CP $59        ; Compare A with $59
 $DFBD JP NZ,$E000   ; No: #R$E000
 $DFC0 LD A,($9FE1)  ; Read the day (#R$9FE1) into A - And the day is Monday
                     ; (0)?
 $DFC3 CP $00        ; Compare A with $00
 $DFC5 JP NZ,$E000   ; No: #R$E000
 $DFC8 JR $DFCC      ; Yes: carry on at #R$DFCC

; Action record: Basil shoots you (RUN at $EF99, and from $DFB6)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$EF99, #R$DFB6.
; .
; Game over. If Basil (thing 6, Phipps) is not in the player's room it prints
; 'Basil enters and says " Holmes, your interference is becoming annoying. "
; ...' (#R$759C), if he is it prints the version without 'enters' (#R$75D2),
; then ends the game through #R$9E74. Reached from the wall-safe script on
; Monday and from Phipps's wake-up routine #R$EFC7 when you are inside his
; house at that time. The three instructions at #R$DFE3-#R$DFE8 belong to the
; pre-check of the next record (#R$DFEA), not to this one.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 3 times in 3 of them, run by phipps, you.
; Every one of its 11 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py plus the targeted tests described above).
;
; Output:The game ends through $9E74 with Basil's message
c$DFCA DEFB $00,$00  ; Action record header: flags $00; byte 1 $00
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$DFB6 and #R$EFC7.
*$DFCC LD HL,$759C   ; Point HL at the message "BASIL ENTERS AND SAYS" HOLMES,
                     ; YOUR INTERFERENCE... (#R$759C)
 $DFCF LD A,$06      ; Load A with $06
 $DFD1 CALL $D237    ; Call the routine "Resolve an id (in A) to its full
                     ; entity record" (#R$D237)
 $DFD4 LD A,($A009)  ; Read the room the player is in, which decides what is
                     ; shown (#R$A002($A009)) into A
 $DFD7 CP (IX+$0F)   ; Compare A with byte +15 of the record at IX
 $DFDA JP NZ,$9E74   ; If A differs from byte +15 of the record at IX, go back
                     ; to the routine "End the game with the message in HL"
                     ; (#R$9E74)
 $DFDD LD HL,$75D2   ; Point HL at the message "BASIL SAYS" HOLMES, YOUR
                     ; INTERFERENCE IS... (#R$75D2)
 $DFE0 JP $9E74      ; Go back to the routine "End the game with the message in
                     ; HL" (#R$9E74)
 $DFE3 LD A,($A036)  ; Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A
 $DFE6 CP $64        ; Compare A with $64
 $DFE8 JR $DFFD      ; Skip ahead to #R$DFFD

