; Action record: Lestrade puts the policeman into the police cab (RUN at $EA5F)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$EA5F.
; .
; The direct object (the police cab) becomes the holder (+1) of the indirect
; object (the local policeman) and both are placed in room $32 (50, the main
; street). Seen: the policeman's holder went from 255 to 109 (the police cab)
; and his room, and the cab's, from 0 to 50.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 7 times in 7 of them, run by inspector
; lestrade. Every one of its 8 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
;  Input:$A034 The police cab
;        $A015 The indirect object's record
; Output:F NZ; the policeman is held by the cab and both are in room $32
c$EAF6 DEFB $07,$07    ; Action record header: flags $07; byte 1 $07
 $EAF8 LD A,($A034)    ; The direct object's number (#R$A032($A034))...
 $EAFB LD IX,($A015)   ; ...becomes the holder (+1) of the indirect object's
                       ; record (#R$A012($A015))
 $EAFF LD (IX+$01),A   ; Store A in byte +1 of the record at IX - Both are
                       ; placed in room $32 (+15)
 $EB02 LD (IX+$0F),$32 ; Set byte +15 of the record at IX to $32
 $EB06 LD IX,($A013)   ; Read #R$A012($A013) in "Game variables" into IX
 $EB0A LD (IX+$0F),$32 ; Set byte +15 of the record at IX to $32
 $EB0E OR $01          ; Make A non-zero, which clears the zero flag, and hand
                       ; that back to the caller - Succeed
 $EB10 RET             ; Return to the caller

; Script helper: is THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD proven?
;
; Succeeds when bit 1 of the statement's flags (#R$99A9($99AC)) is set; #R$EB19
; is the same for PHIPPS HAS PLANS (#R$9997($999A)). Lestrade's script uses
; them at #R$EA2C/#R$EA31 to decide to set off for Old Mill Road (#R$EA5B).
; Read from the code.
; .
; Used by: RUN at #R$EA31; RUN at #R$EA43.
;
; Output:F NZ if the statement at $99A9 is proven; Z if not
;        HL 0
c$EB11 DEFB $00,$00  ; Action record header: flags $00; byte 1 $00
 $EB13 LD IX,$99A9   ; Point IX at #R$99A9 in "The case: statements about the
                     ; crime, and..."
 $EB17 JR $EB1F      ; Skip ahead to #R$EB1F

; Action record: has Lestrade's statement been proven? (RUN at $EA2C)
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$EA2C.
; .
; Tests bit 1 of the case record at #R$9997 and returns NZ if it is set.
; Lestrade's script at #R$EA25 waits here until it is.
; .
; Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over
; the 15 stored routes): the body ran 1,682 times in 10 of them, run by
; inspector lestrade. Every one of its 5 instructions was executed
; (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
;
; Output:F NZ if the statement at $9997 is proven; Z if not
;        HL 0
c$EB19 DEFB $00,$00   ; Action record header: flags $00; byte 1 $00
 $EB1B LD IX,$9997    ; Case record #R$9997: is bit 1 of its +3 set (the
                      ; statement is established)?
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$EB11.
*$EB1F LD HL,$0000    ; Load HL with $0000 - HL = 0; the result is NZ if it is
                      ; set, Z if not
 $EB22 AND A          ; Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears
                      ; carry)
 $EB23 BIT 1,(IX+$03) ; Test bit 1 of byte +3 of the record at IX
 $EB27 RET            ; Return to the caller

; Sub-script: RUN at $E939; RUN at $EA0A; RUN at $EA95
;
; Addresses named in the title: #R$E939, #R$EA0A, #R$EA95.
; .
; Entry points: #R$EB2A = body of record #R$EB28.
; .
; Decoded by tools/plan.py; see #R$CC78 for the language. Each sub-block is one
; instruction. Room and object numbers are as the game uses them (the reference
; pages list both). Labels for verbs 128-140 and for the common helper records
; (#R$E52F, #R$E57A, #R$E588, #R$E5A8, #R$DDBE, #R$F198) were read from those
; records' code, not tried one by one in the emulator.
; .
; In an instruction, 'plan dobj' and 'plan iobj' are not fixed objects: they
; mean the direct and indirect object currently held in the actor's plan
; registers (bytes +4 and +5 of its actor record), so the same step works on
; whatever the actor is dealing with - for the player, the objects of the
; command just typed. 'plan verb' is the verb register (+3), 'self' is the
; acting character, and '-' means none.
